      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND THE STRIKERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington s Literary Tastes.</p>
        <p>Interesting disclmions took place at the monthly meeting of the Warringten Town Council held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) presided sad were there also present:—The Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forshow;, Aldermen A Bennett, W Bolton, T Burton, John Evans, James HMV,. W Pierpoint, J Smethurst, D Tinnion, Councillors G Archer, V H Ashcroft, IV H Bellion, T ltirtles, J Broadhurst, T Cbantler, J O Craik, E Cunnington, J Dickenson, J Hawthorn, R Henehall,C Jackson Holmes, F V Lashford, J T Locker. Dr McKee, J Pemberton, II Roberts, W Rogers, J Sankey, J Skelland, T S Steel, 0 Toole, J Wilkinson, W J Wilson, and Messrs J L Whittle (town clerk), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T Longdin (borough surveyor), W 8 Haddock (gas engineer), F V L Mathias (electrical engineer), F G Ruddock (borough analyst), Dr .1 C .11ibbert medical offi:er), J Gray (water engineer,. THE MAYOR COMPLIMENTS STRIKERS' CONDUC F.</p>
        <p>The M VOR said he thought it a pleasing duty to allude to the conduct of the people of the town during the period of the strike. (Hear, hear.) It wee only right in c3mmon justice to speak of the manner in which the Chief Constable and his staff performed their duties. Everything passed off most satisfactorily, and considering that the strike affected Warrington pretty seriously, he thought the people behaved grandly, and with credit. It wee somewhat remarkable that he should have received a communica- tion from the Association of Reilway Servants, asking him to pot on record their appreciatic n of the courtesy and kindness of the Chief Cr salable and his men dis- played towards them. (Applansed No more eloquent tribute could be paid to the Chief Constable and hs men than that the very men they were commissioned to keep in order were so completely satisfied that they complimented the police. No words of his or of any- one else could describe the satisfoctory state of affoira Letter thus the letter. (Applause ) HIGHER SALARIES FOR BOROUGH OFFICALS. At a meeting of the Finance and Estate Committee it was resolved that the salary of Mr. F. Timperley, chief assistant borough collector, be increased from £17.5 to £l9O per annum, as and from the Ist pr's. The Town Clerk laid before the Committee the sanction of the Local Governmett Board to the borrow- ing of the sum of £2,81% for the paving of Gorsey-lane and Norriwatreet. It was also resolved that the remuneration of the auditors for the audit of the accounts for the year ended 31st March, 1911, be fined at .£l2O. At a meeting of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee it was resolved that the salary cf Mr. W. Sugars, building inspector, be increased tram £ll3 to £l9O per annum. These minutes were unanimously agreed to, both Councillor Wilkinson and Alderman Bennett speaking in very high terms of the services rendered by Messrs Tisoperley and Sugars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>REPORT OF THE AUDITORS.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT OF THE AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>Councillor W lI.KIN,ON proposed the passing of the minutes of the Finance and Estate Committee and in doing so said the committee had received the report of the borough auditors and his Worship's auditor. The report was entirely satisfactory and was a matter of credit to the officials. (Hear, hear). That year it had been a very extensive audit, and Warrington in the plat bad not bid such an exhaustive audit as the pres.ent one. After considering the whole matter the Finance Committee thought they might ask the Council to sanction extra remuneration to the auditors. The quality a the audit was shown by the time spent hy the auditors this year. In 1969 the total hours spent by the three auditors were 160, in 1910 200, and this year C7l. The audit had never beea so thorough as IL had been this year—they had gone into the minutest details and everything had be, n thoroughly checked (Hear, hear.) He had pleasure in asking the Council to pass those minute', which included a resolution that the remuneration cf the auditors for the audit of the accounts for the year I nding 1911 be fixed at £l2O, which was an increase of £3O on their previous salary. Councillor LOCI:Ell sec, nded. Alderman BONETT said he was pleased that the audit had been so thorough, and the increase in the audit fees was well deserved. Ile protested, however, against Councillor Wilkinson saying that the audit was not always a thorough ocean in the years he had anything to do with the audit it was always thorough. Councillor WII.I•IMCN ce'd he commenced with his</p>
        <p>remarks in 1909. Alderman BreNM' said he supported the recom- mendation of the committee. Councillor PEMBERTON said he did not agree with the recommendation of the auditors when they said that the practice adopted of creating a reserve fund in the trading departments was, in their opinion, highly desirable, as to provide for the obsolesence of assets. If tbey pinned that report without any remarks it would le token that they tally agreed with it. That practice was a sjetern he was dead ecainst The system of Corporation financejwas quite di fferentfrom ordinary trading finance. To create large reserve funds when they bad such intolerable rates woo quite wrong. bE) Mayon said the reserve fund was fixed and could not exceed £lO,OOO for gas and a similar sum for water, and one-tenth of the capital for the electricity depart- ment. Councillor PEMBERTON said it was a questica of whether they should do it every year. There were some years when they had extraordinary expenses to meet. With all due respect to Alderman Bennett, he knew very well that the audit had not always been thoroughly done. No doubt Alderman Bennett looked at the question from an accountant's point of view, but no one could possibly go through every item for the sum which had been previously given. There was no doubt that the little clause inserted in the Parliamentary Bill that they should have a Local Government Ward audit had born fruit. He would have liked to have seen a fuller report and known exactly what the auditors thought. Councillor WILKINSON said he was pleased to see they Were complimented by the auditors that their practice of creating a reserve fund was a sound financial Gee. They were living in an age when at any time something might he invented which would make any portion of their plant obsolete. aid especially van that so with reference to electricity. So they were proceed- ing on lines of aound finance by having good rt serve fund*. With reference to the report not being satis- factory to Councillor Pemberton, it was a small report to the Council to say that they had found everything correct. They could not have more competent men than Mr Sikock and Mr. Pemberton. (Hear, hear). The MAYOR salted if it was the complimentary part Council objected to. (Laughter.) CCOCCiIIOr PEMBERTON thought it was too compli- msntary. Councillor WiLsole asked to be allowed to make a statement into his ability as an auditor. Several members said they were quite satisfied as to his ability. (Hear, hear). Councillor WILKINSON said Councillor Wilson had acted vs one of the auditors, and it was an inadvertence that he did not menticn Mr. Wilson's name. Toe minutes were agreed to. INSPECTION OF BUTCHERS' SHOPS.</p>
        <p>The following resolution from the National Federa- tion of Meat Traders' AlSOCiatiollll was read at a meet- ine of the Health Committee i—" That this Federation bails with unqualified satisfaction and cordially ,pports the recommendations by various local authon- ties of legislation in favour of uniformity and elscient inspection, and compeneation ti butchers for confisca- tion of tuberculous caresses voluntarily submitted for iospection."</p>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE TOWN. BETTER CONDITIONS OF HOME LIFE. The MEDICAL OFFICER, ate meeting of the Health Committee, submitted a circular letter from the Loma Government Board, en the prevalence of epidemic dianbces amongst children, and it was resolved that the letter be placed before a westing of the Joint Sub- Committee, to be convened at an early date. He also reported on the number of deaths due to diarrhcem Alderman JAMES EVANS, in proposing the minutes of the Health Committee, said on a recent visit to the sanatorium at Delmore, some useful information was obtained, which would no doubt fucilitste the currying on of the work at Sankey, and would, perhaps, save considerable expense in expetircenting in methods when they saw what had been adopted. With regard to the general health of the town he said the death-rate wan higher than at this time last year. They had been passing through a climatic period which bad had its effects on the health of the borough, and especially was that so in the case of disr,ffies. Compared with ether towns Warrington had come through fairly well. The number of deaths during the lest four weeks from diarrhout was My- two, forty of which related to infants. The infantile desth-rate was down considerably and the committee believed that that was largely due to the work which eras lasing carried on by the Medical Officer's department. (Applause). They had an organised staff who were visiting the homes of the people, and to the educational work carried on by their medical cflicer, lady visitors and other inspectors be largely attributed the rednotion in the death-rate from diarthes. While they had brought considerable pressure to hear upon the property owners who had houses of an insanitary character, they had also brought pressure to bear upon the persons occupying them. It was very easy to bore the property owners but there was a great deal of educational work to be done in connection with those occupying the houses, and they hoped in a little time to have a better condition of home life p 'ling than obtained at present. Consider- able difficulty *as caused by poverty, bat he knew from observation that better conditions of home life pre- -veiled in the town than was ths crse last year. With regard to the question of closing slum property hey had not actually closed many boasss which</p>
        <p>were insanitary, but many of the owners had done their best to comply with the requirements of the committee. They found there were many good and healthy houses empty in the town at the present time and those who had been removed could occupy them at a very small increase in the rent. From a return he had there were</p>
        <p>..- 14,661 houses in the borough and of these there were 432 unoccupied at rents varying from Is. id. to 17s. 6d. Alderman 131 RTOS seconded the adoption of the minutes. The NIAVOR said the subiect of ending houses for those people displaced by the closing of insanitary dwellings was a difficult and wide sabject whioh ought tote considered very carefully. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>The minutes were'sp-proved: SHORTAGE OF SETTS FOR STREETS. At &amp; meeting of the Paving and Seiversge Committee,</p>
        <p>the TEA% x CLiRK submitted correspondence with Mr. A. A. R. Lang, of fiourock, with regard to the delivery of setts under the totaling contracts. end reported thereon ; and it was resolved that the minute of this Committee passed on the 15th Mey last accept- ing the tenders of Mess's. Clare and Ridgway, Ltd., and Mr. A. A. R. Lang for the supply of 1,000 tons each of lin. granite cubes be rescinded. It was also resolved shat the tender of Messrs. Alexander Stark and Sons, Ltd., of Glasgow, for the supply of 1,000 tots of 41u. granite cubes, be accepted, scbject to their being satisfactory to the Borough Surveyor. At the Council meeting it was explained by Alderman TINSItos that the first-barred firms had contracts running with the Corporation which took up practically all their available supply of setts, and as the paving contractors' of the town wanted more setts urgently for street work the Committee thought it advisable to transfer this additional contract to another quarry, and that of Stark and Sons was selected. He (Alderman Tianion) and tce Bort:nigh Surveyor had visited the quarry and were perfectly satisfied with the setts and the arrangements for prompt delivery. The minutes were agreed to.</p>
        <p>CRITICISMS OF THE LIBRARY. SLUMP IN POETRY. WORKINGMEN AND THE REFERENCE LIBRARY. Alderman BEN-NT:TT moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Muse= Committee, which contained the mime report of the Dii ector and Librarian (Mr. Madeley), a summary of which appeared in our last issue. With regard to the percentages of the different classes of books issued the Aldernsan said he did not</p>
        <p>know that he would shed tears Over theolo hi h . -</p>
        <p>bad only 9 per cent. It looked as if there was a slump in theology. (Laughter.) It was not creditable to Warrington to find that with such a famous library the issue per cent, of books on poetry and the drama I was only '9, while for prose fiction it was I'7l. It was a pity that in these sordid dnys, especially in an industrial town, where the conditions were so unimaginative and so commercial, that people did not cultivate a taste for those books which stimulated the imagination. But it was typical of the prevailing levity of our national life, which spoke ill for the future of the country. Be hoped the public would make full use of the Museum and Library, which was rich in all sorts of treasures and in every branch of literature. Over the returns of the demand for poetry be could shed tears. Councillor ROBERT, We often shecltears orer it.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERN,YNT (who seconded the confirmation of the minutes) : There are shoals or poe's in Warrington, but we are ashamed to publish cur works.</p>
        <p>(Loud laughter.) Councillor PEMBERTON thought the real reason that the poetry books were not patronised so much at the Library was beceuse those who bad a tiste for them could afford to buy the hooks. He regretted that Mr. Greening's collection of reptiles was locked in a room of which the pecple were not aware. (Hear, beer). Councillor Pemberton also complained about the Reference Library which he alleged was locked up most of the tim.. People hardly knew of the fine Reference Library we had, frr when they passed icto the reading room and looked in tha direction of the reference library they saw Mr. Madeley (the librarian) sitting at his desk and they naturally thought it was his private cince. He (the speaker) advocated free access to the books. Other towns had it and he did not see why Warrington should not. Ours was the first publiF, library, and despite our start we lagged behind</p>
        <p>La fine sort of Miumum Commi tee, ;min% it? (Laughter). As to what the people should read, the committee should not dictate to the ritepayers. The ratepayers had to pay and therefore they had the right to say what they would read. Alderman Bennet t had spoken about the public not beiug serious, but the majority of the public had to he serious—poor beggars. They worked for .£1 a week and the only recreation they bad was reading some novel. It was their only pleasure in life. Alderman BOLTON: They would sooner have a pint of beer than read. (Laughter.) Continuing, Councillor PEN:BEI:TON said that after working hard all day they could not expect people to read philosophy sad theology as they wanted some recreation. Alderman BOLTON: There ere too many daily papers there. That is why they don't read hooks. LIOUOCiIIOr PEDIDELITON concluded by saying that the Council ought to waken the Museum Committee up and if they would not put the Library in a forward position then they should step the supplies. Councillor ROBt:RTS said that Alderman Bennett had spoken about the spirit of levity and they had just had a fair sample of it, for he could not for a moment think that Councillor Pemberton bad made his remarks in any other spirit than that of levity. (Hear, hear and laughter.) fie (Mr. Roberta) would not suggest that he had no imagii ation, for they had never had a better instance of a powerful imagination than what they had just heard. Everyone of his remarks in regard to the Reference Library was a sample. There was not the slightest bar to any citizen of the town who wished to use the Library. And beside wherever the Reference Library was the people who wished to use it would not be slow to ask, it was an admitted fact that the Reference Library was used to-day considerably more than in the past. One thing which ought to be borne in mind was the fact that the committee had only a l(d. rate by which to carry on their operations. Mr Roberts further pointed out that the reason Mr Greening's collection were placed in a separate room was because the specimens were very valuable and should he kept away from the light. Mr. Madelay was always willing to show them to anybody who wished to see them. At the same time he suggested that there should be some notice drawing the attention of the public to the fact that there was such a celiac tion. He did not agree with Alderman Bennett that because 70 per cent, read novels they were actustedby a spirit of levity. How many fine moral principles were better told through the medium of fiction than by any other means ! (Hear, hear.) It was a very rare thing indeed that an improper book in fiction ever found its way into the Library and by a carefully selected library of fixtion no harm but a con- siderable amount of good was being done. By all means let them encourage the readirg of the works to which Alderman Bennett had referred, but when it came to poetry—well. speak tag as a voracious reader of novels he would infinitely prefer them to verses. (Laughter). Councillor BROADLIURST said that practically the whole of Warrington were in entire ignorance of the gift of Mr. Greening and he sugg, steel that a notice should be put up outside the room in which the collection was kept. He agreed with Councillor Pemberton in regard to the reference library. The opinion generally throughout the working classes was that it was kept for the upper classes. If it were suggested to the ordinary workingman that he should go into it he would say that he felt he wan intruding and imposing. But no blame must he attached to Mr Madeiey for that, because it was the position of the room. The officials were to be commended for the way in which they performed their dirties. Councillor LASHFORD said that when he was a member of the Museum Committee he went to the door of the Reference Library and was challenged by Mr. Madeley, who let him in when he told him he wax a member of the committee. He had been to the Refer- ence Library three times since, and had found the door . locked. _</p>
        <p>Conneillor ROBERTS : I don't believe you. Councillor CUNNINGTON : Somebody is telling lies. Councillor HAWTHORN said this year the Liverpool Corporation had allowed free access to the books. Ho thought that they were the first Library to do it. Councillor PEMBERTON said he was able to produce many people who could testify to the dcor of the Reference Library being locked. Councillor ROBERTS said that the door proper was kept unlocked, but there was a small catch at the counter door (which led to the Reference Librarj) which could easily be undone. Alderman BENNEET, in replying to the discussion, said thet if Councillor Pemberton, when he was a member of the Museum Committee, bad attended to the work a little better be would have been more familiar with the circumstances. The public could have any book of fiction they liked providing there was nothing objectionable in it. The Alderman said that one of the greatest difficulties they had to face was their accom- modation, but they were doing their beat to carry out the wishes of the public. The minutes were then agreed to. MINERAL WATERS AND MUNICIPAL BOWLING GRERNS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Town Ball, Parks and Baths Committee a petition signed by 66 patrons of the Victoria Park Bowling Green, was real, requesting the committee to allow mineral waters to be sold at the bowling greens, and it was resolved that as the season is no far advanced the matter be deferred until the beginning of the neat season. Councillor PFNIRERTON said the request was a sermon- able one and he considered that the committee had not done right in passing such a resolution when the season wan not advanced at the time when it was passed, i.e. 29th July. There had been plenty of hot weather since and it was only fsir that the men should be afforded reasonable facilities for refreshment. In other towns it was allowed ant it would tea sonroe of income, for they could let ast (nd to someone. Ile moved that the minute be sent back for farther consideration. Alderman BOLTON It is wrong to supply them I according to the bylaws and wrong according to the I time of the year. Councillor PE3IIIFRTON : We want something to drink all the year through. (Laughter.) Alderman BOLTON said he was willing that the matter should be referred back as it would save discussion—he wanted to go home. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS thought that that should not be</p>
        <p>done until the matter had been discussed. 110 found himself in the unique position of agreeing with every word Councillor Pemberton bad said. (Laughter. Councillor \WILKINSON wanted to know why mineral waters could not he supplied Would it he illegal for anyone to go into the pork to sell bottles of mineral water? The Town CLERK : illegal without the sand ion of the committee. Councillor WILKINSON : Suppose a woman walked in with a basket containing ginger beer ? The Tows CLERK : She could not take It into a public park without permission. Councillor WILKINSON 7. It is a downright shame. Alderman BOLTON said there were ratepayers near the spot who could supply mineral waters, bet if a bowler left be had to pay • penny to come back. That was the real cause of the trouble, but a remedy could be found by altering the rules. Councillor WILKINSON a The summer is DOE yet over. There are six weeks of play yet. The amendment was carried. LIVERPOOL-ROAD RECREATION GROUND. Councillor BROADfilliwr alluded to the minute of this committee instructing the Surveyor to report OD the condition of the Liverpool-road Recreation Ground, and said he hoped that it would he made into a pleasure retort. It could be made as pleasant apiece as Queen's Gardens by the provision of chairs and the planting of tree..</p>
        <p>BOWLING GREEN IN ET. ELPHIN'S PARK The Tows CLERK reported to the Parks Committee that the sanction of the Local Government Board bad been received to the construction of a bowling green at St.Elphie's Park, and that in accordance with the previous resolution of the committee, he had given instructions for the work to be proceeded with. Councillor Locweit asked when St. Elphin's Park bowling green would be begun ? Alderman BOLTON.—Tbe matter is in hand and the contract is signed. (Hear, bear.) The other minutes of the committee were agreed to THE MARKETS. The minutes of the Markets Committtee contain a summary of the annual report of the Inspector of Weights end Measures :—Out-door inspection : Weights examined, 11,686 ; measures, 14,005 ; weighing instruments, 2,604 ; weights correct, 3,897 ; measnres, 13,531 ; weighing instruments,2,o94 ; weights incorrect, 7,789; measures, 654 ; weighing instruments, 510 • coal carts examined, 179 ; bags weighed, 471 ; bread carts ease:deed.? Isecloor verification Number of weights verified, 11,629; measures, 45,910; weighing imam- masts, 683; weights already stamped and correct, 912 ; measures, 25 ; instruments, 48 ; number of weights stamped, 10,528 ; measures, 45,910 ; instruments, 683 ; number of weights rejected or condemned, 189; measures, 5,384 ; Instruments, 48 ; number of v eights adjusted, 9,833; measures, 37 ; instruments,—. Alderman JANIE, EVANS moved and Councillor ASHCROFT seconded the passing of the minutes. Replying to Conncillor Hawthorn, Alderman JAMES EVANS ohserved that an effort was being made to pre- vent children eating rotten fruit which was found in the mutat. The committee were endeavouring to remedy the matter. The difficulty was in keeping the children from the receptacles. The minutes were confirmed. WARRINGTON GAS COOKERS FOR WARRINGTON. Alderman PIERPOIrT moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Gas Committee and in doing so referred to the following minutes : " Letters from Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., and the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., with reference to the purchase of gas coot e-s by the committee, were vi a 4. The Engineer was reqiieste I to inquire and report upon the size of gas cookers used in other towns." "Letters were read from Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., and the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., with respect to the charges for the supply of gas." He said the question ot the gas cookers was being carefully considered by the committee, and they hoped to to able to keep the orders within the town. (Hear, hear.) The committee would strain every point to do so. There were complaints that the charges for the supply of gas were too high. He agreed, and this matter was &amp;so under consideration. Alluding to the application of the labourers at the Longford and Mersey-street works for an increase of psy, he mid he and Alderman Tinnion bad met the men, who submitted four point", which be would not disclose but which would have favourable consideration. He believed the matter would go on as pleasantly in the future as in the past. (Hear, hear ) Councillor BROADHURST : I am glad Alderman Pierpoint has alluded to the matter. I hope we vi al hear that the application has been acceded to. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were passed. THE NEW ENGINE AND TUE 'WATER SUPPLY. At a meeting of the Water Committee, the Engineer submitted the report of Mr. Michael Longridge on the specification for the proposed new engine at Houghton Green, which was approved, and it was resolved that tenders for a new engine be advertised for. Alderman JOHN EVANS, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, alluded to the question of the proposed new engine. The speciflation had been carefully drawn up. Councillor PEMBERTON asked if a certain pattern had been prescribed, which would prevent free competition. Alderman EVANS replied that no one had beea pre- cluded from tendering. A certain engine was described in the specification, and the committee had good reason for asking for one tube supplied on the same principle. Proceeding, he alluded to the strike, and said he authorized the purchase of 50 tons of coal at,, increased price to provide against emergency, es be was informed that the coal in stock would only last 21 days. It was delivered, but they were nut called upon to use it. Alderman EVANS added that the western portion of the town was now furnished with a complete supply of water. The higher pada of the town on the western side of Market Gate were perfectly supplied but they had to go further and finish the eastern side. The inhabitants on the east ride of the church were very muck inconvenienced because the mains were not sufficient to meet present requirements. On many occasions there wee little or no water running through the mains. The attention of the committee was called to the matter yesterday and they decided to proceed at once with the 12 inch main they had power to con- struct. It was intended to start at klarshhouse-lane and go along Padgate-laue, Manchester-road, Church- street, Buttermarketatreet. The Aldermen concluded by observing thet the additional cost would be included in the £17,000 sanctioned by the Local Government Board. (Hear, hear.) Councillor LOCKER said it would be a great pleasure to residents to know that they were going to have an adequate sof-ply of water. Baths in the housee had been scarcely of any service for many years for they had to wait half smhour to get a sufficient supply of water, which dribbled in, for a bath. (Laughter.) Thin was very unpleasant. The minutes were confirmed. THE STRIKE AND THE COAL SUPPLY. In proposing the adoption of the minutes of the Electricity and Tramways Committee Alderman Smet- hurst nised objection to the following note which appeared in last Saturday's issue of the Examiner : " There in a significant note under the minutes of the Water Committee for August 2lsl, wherein approval is given of the action of the Chairman of the Committee in sutborising the purchase of 50 tons of coal at an increased price, to provide against emergency, during the recent strike. We believe it is a fact that the dislocation of the rail- way services found both the Water and Electricity Departments without any coal reserves, and there was, consequentty. some scurrying about to secure enough fuel for a fortnight. It would be advisable for departments such as these, which are responsible for public services, to make a practice of always bavin genough coal in stock to tide them over such dit9cultiee. The hand to mouth policy is too precarious in these days of labour unrest." The Alderman said he should be unworthy of the position he held as Chairman of the Electricity and Tramways Committee if he did not refer to the para- graph, because they supplied a great many people with electricity power. He only wished that those people, when they wanted to get information, would go to the fountain bead. And the result of them not doing so was The Alderman, then noticing that Councillor Pemberton was smiling, remarked that he had no need to act likes laughing hyena. (Langhter). Councillor LASHIORD 1 Conncillor Pemberton only wanted to know who was the fountain head. Alderman Sktfcruces2 : They would not go to you for it. They might go for a cow's head. (Loud laughter.) Continuing, the Alderman said that such a statement in the paper might give a wrong impression to those who got their electricity from the Corpora- tion. At the time the paragraph was inserted they had no leas than 200 tons of coal in stock and had had ever since the new boilers were put down, and between 15 and 20 tons of slack and coal were coming in per day. Councillor WILKINSON seconded the adoption of the minutes and remarked : "1 hope we shall not haves, much Examiner 'slack in the future." (Laughter). THE ELECTION OF MAYOR. It was resolved that the next meeting of the General Purposes Committee be convened for 6 p.m. on the 25th September, and that the meeting for the selection of Mayor, in accordance with Shindies Orders, be held immediately tarter that meeting. THE PROPOSED HOWLEY BRIDGE. Alderman PORSHAW proposed and Alderman Stir:mu-awe seconded the minutes of the General Purposes Committee. Councillor ARCHER asked what progress was being made with regard to the new bridge at Howley. Considering the matter had been banging fire so long It W4ll time the ratepayers of the Howley and Fairfield Wards beard something about what was being done. (hear, bear). Councillor JACKSON HOLMES 8180 desired to put the earns qrsestion. The Tows CLF.RK said that the tenders for the bridge were obtained foes dill. rent site to that now proposed, and new tenders had been asked for and would be considered next week. Councillor JACKSON HOLME, asked if the tenders would be submitted to the Council before being passed? The Tows CLERK : Yes. Councillor JAC itSOS: HOLMES said he hoped that • more substantial bridge might be erected than sai actually intended at present. Councillor Pest BERTON remarked there was no qtrs- tion of any other bridge but a footbridge. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>VISIT TO POMPEII.</head>
        <p>A VISIT TO POMPEII.</p>
        <p>THE BURIED CITY OF THE ROMANS.</p>
        <p>By Charles</p>
        <p>E. Parker.</p>
        <p>ARTICLI</p>
        <p>No. 2.1</p>
        <p>BRONZE S A.CRIFICIAL AL records of such homely matters as the number of tunics sent to the wash ; how much wool had been given out to be spun by the slaves of the household ; bow many flasks of wine had been bought &amp;c. THE SHOPS AND ARTISANS.</p>
        <p>The frontage of the streets is most likelyl taken up with what have been shops and tradesmen's premises. Many of these have been identified. There is a dyer's yard where the dye vats, about the size of a modern cottage house wash boiler, are still intact with the colours in them distinct and vivid. There is a sculptor's workshop where several unfinished marble pieces were found, and which are now stored in the Museum at Naples. There is also a wine shop with the word Salve in mosaic on the threshold—the publicans of to-day might take the tip. Still are to be seen the stone seats where the customers sipped their wine and talked over their neighbours' affairs, and may be of the doings of the Great Emperors at the Capital. In the same building is a tomb, also a palumbarium, or receptacle for cinerary urns, holding, I presume, the ashes of those who believed in cremation. The ancients had a curious, morbid habit of reminding each other of death. Tombs and other evidences of our mortality were put in the most promi- nent places, but outside the city. All the entrances to Rome are lined on either side with tombs, and the Appean Way is like an avenue of death. They did not share Abraham's feeling when he said "Bury my dead out of my sight." Nor is this attitude of mind confined to the ancients, it still holds good with all the Continental Latin races. An undertaker's shop can be seen in almost every street with coffins of all sizes exposed to view. You could get fitted with a coffin as easily as with a ready-made suit of clothes. Still we should hardly expect to find a tomb in a drink shop, and yet we have all heard of the custom in ancient banquets where a skeleton was carried through the feasting room to remind the revellers of their mortality. In a baker's yard were found several stone mills for grinding corn. One of these is still there and we got a photograph which is here reproduced. These mills are on the two stone cone principle and have apertures for the shafts by which the top stone wa% made to rotate by hand or donkey labour. In this baker's yard was found by the exca- vators an oven containing about forty loaves in good preservation. The baking' no doubt was completed by the heated ashes from Vesuvius enveloping the oven. The loaves are quite black, carbonized by the heat. In the ordinary course they would be about one pound loaves, circular in shape, and decorated with a rude pattern. I found out after leaving that there were the remains of a tannery in Pompeii. I missed this—missed the most interesting thing in the place. The relics are mostly stored in the Museum at Naples and there we saw the loaves spoken of above. There are also eggs looking very modern in size and shape; during two</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>'AK It OUND AT YOMVELL</head>
        <p>'AK It OUND AT YOMVELL</p>
        <p>and which in many places is aa fresh as when it was put on, and the surface is as hard as flint. The frescoes represent various historical and mythical scenes of ancient poems and lege -ids, as well as many domestic incidents whi.2l serve to throw light on the manners and customs of the ancient Romans. There are few attempts at landscape painting. The aim of the painters in both Greece and Rome appears to be to give prominence to repre- sentations of the human form. The figures are full of life and grace, but wherever there are trees and landscape introduced these are poor. Many of these frescoes and mosaics have been removed to the National Museum at Naples, but there are a good number still left in the principal houses of Pompeii. In the Museum there is a mosaic taken from Pompeii. eighteen feet by twelve, containing one and a half million of pieces. On the occasion of our visits several paintm-s were 11 at work copying the best of these frescoes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>THEATRE.</head>
        <p>THE THEATRE.</p>
        <p>There is something uncanny about Pompeii. We see many places and things as old and not a few older, but such are more or less changed, weathered by the passage of the years. But in Pompeii this seasoning by time is to a great extent absent. When you walk these streets, enter thes.r• houses, see the fresh paintings on the walls, view the objects of daily use, see the children's toys and the theatre checks, you lose the sense of the passing of the centuries, an eerie feeling takes possession. You would scarcely be surprised if on turning a corner in a house you were confronted by the owner and asked your business and your right to invade his domicile. Mark Twain appears to have experienced this feeling when he visited the theatre but instead of it transferring him back to the first century it seemed to call the Pom7eans into the Nineteenth, or rather mixed the two periods together. He approacl-.es the theatre, reads an imaginary placard in infamous grammar. " Positively no Free List, Except Members of the Press I entered the thsatre ' I.esays, " and sat down ca o.ct t:f tie long rime'let no let:aches Id !lobed at the pLoe fcr the crAestra sal the mined stage, and aratod at weep cf empty bones and thsught to tnuae arnal't p.ay. I ft:el to imagine ',Lat, tie leader if the erch s're --s•i'e ea a d so (who bad -ss,rnt t nr in the</p>
        <p>in the &amp;es; c de. al</p>
        <p>the iv:de Neve</p>
        <p>myself,</p>
        <p>beating ti e,ant tt ' just zetu</p>
        <p>f rol</p>
        <p>Lees to play his Lit and farewell engage- eot of p-sitirely eit r•ihte oolr. in Pompeii. parture t, lie cuhiniun,'j an.' piling the agony Li not do it with such a 'Lou.' 114 that those empty leeches tied toy dlocy down to dull reality rreallzed these peeple that olght to be here hare been dead, and tell, inoul,iering to dust for ages and ages. And so I tunted awq. sal weal through shop eter shop far d 'an the lorg stet of the merchsnts and called for the wares cf firms end the Etst, bat the tradesmen wee gone, the marts were silent and nothing ens left but the Iwolon jun all set in cement cf cinder, and ashes : the wine and the oil that once had filled them were gone wilt the:r owners.'</p>
        <p>prert us cliargiLg rec3d the cc:::ntsios high—hut I c</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>BATHS.</head>
        <p>THE BATHS.</p>
        <p>No Roman city was complete without it. baths, and in Pompeii I read they had three, but we had no time to visit these. As in Rome these baths were in sets of three, i.e.. there was the hot, the tepid, and the cold rooms and baths, or as they were called the caldariurn, the tepidari unr, and the tigidarium. BRONZES AND MARBLES.</p>
        <p>Although the bulk of the relics unearthed at Pompeii illustrate the life of the people, and are therefore specially interesting, the town was by no means devoid of objects of art. The bronzes do not compare in value or number with those of Herculanium, still there are some very fine specimens. There are also a few works in marble, including a number of busts of the great men of the time. These were used in the decoration of the better class houses, and especially for the fountains and bathing places which frequently were to be found occupying a central position enclosed by a covered portico to which the surrounding rooms opened. To illustrate the character of the bronze work I have had reproduced a photograph of a</p>
        <p>AN OLD CORN MILL (The figures in the photo are not ancient Romans. and may be recognised).</p>
        <p>Sacrificial Tripod Altar which wa, found in Pompeii. This beautiful altar, though green with age, is still perfect, and is a fine example of the advanced culture both in art and workmanship of those bygone days. The artist has evidently borrowed from Egypt the winged lion with human head. We too have adapted from these beautiful relics much of our present day decoration, notably the festoon round the vessel and the supporting foot. It is my good fortune to possess a very fine reproduc- tion of this altar, and any reader interested in bronze work is welcome to inspect this and other pictures of famous Roman bronzes.</p>
        <p>jfick • A , , • , • •• , 4r,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>FAITHFUL UNTO DEATH.</head>
        <p>FAITHFUL UNTO DEATH.</p>
        <p>Everyone knows the picture and the story of the brave Roman soldier who lost his life through his fidelity, standing at his post as sentinel until the falling burning ashes ended his life. Unfortunately at Pompeii I could find no eviuence of the truth of this story although given in many guide books and made the theme of a famous picture. No doubt if there is any truth in this tale it was a striking instance of fidelity to duty, but I am disposed to think it was also a striking instance of stupidity. A Roman soldier ought to be a free man ; if he acted as this legend affirms he was a slave to the letter of his command, and had not appreciated it,. spirit. There is another tale we used to hear of in our youth and which I believe has no foundation in fact. It is told in the poetry I which begins " The Boy stood on the burning Deck." There is yet another story about Pompeii which I think, must also be a fable. It has been said that a dove was found upon a nest with her eggs beneath her which she had refused to desert, and paid the price by her life. This may possibly be true, but as the entombment of the city is believed to have occurred in November it is very unlikely. I think this is all I have to tell about Pompeii at present. I should like next to give your readers our impressions of Rome. I wonder if I will have courage to tackle such a gigantic theme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>NERVES AND NEURALGIA.</head>
        <p>NERVES AND NEURALGIA.</p>
        <p>Go to any chemist and obtain ore ounce of Aker', one ounce of Glycerine, and half an ( once of Tincture of Gentian. Pr.ur these into a six-ounce bottle and fill up with water. The dose is a tea.poonfol three or four times daily, in water if pief,rred. Jo sever,. eases take a dose hourly until relieved. This very simple mixture will cure the worst possible attack of nerves, neuralgia, sleeplessness, headache, and similar ailments arising from a run- down state of health, will entirely remove " that tired feeling,'' and promotes digesti in and good spirts. Dr. flood, who di,covered the methods of Alvexa, believes it to be the greatest and cheapest tonic in the world. It is perfectly harmless, and whilst possessed of marvellims recuperative merits, leaves no ill-effect whatever.</p>
