      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>TILE PAIL) SPEAKER OF UNIONISM.</head>
        <p>TILE PAIL) SPEAKER OF UNIONISM.</p>
        <p>" An Indiscreet Unionist " contributes an attic!e to the" World's Work " in which he makes the following oh/wreath° on the paid speaker :—Whatever be the co-ordinating evil inspiring all that is wrong wit' Unionism, its symbol end monument and spokesman In that melancholy figure the ;Aid speaker, whom everyone most have seen and heard at some time, lie stand', in lois cart or on bin platform wearing a black tail coat and a gold mach chain, boasting that he it a working man, loud of voice and tierce of gesture sometimes sober and sometimes drunk, calling on the Name of God end quoting the examples of Nel.ao, Wolfe, and Wellington, quoting figures from the half- penny Press and patriotism from Shakespeare. vow- ing beliefs upon lois soul and conacience.lastaing himself to frenzy, and all for twa pound ten a weet who no longer put our trust in princes, rely upon the potent help of this creature whom we pay to befoul the electors is. 4 save on from th 3 Radicals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>CHILD'S FATAL PLAY.</head>
        <p>CHILD'S FATAL PLAY.</p>
        <p>Willie Whitlow (33, of Sidnepetreet, Weston Point, was playing near Wright's Dock, when his ball rolls into the water. The child overbalanced In trying to reach his toy. Captain Jonathsn Hughes renovexed the body speedily by grappling, but life was extinct.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>10T1</head>
        <p>10T1</p>
        <p>By " Orallst. " Members and general public alike were well satisfied with the opening match of the season at Wilderspool. The team more than realised expectations and accounte 1 fur Leigh by a substantial margin. Tine, there were weak places made manifest, bit these will be strengthened as time goes on, and I re no reason why the Wirepullers should not hare a first-cies.. team before the season is many weeks old.</p>
        <p>The infusion of new blood c mimenced just at the right time, mid it was a stroke of genius which secured Woodburn of Coventry for the opening match. Without him there would have been a cons.derably less gate. and probably much less satisfaction locally at the conclusion cf hostilities. Both club and player were lucky on Saturday, for opportunities came to the new threequarter which enabled him to cover himself with glory. As a rule a new man has trn be satisfied with small successes when be first appears among strange companions and before a critical ciowd. 00 Saturday the run of the play enabled Woodburn to score two tries and—what was almost more imporlant —to save tgo others which Leigh would hare got brio for his speed and sure tackling. Opportunity came and Woodburn grasped it with both hands. Without rming a hasty opinion—because I have seen the player thrte times—l think Woodburn will wake a name for himself and for the club. He is young, clever, and enthusiastic. Here's luck to the DV% Wit epuller</p>
        <p>Thoto</p>
        <p>Tonge</p>
        <p>• W. WOODBURN, Warrington's new wing threequarter.</p>
        <p>With Warren fit anain, and in the form which dis- tinguished him in the early part of last season, the wings should be better than for eome time past. The greatest weakness is at centre, where a man of real race and dash is badly required. I have the utmast respect for George D:ckinsin. Ile is a good fellow and a good centre, but he is not as fast as he was ten years ago. and he i 4 not fast enough for the rest of the backs. NJ one reabass this better than Dickenson imself, and by not trying to do too much he keeps t be line together very well, Lot that promized new centre is urgently required. Unssrorth seems to have improved and on S.tarday he and Woodburn made a very effective wing. Bradshaw was disappointing again. If be would only show more resolution and use his speed he would do better, but I am afraid he can only be looked upon as a substitute.</p>
        <p>The best part of the team is undoubtedly the halves. Brooks and Cree my played a game that was Lot only powerful but brilliantly clever. It i, a long tote since Brooks worked the pack and broke away to cleanly, and he served Creevey well throughout. The !attar was both artistic and effective. Once we have got the right centres behind this pair, and the lack of sccring power which was so obvious last season, will be remedied.</p>
        <p>The forward duel was interesting, and, on the whole, I think Leigh had the pull. They got posseesion oftener, but were not as well served behind. Warrington's sx were irresistible in spurts, but there are too many old heads (and legs) in the pack for the pace to be maintained. (Johnston, who captained the tram with judgment). Cheaters and Skelhorn were the pick. Tiiley fought a sound rear- guard action, but it was solely rearguard ! Unlike Boleweki he could not turn defence into attack, and was take left standing by the Leigh wings. A change is needed here, but until we have a better man the steady-going Widnesian will do.</p>
        <p>can imagine someone saying " What about Mclntyre ? " from all I can learn at head- quarters Mac has only himself to blame for not being on the team this week-end. In an interview with the Committee he showed a very stubborn attitude on the quest;on of terms, with the result that he has been unanimously grassed and given titre to reflect Meanwhile there is another back in view, and it would be wise of Mac if he pocketed some pride and made ma effort to demonstrate his loyalty to the Club as a player and a Warrington ha He has his chance now—will he let it slip ?</p>
        <p>Juat a word abont the men of Leigh. They were handicapped by the absence of Tom Johnson, but showed themselves a workmanlike side. Without being really classy anywnere bnt at full back, they vete reliable in all departtnents and should have a fair season. I cannot fancy them gaining many victaries on fore,gn sit, however, without an accession of strength in the back division.</p>
        <p>One thing on Saturday drew unqualified praise and that was the appearance of the ground. The improve- ments are mo,t striking, and to-El ty Warrington p ~es.es an enclosure which is second to none in the Northern Union world. In the brilliant sun of a September afternoon, with new paint sparkling and a crowd which lined the ring completey, the ground was a picture. Mr. Tom Locker the genial president. opened the new stand in a very fi;ting manner, and treated the company to a very happy speech. Members were busy picking their seats for the season's campaign, congratulating themselves on the excellent aecumnodation provided. and the fine view (.1 the play from all parts of the stand.</p>
        <p>Our A "team also began well by belting Leigh " A" at Mather-lane, and with the handicap of a man short all through. Several of the juniors acquitted themselves well, notably Critchley at centre and Brocklehurst forward. Ike Taylor alio showed that he has pienty of football in him yet, and is a stalwart among the juniors. Men like he and Dickenson can do good service by a spell in the "A " team bringing cnt the young fry.</p>
        <p>To-day, Warrington are due at St. Helens, where they won last season by 17 points to 9—a Tinton- that was somewhat discounted by the fact that the Saints retaliated by winning at Wilderapool later in the season. I was hopeful of seeing one or two nece•sary changes in the Warrington side, but owing to various causes tbe backs are the same, and the only change forward is Naylor for Boardman. Waywell is still " on the club," but should be fit for play in another week.</p>
        <p>The team will be : Tilley, full back ; Woodburn, l'nsworth, Dickenson, and Bradshaw, three quarters ; Brooks and Creesey, half backs ; Singers, Johnston, Che..ters, Skelhorn, Naylor and Thomas, forwards.</p>
        <p>The "A" team has no less than six juniors in it, most of them lad+ who have shown both pace and promise. They are getting a thorough trial. and I hope some of them will qualify for regular berths The thirteen selections are ; Fletcher (P. and Kl, full back; Shakeshaft (Crosfielle), I Taylor, Critchley Belenal and Posey (Stockton Heath), three- ,riarters Lloyd and Daintith, half backs : Harmer, Brocklehurat (Widnes), Arnold and GI een (Wigan), Bradshaw and Gibb., forwards.</p>
        <p>44. 411, .400</p>
      </div>
