      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>New Player Heads the Scoring.</head>
        <p>New Player Heads the Scoring.</p>
        <p>The 1911-12 football season had a ward sendeaff at Wildersposi on Saturday. when the spacioue and well- appointed ground of the Warrington Club was enercled , by al enthuskstio crowd of spectators numbering quite 7,000. As the werther was more et:liable fur lawn tennis or cricket than the wirier game. the *atter:dance was very satisfactory, and augurs well br the Club. The hands:eine new members wean. was 'smelly opened Cal-an-hour before the match by Mr. T. Locker, presi- dent of the ch.'s. Warring. ore were represented by practically the Bottle p rusts who have done duty in recent sensor s, the only new-csmer being W. Woodbure, right three-quarter of the esventry Club. Great interest was Emma in his .1 but. gad at the closer of the game he had made himeel a favourite by a skilful and pluaky exhibition. Leigh were also well represented, though they missed T. Jslansou at centre. The teams were as follow:</p>
        <p>Warrington : T.lky, back ; Bradshaw, Dickenson, Casstoith aod Woodhuro. tbree-qoarter backs; Brook( s and Crtevey, half back.; Shuns:a. Johnston, 'lh nuns, Boardman, Skelborn, and Cheaters, forgards. Leigh B.lewk, back ; S Jcbusos, Davies, Atkin- son and Ganley, une,ti tirter had a; it!sker and WGeiTer, half hacks ; V,cood,, Marsh, Lowe, Ogilvy, Barton sod Lee, forwards. _ . _ .</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr A He tford, Ilrrmghtoo. Warrington Lott too toss sad ruoruss stnrted against son and breeze. Leigh were very energetic, end nippy play by Whittaker at the bane or tho serum leiultod Tilley having to clear. The v •teran full bock pr ,ved quite v ife aol thw boll was tranoferred to mid-field.</p>
        <p>IN DAY: the Stand which did duty when the</p>
        <p>Play was here for only a mown', for the visiting forwards seemed to mettle down to their work sooner than the Warriugt-n men and forced matters at the railway eat Brooke and Creevey cleverly broke away and tested Bolewski, syko In cle a poor kick, an I the homesters attacked. Leigh gat pormessi en, however, and smart passing resulted in Brads'ut v being drawn on theright. Sam Johnson Blipped by and war sood making tracks for the goal-line. Tilley was easily elu 'e I and the chase seemed hopeless when Woodhurn wis seen to bs tearing across from the oppos'te wing. Tr thriliing race was watched in breathless suspense until the Warrington acquisition over-hauled Johnson end grassed him a yard from the line. Then there was roar of cheering which was heard a few miles away, and Woodburn was at once in the good graces of the crowd. Before Leigh could be driven hack they opened the sooting in another way. Warrington were penalised and &amp;inlay placed a neat goal. The reverse did Warrington good, and come dashing play both fore end aft won witnessed. Brooke, C eevey, and Unsworth came away with beautiful passing at mid- field. Unsworth friend Woodburn c csered and doubling smartly inside he threw the Leigh defer rai into a tangle. Cerise y came up at a fast Face and te Bolewski was reached accepted the tall from the centre end completed a pretty movement under the posts amid great cheering. Thomas kicked a goal Warrington were now going ahead, in • great style and *powerful run by Brooks failed n as the corner through a bad pse to Bradshaw. Johnston, Coasters and Slrelborn were following up wall, and the Leto I custodian only saved again by a indiCi 505 mirk. The punt was fielded by Woodburn, ash, ran nod kicked loftily into the far corner. Here Johnson missed the , ball on the bounce and Cheaters and Boardman srere over in • trice the latter notching the try. 'Thome missed the goal. It was now Leigh's turn, and a strong , burst by Gallop and Marsh sow Tilley collared close in. ' Scrummaging followed, end the left wing being lefs unmarked, At ciason slipped over easily from slcGiever's transfer. Ganley failed at gm% and half time arrived with Warrington healing by d points to 5 The resumption saw Leigh first on the attack, but Johnston led his men well to the other end. Botewski saved daringly, but failed to clear, and the Warrington men heeling out the ball was soon on the move. Creevey (ire,/ the defence beautifully before passing to Uonworth who ran well and threw to Woodburn. Tree latter took the ball on the run and racing past all opposition scored a grand try behind the posts, and also added a goal. Ooze again Leigh invaded, but the passing wan too orthodox to break down ma home defence. Carelessness brought its penalty however The ball came loose near the Warringron coal, and both Tilley and Bradadaw failed to clear, with the !emit the. Atkinson sloshed up and dribbled over near the poets Ganley adding an easy goal. With only a three poiat- lead the Warrington men put in all they knew and were soon In loigh territory. 0 ualey caused • thrill be breaking clear away and running over half the length of the field before falling a victim to Woodburn', au ovior pace. Cremes, brake throuxh braining', with a short punt and splick gathering and took play to the I Leigh rprirter. Here Brooks secured and with • dash- ing run cleared the centres and parted to Woodburn who sprinted over with his second try at the corner. The scorer failed at goal. Warrington were now all over the TiE1.50115 and Creevey missed a p as fr in</p>
        <p>3 OF OLD. ground was behind S. James' Church.</p>
        <p>Aldermon Tttgmots, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Mayor and 3lsyoress eel Aldermen for their presence, said be could say witsout hesitation that was the ti lest ground in the Northern Union, and they hoped to make the surroundiegs the finest in the Northern Union. (Hear, hoar,. He thought that Northern Union footbal was beginning to lake its proper place in the sports of the day. (Applause). Mr. If. Ashton seconded the vote of thanks to ably proposed by Mr. Alderman Tinnion to Mr. Locker, the president of the club and ass to the Mayor and Mayoress. Mr. Ashton made mention of Mr. Locker's career as an athlete who had adorned the world of sport as a runner and summer with great suc:ess. (Hear, hear.) He bad taken a very autive interest in the management of the Club for a great number of years, dirEcting his eff nu earnestly for the good of the game, as well as sell as the welfare of the players. (Applause) Eur'ng Its presidency great improvements bad been u. dertalen in the groins&amp;' Mr. Locker giving his close attention to various details during the alterations. He wished for bins is most successful moots, and trusted the players would show their loyalty by trying to make his period of rffica one of the best 'in the history of the Club. (Hear, hear.) 'The Rugby game sae one of the greatest games played I in this courery, and be should like to see it have its fair share of support, which he regretted to say was not neat the present time. To remedy this could only he by making it a national institution, setting aside all class prejudice, permitting legality of payment for play where desired. Then the game under the auspices of one controlling body promoting a cup competition and League on rational lines, would uadoubtedly meet with greater patronage. (Applause). The MAYOR said that although he was not advanced in the knowledge of the game of football, he thought he knew enough to know that its good qualities tended t' make good Englishmen and helped forward the welfare of the country. This wan his lira visit to a footnall match for 30 years, hut he hoped to have more opportunities of visiting the floe ground of the War- riogtun Club. (Applause). ST. HELENS' FINE RALLY.</p>
        <p>At B snow, before 5,00 spectato in. St !felons faced the nun and light breeza, and !Lamm early took up a strung pasiti sn. Following • penalty against Flumagsn. Hogg smartly snored • try. Turtill stayed off several irtrolg rushss, and Barrow penned the Si. Helens men on Vie 1i,., Tre!oar and 13,0wn each being held a yard from the line. Dickinson put Barrow further :mead aftsr a li se piece of work by Treloar and Purdh ins, but 0 if ,rd's kick at god failed. BAB-time : Barrow 2 tries i poiots), St. Helens nil. la the second half Turtill savad in the first minute Iron Brown, tiut the game gradually went in favour or the South Lascishire men. Following a penalty goal by Tuna', Flannasati, lidshaw, and Scholes troubled Gifford, and the former went in with a grand burst after several r ?pulses, and Turtill placed a goal. In the closing steps Prescott WSJ the means of the Barrow defence being mzel, but he got past twice end registered two line tries, Burton adding the goal points , to the second effort. Result :—St. Helens 3 goals OS points), Barrow 2 tries (0 points). Teams and othcials : Barrow—li Gifford, back; S Treloar, ())) Dobson, A Ho 4g, and A Brown, three-quarter becks; W Dickinson, an! W Purdhato, half kicks; 3 Atterbury, W Yoolden,</p>
        <p>NI Mason, A Logan, W Blacklock, and .1 Hughes, forwards.</p>
        <p>8L Helens—TurtilL hack ; Flammgan, Belshaw, tfolyneux, sod Barton, three-quarter backs ; &amp;boles and Trenwitb, half bucks; Briers, Pope, Lee, Prescott Ilutterdeld, and Mau itty, forwards. Referee, Mr F Ream (Hunslet), Touch judges Messrs W Hall (Holme) and J R Robinson (Clverster),</p>
        <p>h. I . ' ! --1 r 7---- ---111 _ 1 f I '- - , r , ow .1-.• " _woo_ "I WARP-4 ~."-11r-lio 11 riiiiililmJ .1 W - 1111, i - - -- . 4 ' - i lit ~.. _.... ....' . y... 11 , ..10„. ,i . : ~ 't . v; ~.',, . , ' '4 - '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>KEW FOOTBALL STIND.</head>
        <p>KEW FOOTBALL STIND.</p>
        <p>Opening Ceremony.</p>
        <p>The commodious stand which forms such cn important feature of the recent alterations t the Warrington Football Club's ground woe ape red on S tturday prior to the commencement of the season's campaign by Mr. I'. Locker, the president of the club. The stand which is sduated on the reserved side of the ground fulfils. long felt want. It is a fine piece of warernanship finding accommods- ion for 1,000 people and being on a par wits many of ths stands to b, seen on thegroundv of the Firet Division Am 'elation Clubs. After unloceing the door at the bock of the stand Mr. hacker led the au), and the company took their seats for the speech-making. Among those present were :—The Mayor and Mayoress (Councillor Ur. and Miss Joseph), Aldarmen Tinnier) (the es-president of the club), Smethurst, Bolton, Plerpoint, Burton, Councillors Steel and Locker, Mr. Darold Smi h, and Mr. Harry I Ashtoo (sacretar)). A letter of apology for non- attendance was reoeivel from Mr.Lelco, tree architect. I _</p>
        <p>Mr. LOCKER said that as the President' Of that club, it was his exceedingly pleasant and very important duty to declare that stand open. That great improve- ment, fiadieg accommodation for so many additional spectators, Was the outcorue of thirty yews' growth. Prom very modest beginnings and through many great changes H.°, bad progreesed, until now they bad a ground and an accommodation which would challenge comparison with any other clun in the Northern Union. He thought that with their thoroughly up-to-date and I really splendi I enclosure they ought to legitimately I expect to be favoured by the Northern Union with one lor more importent matches during the season. What- ever the future had in store for that club and for Northern Union football generally, one could not look !forward without a little aovious foreboding whether ' the rate of progress w ould be maintained or not, although there could be no doubt about the past. They vere that day where they were because of the un9 iggieg t new, splendid loyalty and true sportsmanship of the officials, cammittees and players in the post, whose labours had perhaps not, been sofiitiontly tee guts and yet the monument of whose works gas so plaitly visible to them that afteruoon. They had been cu nteut to be lorgotter, while their worts had praised them. (hear, bear.) Ile thought he ouabt to take that opportunity of saying they ass football club looked upon their function as something wider and more important than that of providing first- class football from week to wiek. They had a social se vita to di:charge. Ihe money for the purchase of trod and its fixtures and buildings hid come of course from the polio to whom they recognised ra spousioility. Easy ovenoed those premises and grounds of theirs to be be elcial to the town generally. They had provided splendid accommodation which would be saheb's, for all kin s of out-of-Joor spoits and they wanted that secioinimatation utilisad so that t ha, tiro:bid would become the bead and centre of oil that made for true manhood and god sportsmanship both in the ton-n and oist:icto (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WIDNES WIN AT SWINTON:</head>
        <p>WIDNES WIN AT SWINTON:</p>
        <p>At Swinton, before 3,000 spectators. The borne side did not include .Vick Bailey, D. B. Davies, or Fairhurst. Widnes again rely on local talent, and George Aspey led his men t 3 a well-earned victory. In the first half the home side had most of the plio,but several chances were missed, and the only reward came through Grifilthsdropping a goal. On crossing over Widnes penned the " Lions" in (heir own quarters, and Lally by dropping a goal placed the sides on level terms, but the visitors went ahead when Reid ran over, for Jones to place a goal, while a little latter O'Gara registered an unimproved try. Widnes thoroughly deserved the honours. Dal Davies led the "Lions " ably,but was poarly sant:warted, both wing men being often it fault. Result : Widnes goals 2 tries (10 paints), Swinton 1 goal (2 points,. Teams and oiheials</p>
        <p>S %into o.— riffit he, back ; inward, Williams Wharton, and Bamford, threequarter backs ; Di Davies and Parker, half backs ; Gartrell, McVeigh, Preston, Pollitt, Priv., and Randall, forwards. Widnes.—Jones, beck ; Laws, Reid, Taylor, and Swinton, threequarter backs ; Lally and OLiar.o, half backs ; G Aspey, S %spay, Rigby, Lloyd, Johnson, and McCarthy, forwards. Referee, hir J C Lumley (Leeds.) Touch judges, Mews A Johnson (Rochdale Horn:eta), and J McGowan :Broughton Rangers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>RENoORN'S EASY WIN.</head>
        <p>RENoORN'S EASY WIN.</p>
        <p>Runcorn opened the season in grand :Pyle, obtaining a splendid victory over Coventry, who were weakly represented. The visitors were the first to score, as a result of a blunder on the part of Okell, who when tackled made a feeble pass back to hls own goal line, Deakin failing to kirk clear, Beaver running in and scoring. Davie added the extra points Ransorn repliel with a splendid passing bout, which carried the ball down to the •isitors line, giving Richardson an opp ar- tunity to score at tha corner, no goal resulting. Further tries were added by Woods sad Padbury, Lewis adding one goal. The opetaug of the second half arm e7en, but Coventry were gradnally forced tack. Woods scored early on after tricky work by Lewis, and the latter player added the goal. Good passing by Watton Stevenson and Deakin enabled the latter to get over at the corner with an unconverted try, and Stevenson Inter added a further try. Result :—Runcorn 2 goals 6 tries (22 points), C went ry 2 goals 1 try (7 points). Teams and officials : _</p>
        <p>Runcorn—G Shaw, back; T Wood', P Okell, J E Stevenson, and T Ci Deakin, three-gnarter backs; J W Lewis and S Walton, half backs; A Peacock, J Williams, A Itichtrdson, S Walker, and It Padbury, forwards. _</p>
        <p>Coventry—Mosedalle, back; II aughton, T Oldham, Burdett, and Da•ies, thsewluarter backs ; Elton and Brnyon, half haelo ; Fag', Harris, Coaper, Caesar, Homes, and J P Oldham, forwards. . .</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr W tC Ihirei, Dewsbury, Touch judges, Messrs J Jones, Wigan and A Jones, Warrington.</p>
        <p>The League matches for to-day are : Batley v Swinton Bradford v Dewsbury Broughton Rangers v Salford Coventry v Barrow Hun.let v Ebbw Vale Huddersfield v Leeds Widnes v Hull Kingston Rovers Keighley v York Leigh v Oldham Rochdale Hornets • Runcorn St Helens v Warrington Wakefield Trinity v Wigan</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</p>
        <p>" A " V. WARRINGTON "L"—Warrington "A " opened the season with a fine win at Leigh by 8 points tot. Although the snore does not point to a walk-over," the result is all the more creditable when it is taken into account that the visitors were handi- capped by only being able to play twelve men, the centre threequarter Davies failing to put in an appear- ance, which necessitated the transfer of le Boardman to the wing. A large crowd assembled ant a fast game was witnessed. On the Warrington side Cldre and Lloyd scored tries and one of these win converted by Jordan. Ike Taylor played a dashing game while Critchley got in soma useful runs. Both tries were the result of excellent combination on the part of the backs. Mclntyre was sound at back his hog Lidding greatly troubling the home custodian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MAYOR AND CORPORATION AT HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>MAYOR AND CORPORATION AT HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday moruigg the llayor (Co -ncillor Df Joseph) and members of the Corporation attended service at Holy Trinity Church. A procession was formed at the Town Hall and left for church at ten o'clock, headed by the hand and a detachment of the ith Battalion Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) under- the command et Lieutenant-Colonel J D Fairclough. The Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade paraded in charge of Captain Waring, chief superintendeat,and there were also detachments of polioe, under the direc- tion of the Chief Constable, and the Fire Brigade, led by Superintendent Turner. Preceded by the civic regalia, the Mayor was accompanied by Mr. Harold Smith, his chaplain (the Rev. E. C. E. Csrleton), the Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) and Mr T H Spinks (assistant town clerk). Among others present were Aldermen Smethurst, Bennett, It Irton aol Pierpoint, Councillors Arabs-, Be Ban, Drckenton, Hawthorn, Rogere, Wilson and Wilkinson, Messrs It T Falleclough, W Furnems, J W Hayward, C S Burgers. 8 Wright, C D Parkinson, J H J Hampson (secretary of the Infirmary), Mr Thomas Longdin (borough surveyor), Mr J Moore Murray (director of education) Mr Ci F F White (magistrates' clerk), Mr F Ci litiddock (public analyst), Mr F V L Mathias (engineer, electricity and tramways department), Mr R Young , (cemetery registrar), Mr A 1.1 her (deputy borough surveyor), and Mr James Gray (water engineer). I The procession was met at the church by the church- wardens, Men-rs W Gill and J Hesketh. An impressive , service was conducted by the Vicar, the Rev E C E Carleton (chaplain). The anthem" S vest is Thy mercy" (Barnby) was expressively rendered by the , choir, the soloist being Mr A V Hodgson. The musical arrangements were carried out by the choir- master, Mr J A Westmott, end Mr Randall presided at the organ. The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON preached on Nehernish's work for his country, which be described an t• a work of reformation, of reconstruction and or government." His life was a rare instance of faitafnl public service tsited with the service of God. Nehemiah was a religious man and he was also a patriotic man, and he was a loyal man—and as such a typical sample of the great heroes of Hebrew history in the Old Testament He was one of those who realized how the welfare of the nation was dependent upon the moral anl religions character or the people ; and in time of the netion's calamity his first thought was to look to the condition of the people, and to find the source of the evil alas too often in their follies, thtir lack- sliiings, and their sine. As regarded the troubles of oar own time—and they were not a few, nor wete they light—we must, he feared, look to the same source of I their origin ; and recognizing this we might learn from Nehemiah that the downward course to which a nation might be hurrying if left to itself, it was often in the power of the temporal ruler, with God's help to arrest. The presence of the Mayor as the chief magistrate of . . . . . .</p>
        <p>the two, with those associated with I to the • h 6' ca • • -</p>
        <p>government of the borough, was a recognition of fact that the duties be discharged had a religious espcot, which that good old custom of assembling from time to time in the Houle of God for worship mil calculated to foster and perretuste amongst us—end should teach es to he ever looking. And the building In which they were assembled that morning was not altogether without its claim upon the interest and even the affection of the people the Mayor representsd. They had happily amongst them thoso whose delight It wasto cherish and preserve the memorials of the past in their midst, and amongst gush memorials their church must for many reasons have a conspicuous place. There were nacos aseociated with the church that they would not have forgot ton, beginning with the founder, Peter Legh, of Lyme, the Bleckburnes, the Pettiest., the Greenalls,the Lyons and the Watkins and others, till we come to the benefactors of our time, and who, in liberalityhad not been behind any of their predecessors. Their memories were bound up with that sacred build- ing. As they were aware, the defects of the building had been somewhat exposed to •ir w by the admirable improvements which were being made at Market Gate, and he felt that the Corporation's menifeetstion of goodwill in corning there that day in connection with the work the members of the church had in hand, eats • matter of the utmost satisfaction.</p>
        <p>On the return of the prooessioa to the Town Hell, the Moyer presented Private Catterall, the oldest private in the 4th battalion, with the King's Coronation medal. The Mayor sold he happened to hr the f ortir are possessor of one of the medals himself, and they were partners in good fortune. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
