        <p>Rey. J. R. Jones and Contented Germans.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. R. Jones writes in the" St. Mary's (Great Sankey) Parish Maga,ine " r—Since our return from our holiday trip several have asked haw we" got on over there." " What went ye out for to see ? " Well. some went out to see "Tariff Reform," others went to see Free Trade." We did neither, bat went out for a holiday, and everywhere we went, we saw a happy and contented people, well clothed, and prasurnablv well fed. But horre-ffesh and blaok bread. No ! N ! No ! And when we came home we were indeed gild to fled that Mr. Collier and his family bat seeat a very happy holiday in Sankey Pariah. That in all.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. A. Lester's Departure. Stockton Heath " Parish Monthly " says :—Nfr. Lester will be leaving the pole: during the weak commencing September 24th, and will commence his new doties on September 29th. He will go with the best wishes or all who know him. We all know what good work he his done here. Ow loss will be Lindon'• pin. We hope that though ha Is leaving us la go be London he will many times find his svay to Stockton Heath. He and his wife and family may be sore of always having swarm welcome whenever they Come t Stockton Heath. We have not yet been fortunate enough to fled a SUCCPSS tr to him. lie will he. hard man to follow. The Vicar, with the aid a the church- wardens, is doing all he can to Colon,.</p>
        <p>RELIGIOUS &amp;SOCIAL</p>
        <p>would not only greatly affect the neighbourhood for good but also the teen. (Applause.) Mr. W. HISA Lin' (hop secretary at the Wesley Guild), said that they had a guild which was worthy Of the name of a guild as their• membership wits 132. They had the largest number in the circuit and the circuit sere top of the Liverpool district 44 here there were 110 guilds with. membership of eight thousand Most of the ruemberi were Siinday school workers and seven- eighths of them were members of the church. H. could promise the new pestor very loyal assistance. Dr. Fenn rsox hoped that Mr. Beckett's ministry would be very successful and this could be d ,ne if they would all recoinize their indisidosi responsihilitiee and press upon them the need of regolar attendanm at worship. Mr. Join: d'esov raid tiom were elements of sadness about all recognition meetings. They sated not belp thinking bout those who hid gone, and so for him and ter all of them they could n .t meet together on those omissions without some faelings of revet. A Net Lad said</p>
        <p>Thereino no union tare of hearts Dirt nods not here an end. " The Nfethodist Confereace mid " there is no union here of hearts Put after three years must end." But besides diesdvantages there were els, advauiagea whish afforded eppart mitt., of making a fresh start. It was an easier matter t • start at the besioniog of anew ministry the] in the middle, Same of them laid neither begun at the ',Tinning nor ii the middle. As one who had ample opportuoity of observing thing, be could say that during the last three years the members of the church had greatly grown spiritually. Since fair. Keeling's departure he had received a letter in which he said he Iliad never left a church in all his ministry where he had gone away with so few bitter memories. He said that in the Sunday school teviibeis' meetine• leaders' meetings and church meetings there had bees: forbearance, consideration, and evidence of Is. Christian spirit. Those were signs of spiritual growth and they wanted to see still further growth. They were proud of the work that had been done ei 13swesay road. People spoke shout Harold Beghie's book " Broken Earthenware," and they rightly did so, but they could parallel such cases from their own church. They intended to go on working for the salvation of souls and iocreasing of the membsrehip of the chtir.h and they would not be satisfied until they saw that building crowded and began to consider plans for its extension. (Applause,) Them never was a time when the Gospel was no mtrob needed or the call foe service so loud as it was to-jay. 114 codd assure Mr Be3kett that if he would lead, they would all be willing to follow. Wnateser he did in the cause of his Lord ,nd NI nr he would receive their prayerful support. (Applause.) Ines Key. la. A. Brea err said one thing waich made him exceedingly glad wee that hi hal come into the midst of a live church. Ile had never been to a church in his ministry, extending over 3) years, where the ideal had bsen placed no high and where the evangelical note bad been no clearly saunded. That was a great. joy to him for whatever others believed loon did not, for himself, pars many, he was bound to keep to the front the evangelical note. After all, the main thing for them t 3 do as Christians, was the keeping to the fore the rote sion of the Kingdom of id. In bhp— cha&amp;oa he quoted the tent.' I am among you as ono that 'wrath you " and said that applied t his Owe CMS and he promised them the assistance of his family throughout his ministry there. (app!aose).</p>
        <p>Rev. Hugh Williamson on " Excuses.'' Rev If Hugh WILLIAM-ON B.A. conducted the servize at 80ld-street Wesleyan Church cm mummy evening. He took as his suhj--.1." flxett•es 'cod ea text was as follows :--'• And they all began ta make excuse " bike xiv., Is. After remsrkine thftr :Tenon wart a man who never made excuses the preacher then passed on to analyse the nature of excuser. There was tome- t ifuy, he said, that they felt it was right and fitting tl• it they should do but they did not want to do it and toes they applied to their intellect to furoish them with reasons for not doing it, which reas ma were excuses, thus an excuse was very like an attempt to square co: science by one's terson—a very vicious procedure. But it wes not only on the soots of dishonesty that excuses were to be found fault with. An excuse maker , was also a gelf-deceiver—in two ways ; first of all, be I thought he hod got reel reasons for getting co it cf his duty when he hod not, for a man's intellect wts never trustworthy she. noising under the inspiration of self-interest. And In the second place, having got his excuse he omitted to do his duty, felt no pang of conscience for dJing and vre_it on his was thinleng what a good felkw ha was. Tlienfore, it was gaol fur them to 1;-Lve done with excuses and if they did not want to On. their duty let them say," I do not want to do it, therc- foraY am not going to do it," and ao be quite straight- forward and honest. In conclusion, Mr. Williamson appealed to the congrerat ion to cultivate the haLit of downright honesty and have 1,::o with all preten7e,</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church Anniversary. How Prayer is Answered. The thirty-eighth anniversary of Wrcll:Te Congregaw tional Church was etsiebmtei on Sunday", when Mr. Gerard N. Fard. J.P., of Mtnchester (chairman of the Congregational Union of England and Wales), woa the preacher at the morning old evening services. There were good c3ngregations. Special aotheme were rendered by the choir. In the evening Mr. Ford delivered an address taking as his text "When be was come into the ship, he that bad been posseseed with the devil prayed him that h 3 might be with him. Howbeit Jesus autnred him not, but said unto him : Go home to thy friends, and tell them how great nings the I.3rd bath dons for thee. and bath had compassion on thee." St. Mark ye], and He said the request of the man posatssed with the devil was n Aural and p oper, pramptei with gratitude which was the element of true prayer. Toe refusal of .lesue .inly showed that Ifs knew the man's ptsiti )3 Letter th to he did himself. Here:tog deed than an ono—opted soul was the unhappy ground of sin and Setter. lie had* him go home end work, knowing that it would he as absolute safeguard that whilst thus engaged no evil epirit could come back again. They caaght a side- light of the wty Gad dealt with their progress. 'lf Christ were to answer our prtyers rs they were expressed it would be the most dangerous weapon they could possibly have. They so oft.° made miertakes sad asked for things they would ha better without. Happily for them, He translated their prayers mad Eno their wants No mistake would be outdo if they, like the mu mentioned in the text, asked for what was right and proper. When the answer came it would be the very one they deserved. There were no circumetsoces is life which were no I:Ml.:tilt as to be an px,),54, for par not serving God. There were many people aho found it dirticult to environment ; but they could rest assured if tl.ey were faithful to God, those untoward conditions would help to build them into a more robust type of spiritual lire. Jesse recognised the claim of home and friendship es being the first service of his following. It was a simple saving. Yet low often was it neglected in this context ion. If they were unthle to wive God by preaching and teaching the 0, spel, it was the d sty to reek out a pintos over whorn they hid the g ."eatest and give a word of enzottragement and alvice. Such work if taken up would bring &amp;front a marveilirea change is this Englaad of ours. Another lesson from this incident was that although the work began at home it did not end there. If they were to commence work at home it did not mean that they most shut then:selves up with their message. Ever wideoitle doors of ns)fulness would be opened out to theft:. They would not he , Idged by the arnoont of service they pot in or the liienoira of their efforts but by the use cf their opportimitiee. They would not be menvored by what thewhad done, bat by the spirit which they pat into it and by the use of their opportunities. That te:oft the case T.tithful in little wag faithful in much? If wider opportunities came it was their duty to make use of them and not to think their work wet rettricted to home inluence. gni when the work was extended to larger circles might they do their part in helping on the I time when Jesus shall reign, even unto the ends of the earth.</p>
        <p>At too rnoroiog service the choir nytepsthetlcally rendered the sothein " I woold tattier be a ,foer keeper and in the evening they rendered Be glad and ra,oicea.' the soloists' bang Wise Elsie Hewitt. Mesers. B. Hibbert, acid H. Hawthorn. Mr. W. H. Payton Masa Bac, presided at the organ.</p>
