      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>New Wesleyan Ministers. Last Sunday's Services.</head>
        <p>The New Wesleyan Ministers. Last Sunday's Services.</p>
        <p>The Nev Wesleyan n-iin's,ers—tlie Rev.. W. A Beckett; E. Morgan, LI. Elugh Williamson, If. MhUc wood, and W. Niaholson—commeneed their ministri in Warrington on Sunday last and tleir advent WAS responsible in great measure for large congregitio-s Rey. W Nicholson on the Strenuous Lite.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Nicholson preached on Sunday morning in the Stockton Heath church, his text being "Be still and know that. I ant God." It would, the preacher said, be difficult to find any leis n more urgently needed to be learnt at the present day than this one. We lived in strenuous timer, and the 20th century seemed synonymous with hurry, whirl end bustle. Almost everything we did was done in a hurry. We hurried over our men's, rushed for our trams a ashel into our business, and we were kept in that state of high pressure throughout the long hours of the day, so that 13• PO when we got opportunities for recreation the feverish spirit was with us, the craving for excitement was upon us, and we ssught to satisfy suck craving by feeding it with even greater excitementm Oar leisure moments were more often tablet ahoat than realize' and our time far medita;iou and rel action hid become more and more circumscribed, until in some eases it had disappeared altagether. When at length we retired to rest with active br.ins and messy bodies we found that it touk a very considerable time for nature to adjust her balance ani to rsstore the whirling, panting frame into something like cals.. The worst feature of this centinued hubbub of activity was tbyt it tended to that God out of our lives. We could not go through life strong end fresh on coast nt express trains with snatches of food here end there, but w must La e quiet hours, secret places and moments of commission with the Most Iligh—these titres of waiting upon the Lord wherein we renewed our strength and leaint to mount up on wings as eagles. Then we cool' come back again to run and not be wears', to walk and not faint. The soul that Shut itself in and beld its peace chile the ',saril was near had won a greet vict sty ; God drew near and the sonl felt secure in Ills divine Presence and let out its thoughts, hopes and fe•rs into Ike listening ear of Jehovah and revealed it, innermost secrets before His Ii idly eye.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Rev. W. A. Beckett and Conversion.</head>
        <p>Rev. W. A. Beckett and Conversion.</p>
        <p>The Superintendent minister , the Rev. W. A. Beckett) conducted the services at Bold-street and Hawses- ad, morning and evening respectively. There were goo congregationa at each service. In the even- ing he too as his text Paul's declaration ' f was not disolor Rent •nto the heavenly slabs." He said that thy conversion of Saul of Tarsus was one of the greatest triumphs of Christianity. To-day men were attempting to resolve Paul's conversion into a faint in; fit brought about by abnormal heat, or a bad attack of hysteria and thus endeavour to explain away all the sapernatural elements that were to be found in the canyersicn. Surely it was not a case that could be satisfactorily explained in that way. To Pt n it avant • wonderful tranformation—a transformation from the prxcution of Jesus Christ's followers to the service of Chris: Himself. II tt a hatever might he the explara i n cf his c,n;ersion, the fact was andeni able and, aftrr all, that w a the thing thy h.". 'o dee with. It was the reality not .he me,hos of ilst cot version that was the ironortarat thing. Prig to his conversion St. Paul was a Pharisee of the Pharisees and wood not tolerate anything like unorthodoxy certainly he could not tolerate the new religion of Jesus Christ. But after the vision, which be had on thy road to DSIII3.I3U•' what a changed man be was—" He wns not disobedient unto the heavet ly vitinn! Throughout all the ages since Paul's conversion there had always been conversions but they did not all corns in the same wanner. The value of conversion depended not on how it happened, but upon what its effects were and if finally they bad a changed life to show there was no room for further argument—it justified itself. ' ' . .. . ... .</p>
        <p>rte he e' "C ammtniion was held and a large number remeinel.</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Ailddlewood and a Nation's Wealth. Preaching at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday morning the Rev. H. Aliddlewood said that the doom of old Babylon was the doom of any nation thit forgot God. It had ever besn no. History was crowded with illustrations Rome male indulgence its laid, Venice infidelity and pleasure, and they had both gone down. We were thankful for the extent to whinh the constables of the gospel of haus Cmist were affecting the life of our time. Thu wind had no pines to-day for some of the lusts that were common, but Was it not true that some features of those times were reproduced to-day —greed of gold, the tyranny of se tlth, the lust of con- quest? Toe world yet needed to lea n that a nation wealth my not in the abundance of its p issessioss, hut is the character of its lenders and tie strong vi ;grouts manhood and womanhood of i n people. It was the recognition of the individual soul that gave slavery its death Ins and when it was fully recognised that mut was the child of God—male in tae Divine likeness—it would mean that the poor would not be herded in slums, where virtue and morality were almust impossible ; it would mean that the sweating system, which drove psople to an untimely grave, would be abolished; it would mean that the homes of the twin devils of intemperanca at d gambling would be done away with. That would be when it was recognised that man was on ire precious than gold. It would mean, he said in conclusion, that thuussnds of pounds would not be spent upon one banquet, when there were thousands starving in the slums That time WKS not yet, bat it was coming. Before that day could dawn there was much work to do and let them not fail to do their psrt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Recognition Meeting at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church</head>
        <p>Recognition Meeting at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the members of the Bewsey-roul Wesleyan Church gave a cordial welcome to their new pastor (Rev. W. A. Beckett, and its family. Tea w pmtaken of in the Lecture ball by nearly • hundred persons end afterwards the recognition service was held in the church. Mr. E. J.Gerard presided over • good con- gregation and supporting him on the dais were the 1t.,. W. A. Beckett, Mr. It Corbett and Mr. W. Healey. During the service Miss M. Jones gave a delightfol rendering of "Babylon" and Mr. Arthur Pearce gave a skilful interpretation of " Eternal Love." The Cli AIRM us said that they were there to estead a warm welcome to their new pastor and his firmly and also to renew acquaintance with old friends. 'They had got to another milestone in their c inter and he hoped their efforts in the new Methodist veer would he mofe successful even titan in the past. What they wanted was to get the spirit of the Master and if they were united they would do • grett deal of good. (Applause., Mr. R. CORBETT (speaking oo behalf of the Sundae school) said that their past experience tad shown that not only the minis:ers but the members of their families had been a great asset to the life and work of the church. As Sunday school workers they could not forget the efforts of Miss Watkins, Miss Pitt, and Miss Keeling. I Applause.) Their work and influence had been very great and were still felt to-day. Ile hoped that in the coutiog three years Bewsey-road Church</p>
        <p>- CASH PRICE 396</p>
      </div>
