      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>HAIRDRESSER'S Rosiness for Sale ; canoe; no reasonable offer raft/med.-397, EccLas New Wesate, Manchester.</p>
        <p>It was Sir ROBERT ANOTHER PEEL who invented SACRIFICE "Conservatism " as a REQUIRED. description. That is</p>
        <p>probably why Mr. DISRAELI made one of his characters speak of Conservatism as an imposture. Somebody should now come forward with a new designa- tion for the anti-Liberals. Never was there so fine an opportunity. The occasion, in fact, calls loudly for the man of genius in nomenclature. What is wanted is a name that would stand for a continually shifting set of principles. For the Conservative Party was never in such a state of flux as now, not even at the time when it had nothing to adhere to but the cry of "The Young Queen and the Old Constitution,:' The Leaders seem to have lost all power of leading, and, indeed, all inclination for it; and, meantime,every self-important member seems to consider himself qualified to declare the doctrine of the true Church. The doctrine differs in each instance; but the practice recommended is much the same. It is the throwing over of each of the Tory dogmas in turn, and the adoption, to the extent of lip-service, at least, of such Liberal proposals as will continue to look most like themselves through a Conservative disguise.</p>
        <p>During the present week one aspirant to the leadership has suggested tha.. his party, having permitted the passing of the Parlia- ment Bill, is no longer responsible for the defence of the hereditary principle or of the Church Establishment. His ambition must be like that of the fat boy in "Pickwick he is desirous of making somebody's flesh creep. "What, then," Conservatives of the older school will ask themselves, "is there left for us to defend ? " and truly there would be nothing at all. What would be left of the old Toryism would be the hereditary instinct for getting into place. Everything, as it seems, is to be thrown overboard to lighten the ship to an extent which will enable it to get into a much silted up harbour. We are aware of course, that we must not mistake for Conservative policy everything that any Conservative writes to the "Times." We mate every allowance for eccentricity, exasperation of feeling, craving for public notice; but, after doing this and even more, we are conscious of the really too-obvious fact that the Conservative Party is painfully aware of the worn -out nature of its policy of the unsuitability of such a policy to exist- ing conditions, and of the necessit y of stealing something from its opponents if it is ever to beat them at the polls. This is a distressing condition of mind. It should awaken pity rather than derision. It is, indeed, pitiable beyond measure that a formerly great party should not only have lost its faith, but have substituted for it the simple but somewhat nauseous desire to wilt back to office on any sort of profession that may secm to be suited to the moment.</p>
        <p>We do not, of course, presume to advise our political opponents on a matter so exclusively their own as their policy. If we did we should recommend them, as they are jettie- Boning so much that has been of importance to them, to throw over Tariff Reform. It brings them nothing but the luck of the ill- starred prophet. We will not, as we might, go back to the days when Mr. CHAMBERLAIN was predicting the disappearance of English trade and the ruin of England. It is only necessary to turn to a summary of the latest Blue Book to see how ill-timed were those gloomy and excited forecastings. Our trade has never been so prosperous as it is at the present time. Such huge figures in exports and imports were never dreamt of. The power of divination has seldom been more conspicuous by its absence than in the case of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. But let that pass. The Tariff Reformers have for some time past been devoting much of their effort to make it appear that the recent increase in some kinds of food is a consequence of Free Trade. If they now look abroad they will find that violent food riots are taking place in Protectionist countries. How do they explain that?</p>
        <p>An innovation at the GOVERNMENT Trades Union Congress DEPARTMENT was the representation AND LABOUR. of the Horne Office. It was at the invitation of the Parliamentary Committee of the Trade Union Congress that the Home Office decided it would be advisable to be repre- sented. As the Department responsible for the inspection and supervision of factories and mines, the Home Office is even more closely associated with the interests of labour than the Board of trade. As one of the delegates pointed out it has for years been the policy of the Government to bring the Governmental departments into contact with labour throughthe method o f representation at gatherings of delegates of the workmen. But there was a serious , gap in this method while the Home Office remained without representation at the Congress.</p>
      </div>
