        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SA ITRDAY, SEPTEMBER 9,1911</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>The Member for War- THE CHAMPION rington will SOOTI CHALLENGER, possess an acknow- ledged supremacy in one respect—that of challenger. He is getting bold even to the point of recklessness in the way he throws out challenges to all and sundry to "debate the point" when in public. There is method in Mr. Smith's seeming madness, however. He states a case, chooses his ground, and then challenges the world to dispute with him. So far the world has remained unmoved, and the Member for Warrington's position is akin to that of the .small terrier yapping defiance at the moon. On Saturday evening Mr. Smith talked loudly of the "great principles of English charity," and pointed to" the wonderful picnic" as a concrete example. Poor Charity! She is made the cloak to some most nefarious -designs. 'lf the Radical party continued to denounce the picnic," added Mr. Smith, "he would cheerfully face them on any platform in Warrington." Fancy, a debate on the ethics of a Tariff Reform picnic! Only a Smith could rise to such sublime heights. If Lady Greenall and her helpers were actuated solely by" the great principles of English charity" why did they carefully label all those 8,000 children with Tariff Reform labels? The answer to this question will give Mr. Smith all the scope or debate which he requires. As he doubtless felt sure that no Liberal leader would be inclined to spend an evening discussing so profitless a theme with him, Mr. Smith proceeded to throw out another challenge. It is one that is getting some- what stale, and one moreover that he has run away from once already. We refer to his pretty conundrum on wire: '• If the Corporatit n wanted to buy some thousands of rounds worth of wire what would the people of Warring:on say if by their votes in the Council Chamber they said We can buy the wire from Germany a little cheaper than we can buy it here so we will get it from them' Was there any man in that audience who would say that the Counc.l Irould do riBht ? (Cries of no !) Of course not." When Mr. Smith first propounded this pleasing little problem he put the price of the 'German wire at £2,750, and that of the Warrington wire at £3,000, and still argued that Warrington was the place to buy it. An audience of patriotic and parochial Warring- tonians naturally cheered the sentiment, as -they were expected to do. Mr. Smith's new -theory of economics, however, attracted the attention of local political economists and he was taken to task by Mr. R. Pierce Williams. Instead of trying to justify his extraordinary position by logic, Mr. Smith shirked a con- test witn Mr. Williams and asked that gentlemen if his views were those of Mr. 'Crosfield. If so, Mr. Smith would prefer to discuss the problem with Mr. Crosfield. So 'far Mr. Crosfield has not betrayed any interest in his opponent's ingenious applica- tion of politics to business, and as a serious politician we cannot blame him for ignoring so preposterous a challenge. Mr. Smith's " problem " is unworthy of debate, though it may answer admirably to raise a cheap cheer in the open air. We should like to ask him (though we have little hope of an answer) if he would expect Mr. Rylands to spend £3,000 in producing wire at Warrington which he could buy for £2,750 in Germany? Such a policy in business would result in bank- ruptcy, and in municipal affairs it would bring about a rise in the rates and a fall for Mr. Harold Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Ciiiiis i7r416.i0il</head>
        <p>Ciiiiis i7r416.i0il</p>
        <p>"When I hear counsel say a thing is practically so and so, then I know that it is mot so and so," is the best known apothegm of Lord Halsburf, who celebrated his 86th birthday on Sunday last. * *</p>
        <p>* As the Mayor has already pointed out, it is one of the best testimonies to the orderli- ness of Warrington during the strike period that the A.S.R.S. have sent a letter placing on record their appreciation of the courtesy and kindness of the Chief Constable and his mien. Mr. Nicholls' firm, yet tactful handling of a delicate situation on several occasions prevented matters reaching the unruly stage. * *</p>
        <p>The Mgh praise and extra remuneration which have been lavished on the Borough Auditors, appears to be taken as a mild reflection on their predecessors in office.! Alderman Bennett, Mr. E. England, and Councillor Wilson have already voiced their claims to grateful memory, and we hope their services are not considered depreciated in any way. There will be little left for these gentlemen but mutual congratulation after this year, as the Local Government Board audit comes into operation next April. • _ 0</p>
        <p>- • - Before the main battle of political controversy is joined, as it shortly will be on Home Rule, the Insurance Bill, and the like, the Parliament Bill has passed with hardly "a dog barking"—in the Cromwellian phrase. An attempt of the "Pall Mall Gazette" to punish the Bishops for not rallying to the side of the " Die-Hards," and to get rid of "hereditary entanglement " including the " Die-Hards " we suppose— adds to the harmless stock of political gaiety. The triple bases of the Tory Party—Peerage, Beerage, and Bishops, has been rent to its foundations. What remains? Apparently only Beer 1</p>
        <p>• • • What is the ideal length for a sermon? (asks the "Daily Chronicle.") Most readers will probably agree with the judge who, when asked a similar question replied: "Well, twenty minutes—with a leaning to the side of mercy.' "After twenty minutes of sermon hearing," said Dr. Parker on one occasion "deep sleep falleth upon man." A permanent reminder of the need for brevity is to be found in the pulpit of the Savoy Chapel. When the chapel was restored in 1867, Queen Victoria presented it with a pulpit-glass. And the glass was timed for eighteen minutes. a • Alderman Bolton really ought to have more sympathy with thirsty mankind than he has manifested towards the bowlers of Victoria Park. Possibly it is the consumption of mineral waters that he has a prejudice against. Anyhow, he has had to withdraw his veto on the supply of liquid refreshments at the municipal bowling green, as a result of the strong protest from all sorts and conditions of Councillors at Tues- day's Council meeting. We would, however, countenance the bowlers not to be over- jubilant. The Alderman and his committee have a unique talent for obstructive tactics, and it will probably be next summer before the battle is finally won. * *</p>
        <p>The Lancashire County Cricket Club con- cluded their engagements on Saturday with a handsome victory over Hampshire, and this gives them the fourth position in the championship table. The eleven were handi- capped in the early part of the season by injuries to J. T. Tyldesley and A. Hartley, during their absence the team fared badly. Five of the seven defeats occured during the first month of the season. Since</p>
        <p>Whitsuntide the team has had a splendid run of successes, and probably if R. H. Spooner, the most accomplished batsman of to-day, and W. Brearley, the most dangerous fast bowler, had been able to play regularly with the team, Lancashire would have been the Championship county. Corbridge, where Professor Haverfield and "those about him" have made a great haul of Roman gold coins, is one of those places which seem to support William Corbett's grotesque theory that England was a much more populous country in ancient times. , Although now a mere Tyneside village, given over to market-gardens, the town remained important and prosperous long after the Romans left Corsopitum, and in</p>
        <p>1295 sent two members to Parliament. Bing John must have had some inkling of the hidden wealth just brought to light, for he made three journeys into Northumberland "hoping to find buried treasure at Corbridge." Not having a Professor Haverfield handy, he did not get enough to pay for the baggage he lost in the Wash. * *</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett has been comparing, with much travail of spirit, the contrast between 77 per cent of fiction and '9 per cent of poetry in the books issued from the Lending Library. To his mind, the figures are "typical of the prevailing levity of our national life," and bode ill for the country. We fear that he will be as isolated in his views generally, as he was at the Council meeting. The hidden " tragedy " of the figures will leave most people cold and unmoved. Nine people out of ten have a reasonable preference for light literature; it is only the few to whom the metre of verse sings like music, and they as a rule stock their book-shelves with their favourite poets. It is the fiction-lover who is the chief borrower as the figures show so plainly. Not only is he in large majority but he borrows often, and there is really no sound reason why his preference for purely recreative reading should be either deplored or criticised. Alderman Bennett, for once, is guilty of taking a narrow view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LOCAL PARS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Personal. St. Paul's Parish Magazine says :—We congratulate Mr. Arthur Wright, one of the members of the I Vicar's Young Men's Class, upon his obtaining the degree of B.A. at the Manchester University and tram ' his appointment to the position of a Form Master at Hutton Grammar School, near Preston. St. Paul's Magazine says --We congratulate Mr. ' Arthur Wright, one of the members of the Vicar's Young Men's Class, upon his obtaining the degree of ' IBA. at the Manchester University, and upon his appointment to the position of a Form Master at Hutton Grammar School, near Preston. A few Soiled BABY CARRIAGES LO be sold cheap.— Apply J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge at., Warrington.-Advt. Progressive Labour League. An open-air meeting under the ausp'ces of the above will be held on Mnorlay evening at 7.30, when the principal spsakerwillbe Mr. S Gleave, of Manchester Independent Order of Reehabites. A most interesting meeting of the Warrington District Officers and local workers took place in the Memorial Hall Wycliffe Congregational Church on Wednesday night, for the purpose of instituting a Female Branch of the order. Bro. J. E. Gee, D.C.R. supported by the district officers, instituted the tent which was named the Pioneer tent and the following officers were appointed and installed :—Sister H. Patin C.R. ; Sister H Ashworth, DR.; Sister Ethel Smith, FOR.; Sister Dora Ford, Levite ; Sister Winifred S. Evans, Guardian ; Sister E Simcock, secretary. Nine new members were initiated and the new tent will hold its meeting the second Monday in each motth. Bro. James Shaw, D.T., and Bro. P. Howard, T.S., gave a brief address on the opportunity that presented itself in Warrington for the spread of female branches of the Order in the district. The Liberal Club Manager. Members of the Liberal Club, and Liberal Associa- tion, have learned with regret that Mr. J. Randall and Mrs. Randall are about to relinquish the posts of steward and stewardess which they have held for the past 8 years. Mr. and Mrs. Randall have not only been assiduous in their attention to the requirements of the club and its members, but have madethemselves very popular. A striking testimony to Mr. Randall's thoroughness is the state of the bowling green which notwithstanding the dry weather, is in perfect condi • tion and has no eqnal in the district. Mr. Randall has obtained a similar situation at the Seedfield Gentleman's Club, Bury, which is amounted one of the smartest clubs of its kind in the county and has a bowling green and house attached. He will have the best wishes of many friends in Warrington in his new sphere of work. MOTOR CARS FOR HIRE, long or short distances.—J ' 8. GARLAND, 51, Bridge nt., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Pawnbrokers Assistants Associa- tion. - -</p>
        <p>The second ennual picnic was held on the 31st August to Llangollen. Through the kindness of the employers, shops were closed at 11 o'clock, which enabled the party of 70 to travel by the 12-35, in a reserved ca. riage, which had been placed at their disposal by the Great-Westers Railway Company, the destination being reached at 2.15. Chat s-a-banca were in waiting at the station to convey the party round Glyndyfrdwy, the drive along the banks of the I Dee and over the mountain being particularly fine. A bah was made at the Berwyn Arms Hotel, returning to Llangollen by the Holyhead rood, which was reached lat 5.43. Tea was partaken of at the Ponsonby Arms I Hotel, after which a pleasant hour was spent looking round Llangollen. The return journey was corn- ' mewed at 7.20, arriving at Warrington at 9.30, a most lenjoyable afternoon having been spent. Croat leld's Band at Belle Vie. Crosfield's band competed in the 59th September Champion Brass Band contest held at Belle Vue, I Manchester, on Monday in the presence of a huge crowd, excursions bringing visitors from all parts of I England. Originally there were 70 entrants into the competition, all but 20 being eliminated in the qualifying contest, and those who remained to com- pete on Monday represented practically the pick of the amateur banks of the country. The test piece was a selection from Tschaikowsky's I opera " Eug.ett Onegin," arranged by Lieut. ICharles Godfrey, N.C.A., who generally under- ' takes this duty. The awards were the Belle IVue Challenge Cup, valued at 50gs. and I £5O for the winner, with prizes of £3O for the second, 1120 for the third, £l5 for the fourth, S.lO for the 'fifth, and £5 for the sixth. In addition many well- known firms of musical instrument makers added to ' the value of these awards, some of the instruments being valued as high as twenty guineas and over. The judges were Lieut. Charles Godfrey, London ; Mr. Karl Kierfert, London, and Mr. Frank Owen, Manchester. Fodea's Band (Saudbach) bad won twice in succession and were hopeful of winning the cup outright, but on thia occasion they had to be content with second place. The result ass announced as follows :-1, Ilebden Bridge (conductor, Mr. W. Hall iwell, Wigan) ; 2, Foden's Motor Works, Sandbach (W. Halliwell) ; 3, Perfection Seep Works, Warring- ton (W. ; 4, Batley Old, Yorkshire (A. Gray, Manchester) ; 5, Irwell Springs, Baca') (A. Owen, Manchester) ; 6, Luton Red Cross (W. Halliwell).</p>
        <p>Drowning Mystery. On Thursday evening last the body of Thomas Burgess, ironworker, of Wes.-street, Warrington, was recovered from the St. Helens and Sankey Canal. How the deceased got hit° the water is a mystery.</p>
        <p>Distinction for Local Sculptor. Mr. James Booth, a young Congregational sculptor, a native of Warrington, and brother of the Rev. Edward Booth, of Lowther-street Congregational Church, Carlisle, has been selected by the Inter- national Bible Reading Association Committse as sculptor of the m raiment to the late Charles Waters (founder of the association) in Honor-oak Cemetery. 1 Mr. Booth's design, which is to be worked out in Sicilian marble, will represent a child standing at its mother's side looking intelligently into her face as she reads from a Bible open upon her knees ; the mother's right arm encircles the child in loving embrace, typical of the all-inclusive spirit of the movement, and she is seated on a Corinthian capital, symbolic of man's noblest and most enduring work. Mr. Booth was Landseer Scholar at the Royal Academy. Ile is himself a member of the 1.8.R.A. Focsv CHINA TEA SERVICES, 40 pieces 12/6.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge it, Warrington:—Advt.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Cable and Rubber Company's Girls' Plank. On Saturday last, 70 of the girls employed by the above compiny journeyed in char-a-banns to Frodsham, under the auspices of the Works Recreation Club. Starting from Arplev Cannons at 1.45 p.m. in ideal weather, a m-st enj yable time was spent on the road (except dust from motors). The merry party arrived at Frodsham at 3.15 p.m. and the two hours before tea were devoted to games, helter- skelter, brother ass, and guessing when Warrington Bridge will be started upon. At 5.15 the girls were in fine fettle for tea, and quite enjoyed the novelty of the male committee, Messrs. Locker, Shaw and Wa.son, serving out tea, etc. This important matter diap-Ited of, the remainder of the evening was given up to dancing (when the band did not play, the mouth organ did) rambling lad blackberry gathering. On the renurn journey, while less successful in dodging the overhanging leaves and branches of trees than earlier in the day, the Cannons were safely reached at 10 pm, everyone expressing themselves highly delighted with the outing, and a collection for the drivers, and a hearty cheer for the committee, put a pleasant finish to a happy venture. - -</p>
        <p>- - - WRINGING MACHINES, best value obtainable. C,esh price, 39/6.—J S. GARLAND, Ironmonger Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Wesleyan Cycling Club. Saturday, September fad, to Pickering Bridge, 19 miles return from Market-gate. Thin ought ta be a popular resort for cyclists, for Mrs. Ludwig, the lady 1 who caters for us at the old inn (now no longer licensed), is a daughter of Jack Darlington, the old rhampial racer of England in the G 0.0. days. Leaving the main road at the Police Station, Preston Brook, we took the left turn at Aston Hall Gates, and turned down to Aston Grange Farm. Here two roads, both indifferent, give us a choice of evils, the lesser evil going direct to the wooded bank overlooking the valley. The road (en-called) suddenly turns right and tumbles into the field at the bottom with a sommersault, and a footpath takes us to the riverside and the bridge. After tea, two of the party gave an exhibition of what one might call " trick-punting." It seemed to include sculling, rowing, punting, cutting the figure 8, and even practising with the ',miser fr next Monday's washing. As they could not hit the landing stage after fcur attempts, we who were safe and could look on in a detached manner, proposed to shift the landing stage if they would cnly stay where they were a while. After the performance, the more manageable cycles conveyed the party through Davenham and Loetock, home by a route that lengthened the run to 33 miles.</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OF CYCLES, all prices reduced,...) S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge to., Warrington.—Advt. Death of a Prominent Church Worker. We regret to announce the death of Mrs. Hignett, the wife of Mr. Josep`o Hignett, a well-known com- mercial traveller for the firm of Messrs. J. and W. Dutton, Sankey-street, which took place at her resi- dence, 24, Museum-street, on Sunday last. The deceased was a regular attender at Holy Trinity Church, and also for many years a prominent church worker in that body. The hmeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Wednesday last, a large number of relatives attending at the grave-ide to pay their last respects. Prior to the cortege departing for the Cemetery, a service was held at Holy Tiinity Church, being conducted by the Rev E C E Carleton (the Vicar), who also oltiated at the grave-ide. The following is a list of the chief mourners :—Mr Joseph Hignett (husband), Mr Percy Hignett (son), Mr. Samuel Hignett (brother-in-law), Mr Joseph Knowles (Agden), Mr William Shaw (Stockton Heath), and Mr Dunbabin (Altrincham). A large number of floral tributes were received, and included the following : Husband and family, Mrs D Jones and Nellie, Mr and Mrs John Hesketh and family, Staff at J and W Dutton, Misses A and D Mather, Mr and Mrs S Shakeshaft, Mr and Mrs Gill, Mrs Havenacroft, Mr and Mrs Knowles, Rev and Mrs E C Carleton, 5.8.8. Church, Stretton, Mr and Mrs John Taylor, Mr and Mrs Medley Taylor, Mr and Mrs Welsby, Mrs Percival and family. Minnie and Annie, Mrs Harvey Church, Stretton Mr and Mrs Randall and family, Mr James F ann Walter Dutton, Mrs Fergusson, Mr, Mrs and Miss Dnnbabin, Mrs Edward F6x, Mr and Mrs Arthur Rtchards, Mr and Mrs A E Richardson, Mr and Mrs Forshaw, Miss A Longdin, Mrs Kisby, Mr and Mrs Eddowes. The funeral arrangements were in tho hands of Messrs. J. and W. Dutton, Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Cavendish--Arbuthnott.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church, Newton-le-Willows, was the scene of a fashionable wedding on Tuesday when Miss Jean Arbuthnott, daughter of Mr. H. C. Arbuthnott (a director of the Vulcan Foundry Co.), was married to Mr. Alexander Cavendish, son of the late Mr. A. Cavendish (Madras). The church was beautifully decorated with flowers and plants and the service was fully choral. Mr. Edwin Leyland, L.L.C.M., presided lat the organ. The ceremony was performed by the I Rev. C. C. Hamilton, rector of Broom (cousin of the bride), assi=ted by the Rev. H. Grantham, rector of Chester Cathedral, and the Rev. J. Ryder. The bride, rho was given away by her father, wore a dress of white satin and old Valenciennes hand lace, the gift of her grandmother, the Hon. Mrs. Arbuthnott. Her veiLwas of Limerick lace, the gift of Mrs. Grantham, and she carried a bouquet of lilies of the valley and white orchids. The bridesmaids were the Misses S. U. Arianthnott, May and Mary Cavendish (sisters of the bridegroom), and E. S. Hutton-Brown (cousin of the bride). They wore dresses of white satin filled with embroidered ninon, and caps of gold net, and carried bouquets of pink carnations. The pages were Masters A. C. and J. F. A. Gibson (cousins of the bride), dressed in white sailor suits, and the best man was Mr. J. R. C. Cavendish (brother of the bride- groom). After the service the church bells rang out a merry peal. The reception was held at the house of Mr. Arbuthnott.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Corbett —Burthem.</head>
        <p>Corbett —Burthem.</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable local interest was solemnized on Thursday afternoon at St. Luke's Church when Miss Florence Burthenr, daughter of Mr. John Burthem (chief engineer of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd.,) was married to Sergeant-Major Corbett (of the Royal Field Artillery, Britain Ferry,) son of Mr. Thomas Corbett, manager of Orford Tannery. A large conaregation assembled in the church. The Revs. H. B. Firth and H. Wilde officiated. Mr. Reid, of Wigan, presided at the 1 organ and played appropriate selections including Mendelssohn's Wedding March. The hymn " Toe voice that breathed o'er Eden " was sung. The bride who was given away by her father, was attired in a dress of ivory satin trimmed with pearls and true lovers' knot. She also wore a court train and an embrOdered veil, surmounted by a wreath of orange blossom. Her bouquet consisted of white heather, lilies of the valley and white roses. She also wore a gold watch bracelet,the gift of the bridegroom. Miss Beatrice northern (sister of the bride) and Miss Queenie Corbett (sister of the bridegroom) acted as brides- maids. They looked very charming in a pale blue crepe-de-chine Empire dress. They carried bouquets .4,. of pale pink roses. Miss northern wore a gold racelet and Miss Corbett a gold ring, both the gifts 1 of the bridegroom. The duties of beet man were di,- charged by Mr. Thomas Corbett (brother of the bride- groom). After the ceremony the recepti at was held at the home of the bride's parents. The honeymoon is being spent in Loudon and the South. For the journey the bride wore a pale green dress and cream traveling coat with hat to match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Bradshaw—Povey.</head>
        <p>Bradshaw—Povey.</p>
        <p>Happy is the bride that the sun shines on, runs the old adage, and if that be true then the couple that were married at Daresbury Parish Church on Wednesday are assured a large share of happiness The contract- ing couple were Mr. David Bradshaw of Latchford. and Miss Bertha Povey youngest daughter of Mr. Tbotnas Povey and Mrs. Caroline Povey of Hatton The ceremony took place at Daresbury Parish Church at 2.30 p.m the officiating clergyman being the Rev. T. T. Lee Jones (vicar). The bride was very prettily attired in a dress of dark slate material trimmed with white satin, insertion and net, also bridal wreath of orange blossom and fall. She also carried • boquet of lilies, maiden hair fern, and gyhsophila. Miss Frances Povey, sister of the bride, acted as bridesmaid and was prettily attired in a dress of blue silk voile ttimmed with cream insertion and white net, and she carried a bouquet of blue and whito asters intermixed with maiden heir fern The bridegroom was attended Ey Mr. Geo. Brazendale of Lymm, in the capacity of best man, and Mr. R. Lee of Latchford, gave the bride away. The wedding breakfast was spread in the Parish Room Barton, kindly lent for the occasion, after which the guests indulged in various games, music, etc, until about ten in the evening. The couple left a little later for their new home at Orford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>McMullen--Rimmer.</head>
        <p>McMullen--Rimmer.</p>
        <p>The marriage was s lemnized at St. Benedict; Church, Orford-lane, on Thursday, of Mr Hugh , McMullen, 19, Winwick-road, son of the late Mr. Martin McMullen, and Miss Mary Gertrude Rimmer, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. James ltimmer, 17, Orford- avenue. The Rev. Father Swarbreck officiated. The bride, who was given away by her father, wore a beautiful dress of cream silk eolienne, with lace hat to match. She carried a bouquet of lilies, lilies of the the valley, and white heather, and her ornament was a gold necklet, the gift of the bridegroom. The brides- maids were the Misses Alice and Margaret Rimmer (sisters of the bride) an 1 the Misses Monica Rimmer and Teresa Miller (nieces of the bride and bridegroom respectively.) The Mis,eit Rimmer were attired in pale grey eolienne with black silk hats, and the two children wore pretty Swiss needlework dresses and hats. Their bouquets were composed of pink peas and azaleas and their ornaments were gold brooches, the gifts of the bride- groom. Mr. Lawrence McMullen (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best mai. After the ceremony a reception was held at the home of the bride. Later the happy cruple left for North Wales where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride travelled in a blue serge costume and brown hat. Many valuable and handsome presenls have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>England —Beaumont.</head>
        <p>England —Beaumont.</p>
        <p>'An interesting wedding took place at Mottram Parish Church on Saturday, when Mr. William James England, R.N.R., son of Mr. and Mrs. Ebenezer England, of Walden, Fearnhead, was married to Miss Ida Beaumont, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Beaumont, of Market-street, Mottram. The Vicar of the parish officiated. The bride was given away by her uncle who was .over on a visit from British Columbia. She was charmingly attired in a white Indian silk dress, with pearl trimming, and carried a bouquet of white lilies. The bridesmaids were Miss Ethel Beaumont (sister of the bride) and Miss Bessie P. England (sister cd the bridegroom). They wore pretty dresses of pale green rewla silk, with black picture hate. Their bouquets were composed of pink carnations and their ornaments were gold and pearl brooches (the gifts of the, bridgegroom). Mr. Harry Sherburn, of Stockton Heath, discharged the duties of beet man. After the ceremony a reception was held in the Wesleyan schoolroom at Mottram. Later the happy couple left for the honeymoon, the bride travelling in a bronze green costume with hat to match. Many choice presents were received from relatives and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Morrall—Farrar.</head>
        <p>Morrall—Farrar.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding was solemnized at Wycliffe Church on Thursday afternoon, when Miss Emily Farrar, third daughter of Mr and Mrs B Farrar, of 5, Willis-street, was married to Mr Fred Morrell, son of the late Mr George Morrell and Mrs Morrell, of White street. There was a large congregation st the ceremony which was condumed by the Rev. Harry Wall. Mr W H Payton, Mus. Bar., presided at the organ and played appropriate selections including Ifendelssohn's Wedding March. The bride, who was given away by her father, wore a dress of cream silk :alpaca with hat to match. Her bouquet consisted of Mlles and chrysanthemums. The bridesmaid was Miss Jessie Farrar (.ister of the bride), who was attired in a pale blue silk striped voile, with hat to match,and carried a bouquet of lillies and other choice flowers. Mr William Hayes mted as best man and Mr Charles Morrell as groomsman. A reception was held at the home of the bride's parents. Later in the day Mr and Mrs Morrell left for the Lake District where the honeymoon will be spent. The bride travelled in a navy blue costume. Numerous presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>REVISING THE LISTS.</head>
        <p>REVISING THE LISTS.</p>
        <p>" LATCHKEY DECISION NOT TO BE STRICTLY ENFORCED.</p>
        <p>Revising Barrister and Lodger Votes</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H. Marriott Wharton, revising barrister, opened his annual revision of the voters' lists for the borough of Wartington at the Town Hall yesterday morning. Notice had been given of eleven claims, two objections and 41 new lodger chime. The total number of voters on the register, as prepared by the overseers, is 12,910. Objection is being taken by Mr. S. Gough to the vote of Councillor Henry Roberts and the case is expected to be heard to-day. Alderman W. Pierpoint has put in a claim for a vote in respect 1 of an office in Tbelvrall-lane. Mr. Harry New appeared for the Liber as 1104 Mr. Herbert Hayes represented the Conservatives. Mr. W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer) was also in attendance</p>
        <p>• TIIE " LATCHKEY " DECISION. In opening the Court, the Revising Barrister said he understood there had been some little change in the representation of the parties since last year and that Mr. New now appeared; for cue of the parties. He hoped that everything would go on sweetly, as it had always done. They had never had very much trouble with the list. He dared say that was mainly owing to two circumstances—one that they had an excellent assistant overseer who took care to have everything settled that could be settled before they came to the Court, and the other that the political agents bad always met him very fairly end had never raised any unnecessary objections. Since they last met two matters had been raised to which he would like to refer. One was the RATHER SURPRISING rirristrix which had bees given recently on the subject of the payment of rates. He did not think it would be neces- sary to deal with that on the present occasion. It might be necessary because, of course, be could not tell what might arise in the course of claims, but, as far as anything else was concerned, he was not going to raise a point which was unnecessary. While he did not dis- pute the decision or its propriety, it bad come so late that it had surprised everybody and as the effect of the decision had been to upset all their conclusions in the past, including the conclusions which the text book writers had come to, it was advisable is everybody's Interest that as little notice should be taken of it 83 possible—that war, no more notice than was necessary until the electors had had the opportunity to comply with what they DOW knew to be the law but what they thought before was not the law. Tne other point wes with regard to</p>
        <p>LODGER CLAIMS. Hitherto the cases hod been dealt with on the basis of Lord Justice Brett's opinion Oath was not pacers try to give any notice objection at all,but that there should be no mistake be, the barrister) had had handed in to him by the assistant overseer, who was entitled by law to object to bloc, a general notice of objection. He proposed to continue to deal with the cases in thst w.ty, g sing through the list in the ordinary way, and proheoly a large somber of Cases would be allowed. If either of the registration agents thought it was necessary to deal with any case specifically, be would afford every opportunity by giving ootioe to the claimants to attend. Strictly speakiog, his view of the law was that it wes not necessary. but, in orJer that there might be no mistake, he thought it would be the best plan to adopt. The assistant overseer was able to give a lot of informa- tion, first of all because he had the rote book and the 'assessments of the property a nd in addition, acting upon his (the barrister's) suggestion, he went round to the house where there was any possibility of a genuine claim and put a series of questions to the claimant and the landlady with a view to getting full information. It name to this: No previous notice of objection need be given to the claimant, but if he was not present it wee his own misfortune if hie claim was not inserted on tho list. Mr. New, in thanking the Barrister for his remarks, said the agents wonld do their utmost to assist in the speedy condnct of the work of the Court. They were glad there were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NO CONTENTIOUS (ASS</head>
        <p>NO CONTENTIOUS (ASS</p>
        <p>as far as they were concerned, and they owed a great deal to the kindness of the assistant overseer who had afforded them every information in order that the business might proceed without undue delay. On behalf of the party he represented, he wan grateful to the Barrister for his kind sentiments, and could only assure him that he would do his best only to bring forward such claims as were Wad Jac.</p>
        <p>A CHANGE OF TENANCY. THE BARRISTER AND RENT BOOK ALTERATIONS.</p>
        <p>-- - The Conservatives claimed a vote on behalf of John Goddard, 33, Tanner's-lane. Margaret Goddard, claimant's wife, stated that her husband was the tenant of the premises. Se producsd the rent hook. .</p>
        <p>The Barrister : When was the name altered from Margaret Goddard to John Dodd ird ? Warms: I cannot soy the exact date, but it is about two years since. The Barrister : It is quite that, is it? Witness : Yes. Mr. Woodcock : Was it not changed in November, after the elections? Witness I don't think so. Mr. Woodcock : You were on the list last year. IVitneas : I think it is nearly two years Mode it was changed. The Barrister You could not hive been on the list if your husband had bean the teuant. Han did you get on the list ? Witness explained that she purchased the liaainess over three yours ago and consequently the premisis were put in her name. The B mister : How long was it after you bou/ht the basinsis flit Cie aim, wis all ire I ? Witness: I cannot tell the exact date, but it was altered about the time of the eleation whau they cans bothering about the vote. When my husband fouad his name was not in the rent book he told me to go to the landlord and get it altered. Mr. New : Didlyou buy the business with your own money or your husbands ? Witness: Both.</p>
        <p>14;7:-Ze7s. sal; had seen the ailment, who told him</p>
        <p>that he gave his wife some money to buy a business in Warrington' because be c3uld not come over. She conducted the transaction and by soma mischance her name was put in the rent book ani allowed to remain there for some ti ce. He (Mr. New) believed that the business was bought chiefly with the husband's money, and that it was previous to the January election of 1910. _ _ . . . . .</p>
        <p>The Barrister said the landlord stated that claimant had been tenaat since May 30,b, 1910. There was a certain amount of doubt about the case and, strictly speaking, he had no business to accept the landlord's statement without actually hearing him. On the whole, however, he felt justified in allowing the claim. Continuing, the Barrister said there were so many cases where alterations were made in the rent books that be was going to be very strict when evidenoe was not gives. Every year they had some decision which nada it more important that he should settle those cases acaordieg to strict legal evidence. The Revision Appe ii Courts were taking up that point very distinctly. It was not sufficient to take things for granted. There was one course he could adopt—to place those who worn canvassing on their oath—but he did not want to do that. When he commenced revising he told the agents there was a simple way of obtaining the necessary proof without doing it outside the law, and they ought to try ta get the information which was in acaordance with the law. He could not receive evideace which was not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>– -- -- RELIEF DISQUALIFICATION.</head>
        <p>- -- -- RELIEF DISQUALIFICATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. New had put in a claim for a vote on behalf of Michael Butler. The information he receive&amp; he said, was that owing to illness Batter went to the Workhouse or the Workhouse Infirmary, but only stayed there one night. He was not impressed with the surroundings and came out next morning. The whole Ci4o seemed to turn upon whether he slept in the Workhouse or in the Infirmary. Mr. Hayes: It is relief, your Hotour. Mr. New: I was under the impression that he slept in the lotimary one night and came out the next day without receiving any help from the Union. I put in a claim for the vote but I have just learned from the relieving ofiicer that the man slept in the Workhouse and not in the Infirmary. The Barrister: I cannot gem': the vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CONTRACi WANTED.</head>
        <p>CONTRACi WANTED.</p>
        <p>Having heard a number of lodger claims, the Barrister said that in cases of father and son, where the ordinary family amenities obtained, he should require evidence of a contract. He wanted to see whetter there were contracts which justified him saying the claimants had exclusive occupation of rooms. Mr. New : lam afraid there is not a contract in a great many families. A BATCH OF LODGER. CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>A lodger vote was claimed by John Frederick Loy, Cooper's Arms, Bewsey-street. Claimant stated that be was employed by his mother who was the licensee of the house. He practically managed the business and out of his salary he paid Bs., per week for food and the exclusive occupation of two rooms. The Barrister refused to grant • vote. Herbert Isaac, n, Bewsepstreet, made a similar claim. He stated that he bad the exclusive use of a bedroom and of a small room over the hall which be used as a study and sittiogroom. There was no fire place in it, The Barrister: What do you do ix the winter? Claimult : When it is very cold Igo downstairs to tbe'otber sitting room. The Barrister, in retuning the vote said the other members of the family had practically the best part of the house and he could not see how the rooms which the applicant ocoupied could be of the necessary value (£10.) Mr. New asked whether the Barrister would not attach some weight to the fact that the claimant lived at home because it was essential to his business. The Barrister : It amounts to this. You can take a box room, which is of no use except for lumber, and you can use it as a sitting-room and then he occupies a 'bedroom which he would occupy if he lived at home in the ordinary way, and that qualities hive for a .ClO vote. The Claimant: It is sets box ro3m. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. New : He bas practically the use of three rooms.' Toe Barrister : lam petty well satisfied that he has the use of the whole house. (Laughter.) This is one of those cases where I have a greet deal of doubt. If there is a distinct contract which can be produced to satisfy me, it might make a difference, but as it is I have very grave doubts, and I cannot allow the vote. Samuel A. Mackey, 12, Crosfield-street, who also claimed a lodger vote, stated that the value of the house was £2l. He admitted that the sitting room of which he was entitled to exclusive occupation, was occasionally used by other members of the family. The Barraster said he was not satisfied that claimant ' had exclusive use of the sitting room. The question was whether the first floor bedroom was worth £lO out of a total gross rental of £2l. He did not think it was and he could not allow the vote. Gordon A De, 12, Arpley-street, claimed a lodger vote. He stated that before making the claim he had sole use of a sitting room, bat since then he had allowed another lodger the privilege of using it. The Barrister : I aather like this claim, but you will be_in a mess next year. (Laughter).</p>
        <p>The vote was allowed. Graham Fulton Smith, Bold•street, who claimed a lodger vote, did not put in an appearance. It was stated that the value of the house wu £3l and there were already two votes in respect of it.</p>
        <p>The claim was refaed, AN INTERESTING POINT. Mr. New raised a ease in which be said one man had taken a house and shop for Ss. a week and had sub-let • portion upstairs for 31. During his absence the user of the upstairs portion got himself put on the register as occupier and the real tenant of the premises was thus deprived of the vote. Claimant did not put inns appearance. The B trrister How Can I help this? I cannot remove one man from the register. Mr. New: He can be transferred to division three and the tenant put on for division ones bit.l • Mr. Woodcock said the sub-tenant in law was entitle to be on division one. The premises were only assessed at £l5 103., which was not sufficient t 3 allow of two dwelling-houses in occupation. Mr. New : In my opinion the man got on the register by a trick and it seems hard lines for the tenant to be deprived of the vote. The application was refused. A PRIEST'S APPLICATION. Mr. New mentioned the claim fore vote of the Rev. Michael Dawley, assistant priest at the Church of the Sacred Heart. He said he had asked Father Dowley If he would let him have the rent book and he said he never had one. Ile took his orders from the Bishop awl was entirely separate from the Rector wso did not engagd him or give him notice. The Barrister : He must come here. lam not going to take this second-hand. Yon know that the cases within the last two years have been very explicit that the BArrister is bound to take only legal evidence. There are only very few exceptions under the Registration Act which entitle him to alter that rule. Von most not blame me for wanting all this informstion; you must blame the legal position. Priests' houses and their positions are very difsoult matters to deal with and I cannot possibly decide the ease without having evidence first-band. A Barrister cannot now exercise that wide discretion with reitrd to evidence that he could in times past and I am not going to place myself in a position, by trying to be kind all round, to he Mel at later oars the Court a Appeal. The Barrister expressed his willingness to bets the evidence of Fatter Howley or somebody, else in authority to-iay. Mr. Hayes °fleeted on the ground that the specified time for claiming had elapsed but the Barrister said he was not going to take advantage of a point like that.</p>
      </div>
