      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>TERRIBLE SHOOTING TRAGEDY. MR. F. G. TAYLOR KILLED.</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE SHOOTING TRAGEDY. MR. F. G. TAYLOR KILLED.</p>
        <p>SHOOTING PARTY ACCIDENT IN WALES.</p>
        <p>A profound sensation was crested in Warrington yesterday afternoon when the news gained currency that Mr. Frederick George Taylor, solicitor, 274, Manchester-road, had met his death under exception- ally tragic circumstances. Since a week last Wed- nesday Mr. Taylor had been spending a holiday in the Denbigh district of North Wales, along with several! friends from Wairington. Be was expected to home this week-end, but yesterday afternoon*: relatives and many friends were shocked to learn thin he had been accidentally shot whilst out with a shpoir ing party on the Cefn Moore. The circumstances of the terrible olcurrence are related in the following message from our ambi_gh correspondent BT T ELE.: R 41/adi Denbigh, "• 'lit f.llll Feintt- Frederick George Taylor, solicitor, Warrington. met his death with tragiz suddenness near Denbigh, to-day. Deceased was the guest of Mr. P. C. Garnett also of Warrington, who owns 'eyelid farms and grouse moors ia the Saroa district, about five miles from Denbigh and within easy distance of the Alssen Valley where the Birkenhead water works are DOW in progress. From a report which has reached the West Denbighshire Coroner it appears that Mr. Taylor, who was 33 years of age, went out to shoot over Cefn Main farm in company with Mr. I larnett and five other gentlemen. Threa proceeded to a turnip field in search of partridges. whilst Mr. Garnett with deceased and another gentle- man rested on a hedge bank. One carried a coat and a loaded gun. What actually transpired is to a certain extent a matter of conjecture, but the statement made to the Coroner pea to show that the gentleman in charge of the gun handed over the coat to Mr. Taylor and in doing so must inadvertently have touched the trigger. The contents of the gnu lodged in Mr. Taylors head behind the ear, killing him instantaneously. The whole affair was obviously an anident. The party at once took steps to notify the sad event to the police. The body was removed to Cefa Main , Farm where deceased was staying with Mr. Richard Bibby, a well-known yule of Clwyd farmer. The inquest will be held to-morrow and the remains are to be removed to Warrington by the 3')I) train.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>TBE FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>TBE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral will take -Pia—ce- at the Warrington Cemetery on Monday, the cortege to leave the late residence of the deceased, 274, Manchester-road, at twelve o'clock noon.</p>
        <p>Not wfr 111110, *,</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. F. G. TAYLOR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH.</head>
        <p>BIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH.</p>
        <p>The late Ur. F. G. Taylor, who was a native of Warrington, was born in 1873. He was the 'O&amp;M son of Mr. Frederick Taylor, the highly respected secretary cf the Warrington Corporation Gas Department. Having received his early education at People's College, W. Taylor entered the office of Mr. W. H. Brook, a former Town C'erk of Warrington. In 1893 Mr Brook retired and his practice was acquired by Mt. Henry Greenall to whom Mr. Taylor was subsequently aiticled. 116 was admitted a solicitor in 1898 inicontinned his ass ocia- lion with Messra. Henry Greenall and Co. up to the time of his death, discharging the duties of deputy magistrates' clerk in Warrington, Newton and the Daresbury divisions. En addition to his professional work, Mr. Taylor threw himself with great energy into the efforts of numerous local organizations which brought him into close touch with men of all shades of religiius and political opinion. But first and foremost rime his active interest in all matters relating to the Church of England, of which he was a loyal and devoted son. From boyhood he had attended the Parish Church-- in fact he was very rarely absent from the Sunday services, and the congregation had for some years past shown their appreciation of his staunch Church- manship by electing him to the office of sidesman. Work amongst the young men of the parish claimed his close attention, and as treasurer of the Parish Church Young Men's Institute,he was instrumental in placing it on a sound basis. In politics he was an ardent Conservative but his ideals were strongly tinged with democratic tendencies. He was a keen and skilful debater and took a delight in the weekly meetings of the War- rington Parliamentary Debating Society., of which he was one of the most energetic and valued members. On practically every subject which came op for debate he had something trenchant to say and he soots took his place on the Front Bench of the Conservative side. Ozcasionally the duties of leadership of the House devolved upon him. Last session he held the °film of Chancellor of the Exchequer, and his Budget was conceived on bold and original lines. Like his father, Mr. Taylor did much for the advancement of Friendly Society work in Warriogon and district. For mane years he had been connected with the Consitntion Lodge of the Independent Order of Odelfellows (Manchester Unity), and at the time of his death was Past Grand of the Ledge. Be was assistant secretary of the Children's Burial Society, which is managed by local Oddfellowa and provides insurance for members' children. Only a fortnight ago he accompanied the members of the committee on a Coronation trip to Rhyl. Mr. Taylor was an enthusiastic devotee of the camera and hail occupied the presidency of the Warrington Photographic Society. He was also very fond of ontsloorre creations, bowling being am onghis favourite games, and almost any Siturday afternoon would find him trundling on the Arpley green, for he was a Iprominent member of the Warrington Cricket anti Bowling Club. lie was also a practised bend at golf and was associated with the Warrington Golf Club. He was a very familiar figure at the Old Academy, and by hie death the Warrinigton Society loses a faithful member. Mr. Taylor was of a cheerful, urbane and sympathetic disposition, and few townemen cool i claim a wider circle of friends. He set an example of sincerity in religious and political life, and those who differed from Lim moit were the first to recognise the earoestnets with which he expounded his views. Ile wits a hard and fair hitter in debate, but did not resent criticism in retnrn, end was always the essence of courtesy to cpponents, Deep and widespread will be the regret at the untimely end of a life of so much promise, and sincere sympathy will be extended to the deceased gentlemen's father and brothers in their tragic bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
