        <p>2Ath Angrt-t. -simres. 13,1.1i1301,1 : Builders. contr.ctors, &amp;rotators. engineers. A:!., and to acquire (I) the BEl,ch.vlA and Oughtrington Estates, situate at or near Lymm, Cheshire. and (2) shares in the undertaking known as the Lymm Water Company. Signatories:— Sir Wm. H. Lever, Bart., Thornton Manor. Thorn- ton Bough, Cheshire J. L. Ferguson, Branksbolm, Egerton Park, Hock Ferry (one share each,. Private company. First directors (to number not lees than three nor more than 15) are to be appointed by the signatories.</p>
        <p>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND THE SEMI-FINAL. ALBERT °LEAVE V. R. TRIGGER. ATHLETICS. Gleave winning the tors he registered four singles before Trig ter ssored. There was a general comment round the greens ide when (Heave played a short length mark which just suited Trigger, a place where Trigeer hsd done lug mischief in the previous gamer. The opinion of the spectators were rignt to 'certain degree. Trigger drawing the end crested a great deal of excite- ment amongst the crowd by a fise exhibition of bowl- ing. lie registered five couples and fire singles, the break comprising 15, a very fine achievement. At the fifteenth end Gleave drew the end with a single, the next end went to Trigger with a brace of points, 17-5. Gleave realising his position, found pastures new and by placing Triguer itt corners gave an exhibition similar to his opponet ts duting the initial Dort of the game. Recording a break of 9it brought him within 3of Trigge 'a tots!. The game was beginning to tell its tale on Trigger who. I am sorry to sty, has been indisposed for some time pest. The game peeled at 18, and with three singles to Gleare ruade him game 21-19. If ever tht to WAS a a rprise result this wts one, for 17-5 in Trigger's favour it looked as if Gleave would not have a look in. Gleave must be complimented on palling on exciting vase out of the fire.</p>
        <p>mark</p>
        <p>ELLISON V. A. SCOTT..</p>
        <p>This semt-final game was never lathe picture corn- pared with the other. I have seen both of these players give better disp'aya, but it was a tough struggle all through peeling at 12 atd 14 when Sec t: centered sin valuable in iota. Singh a w -re the order oj the EC ire card and with three to Scott and two to Ellison the game was cried in favour ol Scott 21-16. Ellison mei constantly to get his old mark, bet failed each time. The game became too critical to chance his arm a,y more and was compelled to seek new laud. H..cl be wandered in the early stages of the game his return might have been much diffe.e it. Though Scott deserved the victory !</p>
