      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Cricket Notes.</head>
        <p>Cricket Notes.</p>
        <p>King Football in poseessicn, interest in the summer sport is now a negligible quantity. Warring- ton played their last away match t f the season on Saturday when they journeyed to Sale and t ffected a draw. The prospects were, however, in favour of the 11,me eleven who declared their innings at 188 for four wickets, the Warrington; ins responding with 100 , for six. Sale made a brilliant start, the hrst wicket yielding 53. There was some fine hitting by C. it. Hartley who defied the Warrington bowling and made a worthy addition to a remarkable season's record by carrying out his bat with 70 to his credit J. Kershaw was also in great form, his innings realising 50.</p>
        <p>-- Warrington started rather badly, a wicket having fallei with only five runs on the scare sheet. Fisher anri S. Partridge, hosever, quickly pit a rosier com- plexion on the game. Fisher played btilliantly but at 41 be was smartly caught by Woods. Partridge batted with snlendid judgment and kept his wickets intact to the close, his score reaching 43.</p>
        <p>-- Warrington Conservatives, the local league champions, experienced a shaking at the hands of Whitecross on Saturday. The wire men put together :55 and nine Conservative wickets had fallen with the total at 40. The scoting on each side was low, Sergeant-Major Scott making the best effort with 20 for the Conservatives. J. Hutchinson (19), headed the Whitecross scot e list.</p>
        <p>The following is tbe Leli—gne Table up-toglate : Phi. Won Lost Drn. Pt.. W'g't'n Conser. l6 .14 1 . 1. 29 Slakey lt ... 10 ... 3 ... I ... 21 Whitecross Rea Sankey Wire Mills 16 7 ... 7 ... 2 ... 16 Walkers Brewery Latchford Wee. l3 ... 4 ... 7 ... 2 ... 10 Orford Rylands Bros Wildarepl Brewery 14 ... 2 ... 12 ... 0 .. 4 The above table doe. opt include the matches, Sinke7 v. Rylands Bros. and Walker's Brewery v. Orford which were cancelled.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall triumphed over Helsby by a majority of 21,J Hatching displasing capital form for his 19.</p>
        <p>Boiling Notes. TO-DAY'S DIARY. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT DOWLING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>nso. FOR CII Dun Head v. North Asitton,at the Bull's Head.</p>
        <p>FIsL FR COW.ATION. Junior Conservativea v. King and Queen at the Junior Conservative Club. WORKS ASSOCIATION. AXNUAI. H SNl,l4' tr. At the Horse and Jockey. WARRINGTON AND DLSTICr BOW LING ASSOCIATION. INDIVIDUAL MERIT COMPETITION. FINAL.</p>
        <p>ALBERT GLEAVe9 GREAT srecEss,</p>
        <p>Considering the attractiveness of the programme at the King and Queen last Saturday afternoon it was not surprising to find another good crowd to witness the final stages of the Individual Merit Competition. To give more zest to the proceedings, the weather was all that could be desired, there being a wealth of sunshine from stet t to finish. The first hour and a half was occupied in polishing AT the fourth round. In this particular round there was undoubtedly some fire howling shown. It was a bit of a surprise to see Tom Davies go down to Jim Firth. On the whole the expected msn qualified for the fifth round. With four representatives from the Cemetery, two from the Bull's Head and two from the King and Queen the ' draw was eagerly awaited for. It revealed itself (a fact which was remarkable) that none of the club mates had come out of the hat together. The round resulted in favour of Tom Eliison, li. nigger, Albert Gleave and All Scott. The final competitors were Albert Gleave aid All Scott, both of whom have done excellent service for the count, y.</p>
        <p>The final was the outstanding featnre—one indeed which quite dwarfed everything else during this seas m. There was truly magnificent bowling, which can easily be realised when both players reazhed 20 points each. It was also surprising to find that all the four woods had to be played, to decide the winner. S.cott laid game, and Gl,ave ultimately came along with his last wood, and beat Scott by a few inches. tileave well deserved the hearty cheering which greeted him, on such a splendid victory. Scott took his defeat (though hard beaten) in a sportsmanlike manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FOURTH ROUND.</head>
        <p>FOURTH ROUND.</p>
        <p>R. TRI,OER V. IV. NOM:MOVE. Norgrove, of 'Wands, wh had showb some grand bowling in his previous games, now failed to produce his form against Trigger. It was intimated to me that 'Digger would require a lot of beating, his practice games having been perfect. And no it worktd out. On a nice twenty-five yards m irk across the green he simply played ducks and drakes with Norgrove. Game registering 16-6 in Trigger's favour, at the final end Norgrove was compelled to strike, but missed the object. Trigger was declared the winner 21-9.</p>
        <p>JACK JOLLEY y. GEORGE SHASy,</p>
        <p>These two club mates played a formidable game up to the ninth end, the game registering 7-6 in Jolley's favour. Jolley got away in stout ling fashion, increas- ing his proportion to 16-9. Shaw never gave of his true farm, 1 am sure, Jolley twain a splendid victory 21-9.</p>
        <p>HARRY EYE, V. R. StiVDERLAND. Sunderland opened the game with three to his credit. At the sixth end they were three in all,and peeled egsin at five. Sunderland then maintained a steady defence, running his snore to 11. Harry Eyes, not to be out- witted, reversed the proceedings so.neschat, the calling 18 all and 15 all. The concluding part of the game went in Sunderland's favour. He coutributed six of a run to his scare and ran to game 21-17.</p>
        <p>TOM DAVIES V. J. FIRTH.</p>
        <p>Tom Davies looked like an easy winner in the early I part of his game against Jim Firth, but he fell away' in a MOnt surprising manner. Firth, bowling with extraordinary effect, caught Davies the fourteenth end 11 all. It did not take Firth long to get in front and completely change the aspect of the game, when it was 9-2 against him and terminate the game 21-17 in his favour. lb. Fearobead representatives are wondering how Tom got defeated.</p>
        <p>02111 It GAMES. Alt. Soatt easily defeated R. Masksll wbo was no match, 21-12.</p>
        <p>Albert Gleams got the better of Toro Humphreys by 5 cholko. Both Dialers rendered a good scount of themselves, their length 000 excellent, 21-16.</p>
        <p>TOM ELLISON V. TOM ,ILIARRIE.</p>
        <p>For (Name to get only half of tart game against Tom Ellison and on his own green (not forgetting his splendid games in the preliminary stages of the com- petition), requires a lot of summing up. I don't kaow any other game except bowls where it varies so much. However, the only conclusion I can 00193 LO is that Quarrie requires a little Larden'og for competition games. Ellison, with a strongly contrasted style, male nimself an easy winner by 21-10.</p>
        <p>D. BROMLEY.</p>
        <p>V. C. 11.013F.RTS.</p>
        <p>Bromley qualified for the fifth round in defeating Coarlie Roberts 2147. Roberts was always in arrears ft im the twelfth end. Bromley bowled r-ith great skill and judgment alongside the gteen. on • mark woich has proved a good patch for him all through the competition.</p>
        <p>FIFTH ROUND. TOM ELLISON V. D. BROMLEY. So well did Ellison do in this round spin at Bromley that he soon became favourite for the competition. By tiodiog a nice mark commonly known as the velvet, he made an extraordinary break of nineteen. Bromley played an erratic game. I think he would have done better had he weighed up the situation a little more. By Keeping the tooter company and never having a look at the other end, is sometimes unprofitable. It takss Ellison for using his head in a game of bowls.</p>
        <p>A. SCOTT V. J. JOLLY. After peeling at four Saott got aeny with a valuable run of 13 to his credit, thanks to a brilliant piece of bowling. Jolley appeared to be utterly outplayed, Szott easily qualifying for the semi-final.</p>
        <p>A ni.F:Avr_V. IL SMInF.RLAND</p>
        <p>GUM Made himself a strong favourite. So splendidly dm lei WWI 111 tele bulb tee, the game was never in doubt. (Heave annexed the gime quite eaail 1.-17.</p>
        <p>R. TRIGGER V. 1. }MTH. This game was • neck and neck sort of affair, but Trigger being the most experienced player brought oft a clever victory 21-14.</p>
      </div>
