        <p>(Applause.) He trusted the time would not be far divan; when Mr. Soott would be deciarad champion of Warrington. He was greatly intereste lie howling, because it was the game patronized by the work,ag men of Warrington. He WAS always interested is the social f motions of w irking men, for ha realized that after to. strentaaus wark of the week no one bstter deserved nomad n than the waraing man. ( I,pplause.) Bowling win untlcahtedly a flue game and end brought out the deer qualities of those participating in it. He was pleatel that the merit competition had been such a success. He regretted that the attendance had not been as large as usual, but that was no doubt due to the number of competitions at relent being lield and the lateness of the season. He congratulated the officials on the splendid manner in which the arrange- ment of the competi ion had been carried out. (Applause). Mr. Steel then handed the cup to Mr. Gleave. Mr. GLEAVE said he WAS very proud to have won that competition in his own town. He had won many prizes in his howling career and had haen very success- tat to s season, but ha had won no orize which plessed him more than that of winning Warrington's premier prize. (Applause.) Mr. S,,orr, in response to calls for a speech, said he had greatly enjoyed ths game and he congratulated Mr Gleave on the great game he had played. He trusted at some future time be would win that comeetitoo. A vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Steel, on the proposition of Mr. G. BALL, jllO., seoonied by Mr. CLEAVE.</p>
