      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. n</head>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. n</p>
        <p>"THE CASE FOR HOME RULE." A BOOK TO BE READ TO-DAY. At a time when every newspaper you take up has some reference, accurate or otherwise, to Ireland's political problem, and when every man you meet wants to be informed concerning the one live question in contemporary politica, no more valuable service could be rendered to this question, alike from the English and Irish points of view, than that which M Stephen Gwyro, ALP., has performed in "The Case for Home Rule" (Mamma and Co., Dublin). Mr. Jolio Redmond contributes al int -eduction. rime and thin again has Mr. Redmond defined the Dish national demand and his plain straightforward words can surely go no farther in clearness than those of the definition given here. " The demand for Home Rule," Mr. Redmond writes, " means no less and no more than this : Ireland asks for an Irish Parliament, , with an executive responsible to it, to deal with purely Irish affairs, subject to Imperial supremacy." 'All that is asked for, he continue;, is the "liberty to attempt by our ona resources, to cute I our own diseases, to cope with our own problems This Imperial Government is not fitted for the task. What lies at the root of this whole controversy is a feeling in the predominant partner' that to concede Ireland's demand would be to accept defeat that justice cannot be done now without admitting injustice in the past, and that Ireland's vict iry must be England's humiliation. Such an attitude is natural enough alter a struggle so long protracted ; yet it is unworthy of a great country. In such a matter, generosity and statesmanship go hand in hand. That has been proven in Smth Africa. Ireland remains to confirm that noble example." We should like to have this sane utterance graved on the desks of those j iurnalista iu whose thoughts the name of the Irish Leader is nnseparable from notions of a ruthless Dictator—a political iconoclast bent on the destruct- of the historic constitution of the Mash race, with 5.20,000 as his smashing weapon ! Any Englishman who desires to ascertain how the majority of the people ia Ireland regard the system by which they are governed must needs know some- thing of the origin of that system, awl the traditions in which it has been administered. That information is given in the first part of Mr. Gwynn's book. The bird's eye view of England's relation with Ireland to the time of Union, which Mr. ;spin here submits. is for the greaser part made up of .juotations from accepted authorities—most of whom are English, ind free therefore from any Nationalist bias. That little known tract of Lish history covering the sixteenth and seventeenth centuties, with its vehement oppres- sion of the native Irish, a-, well as the suppression of Irish commerce in the sevetteenth and eighteenth centuries, Mr. awynn passes in brief review, and we imagine that the sense of indignation which the English historian, John Richard Green felt in writina of this portion of England's tyranny in Ireland will be experienced by not a few of his c nintryruen when reading this informing summary of historic i fact. And t is with the fact only that Mr. 'Gwynn conceins himself There is here no bitterness of memory and not a word which will make it harder for the m-st sensitive Briton to understand the question cf Ireland, and its untoward history. Tee Act of Union is, of course, examined and the methcds of its carrying, strange methods even for the period in which it was enacted, are also described. There is a real need for this. One Cabinet Minister admits to not having even read this famous Act - the basis of the present relations between the two countries—and we fancy !that s-me of the leading supporters of the Union might fare badly on it. Mr. Gwynn has a number of apposite and valuable! quotations from Professor Dicey and the late Mr Lecky, both Unionists, whose ei udition and standing compel attention. Here is Professor Dicey's verdict. "The Act of Union was, in short, an agreement which, could it have been referred to a court of law, must at once have been cancelled as a contract hopelessly tainted with fraud and corruption." Mr Lecky wrote f the passing of the Act of Union is these terms : "It was not only a great crime, but was, like moat great crimes, a great blunder. It unitel the Parlia- ments and divided the nasions." Mr. Gwynn proceeds to trace the results which have followed the Act of Union dawn to our own day and, when he comes to try the Government of Ireland by the tests of whether it has been jug., whether it has been successful, and whether it has been conducted on rational lines, he has a heavy indictment to put is against the method of government on each of these counts. This instructive linking-up of the past with the present in Ireland is followed by chapters devoted to the question : Is Ireland fit to govern herself ? An abundance of interesting evideoce is submitted t support the contention that Ireland herself can manage her own affairs in a much better manner than under the" mudd,ing through" system of running Ireland from Westminster, where the Pal liamentary machine, in the opinion of all parties, is hopelessly over- weighted. More than twenty-five years ago G'adatone declared ti e instrument of Parliament to be utterly incapable of &amp;dna the work demanded of it, and every Session of the present Parliament goes to show how I increasingly difficult it is to legislate within limita- tions framed fir a by-gone age. But over and 1 above that point. the result of the working or tle County CiAinci's Act, which the Chairman Lord Salisbury thought more dangerous than Home Rulel affords a striking testimony to the efficiency arra economy of administration on the part of the Irish people. The imbecile talk of Separation is dealt with, and Mr. Gwynn, himself a Protestant, is especially effective in his treatment if the supposed fears of the Protestant minority. We think his remarks on the military aspect of Home Rule, an aspect seldom</p>
        <p>10</p>
        <p>10</p>
        <p>9 11 5</p>
        <p>s</p>
        <p>adverts I t muit arreit the mind of any Englis'unao who has soy thJught for the honour and security of his own land.</p>
        <p>But to the practical politician, who is not d:aturbed by the midsummer nastiness "of the Orange threat, of armed resistaece which is here submitted to a keen excanination in the light of their former promises and their uniform non-performance, perhaps the latter part of this book will appeal most strongly. There he will fad rational argument supported by hard facts and figures on the point of whether, even it Home Rule ha granted, Ireland conld manage to pay her own way. Che sugaestive comparison of Ireland and Cacada— the self-governing c olony with the closest resemblances, with its two cieeds and ita two races. to Ireland— precedents to Home Rule, and answers to objections. should all be as meat and drink to the Liberal worker who meats business or the Irish question. We are sure the book will soon be in the hands r.f every such I.iheral. It can be read in an afternoon and the reader will get up from its perusal feelirg stronger than ever before the clear reason and simple justice of the Homo Rule cause. To many of the author's friends, it has long been a subject of wonder how be manages in the suilst of so many and such eared activities to turn out the excellent work he does. The CAse f " tulle to that woolen</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>CRITICISM OF TORY MEETING.</p>
        <p>One of the most successful meet Men held tinder the maniocs of the Warrington Liberal Association tads place at Bridge Foot on Tuesday evening .he quite two thousand people assembled. Mr. Peter Delaney oocnpied the chair and Mr. Harry New, Mr. F.. Wynne Jones addressed the meeting. The CHAIRMAN said that the Liberal Party were fighting for democracy and better conditions of liveli- hood and he was sure that a brighter era awaited the people of this country. He felt that the Government had acted wisely and thoughtfully with regard to the recent, strike in compelling the railway directors to meet the men. Heal. pointed out that if the military had not been called out to assist in maintaining order the Government would have been deserving of blame from all Ousts.</p>
        <p>Mr. NEvv,after alluding to the labour unrest and the land topic, spoke at some length opus the meeting of the Women's Tariff Reform League held at Town's End on Saturday evening. Mr. Smith referred to the picnic. at. Frodil an. What be (Mr New) bad against the Women's 'Arid Reform League was the fact that the league should take t's children to Frodshans nader a disguise and label them with a Tariff " Rvfnros" label, when the funds bad been acquired from all clauses of the community. He thought that Mr. Smith's statement tSet the labour unrest was entirely duo to the speeches made by Mr. L'oyd-Ge rgs in Etat Loudon era cowardly in the extreme and uowcrthy of him as he knew Host such was o,t the case. It wan, Ii mover, ctaracteristio of ths methods emplopsi by tve "din horde." The speaker next quoted from a speech which Viscount Deerhurst made at a Primrose League gathering at</p>
        <p>Dumbteam Ilnil,Worcester, in the con7se twhich -he said " there was no doubt that the Government went to King Edward when be won very ill locked, and demanded from him hie guarantee. and there was no doubt, be added, the., it was the worry and bother of these moo--• Lloyd George and crew '—which hastened his death. They bad KILLED ONE MONARCH,</p>
        <p>and they wanted to dethrone soother. They wanted to create a Rep:fill° in this country, with Mr. Asquith, Lloyd-George, or worse still, the Blenheim pug, se its first President." Mr. New characterised this as a sample of manners from the gentlemanly party. Referring to the Home Rule question, Mr. New pointed out that whereas they had given Home Role to Australis, Cansdi, and Sonth Africa, the latter being only a few sears aeo Heir bitterest enemies on tho field, yet Ireland whicn had produced such men as Lord Kitchener, Lord Roberts, and many other world-famed men, ens not allowed to govern itself. He also dealt at some length with the laid question and explained the &amp;wises in the Budget, qnoting several instances of their good effect on industrial communities. Mr. E. WYNNE-JONES referred to Mr. Harold Smith's . _ .</p>
        <p>statements last Saturday evr sing respecting the Labour unrest and the cost of living, when Mr. Smith suggested that those could be averted under Tariff " Ileform." But Ms} in the world did not Mr mitt tell his audi- ence that Tariff Reform—or Pro on as it should be 13 called—did not solve those problems in other countries? He (the speaker) asserted that there were more strikes in Protectionist countries than there were in Free Trade Britain and it was common knowledge that the cost of Rain; was higher there. How thee was Tariff "Reform" going to do what Mr. Smith sug- gested ? He considered it an insult to temper with the questi m of unemployment In the way that the Tariff " Reformers" did by suggesting that their policy was going to do anything to lessen the evil. Neither Free Trade ndr Tariff " Reform " would solve the unemployment problem. That problem would be solved when the long arm and deep purse of the State came to the reeCtl3—when the State made up its mind that every man who wanted to work should :rave work to do. !Applause). Toe Liberal put y to-day stood for the maintenance of the Free Trade principle upon which—no one could dispute it— the prosperity of this country had been built. Oar industries had grown on free access to raw material from all the world, and to let Tariff " Reformers " have their own way would be to strikes blow at the industries of the country. Liberals not only stood for Free Trade, but for fees land. Land was the primary element in the production of all wealth, and a con- , trolling factor in the distribution of wealth. Under , our present system the right of people to the land was not recognised as it should be. Could It be right that , any class of runs should be allowed to withhold from . use God's greet gift to the race—the land ? And that was what people were able to do ;soder present coo- -1 ditions. Through Mr. Lloyd George's 1903 nudge% a , start had already been made in the unlocking of the land. Liberals said at the time that the taxes were only 1 , the thin edge of the wedge, and now the wedge bad . been driven in, land was already beginning to come more easily, and readily to those who desired it.. : (Applause).</p>
        <p>PRISONER'S ALLEGATIONS AGAIN 3 POLICEMAN. James Paton. of Waterloo-street. orb, insisted on Saturday that hia case should be remanded for further evidence, was again brought before the St. Melees magistrates on Monday. Constables All., and Whittingham gar. 3 evidence that at c cit on Satur- day mining Paton was lighting in Liverpool-street. Paton denied this, and alleged that the constant* watt drunk. Plisonet's record showed that he recently served sin months on. aimilarlor charge. Ile was sent to goal for throe months.</p>
