      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SWAM AFTER A WATER HEN.</head>
        <p>SWAM AFTER A WATER HEN.</p>
        <p>Yowl Mans Sad End</p>
        <p>An inquest was laid at the police station on Monjay 1 by Coroner Uri4house on the body of Arthur Thornitey aged 23 years, who was drowned in the river Mersey on beturday. Jane thorniley, mother of the deceased, residing at 1 30, Asbivood-averme, said thnt the deceased was r employed as a wireurawer at Messrs. li)lauds' Bros. I Church-street. She list sow hi n alive and in god health at 9.40 p.m. on Friday, the lot int , when he returned to work after his supper. About 1 p.m. the , following dry, she was informed that the deceased had been drowned whilst trying to get a bird front the river Mersey. the body was brought home shout 2 p.m Henry Welahy, 11, Sharp-street, said that. ab.,ut 8.30 r a.m, on the 2nd inst., in company with • no über ot , young men Snoring whom wan the doneased,he uentror g walk along the ever Mersey nerr ho Ptddimto:L as A few hundred yards away from the looks they mst some men who had horn shooting, and they asked if they bad is dog with thorn an they bad shot is water-hen which bad fallen into the river. The men then walket away and deceased undressed and re-dressed himself twice u though hesitating wahether to go into the water or not, rho last time be undressed he went into the water and swain towards the bird which was aholt, half-way across the river. He reached the bird and gribbed it with both hands. He appetred Lu get a mow hid of water and started to blow it out. He started to return with the bird, but altar he had gone about three yards ho sank hut came to the surface. Ile again sank end failed to re-appear. Somebody ran for • life-belt. Frank ltortotr, a wiredrawer, residing at 98, Pierpoint• street, a orelsorated and aid that when the lite-belt was procured he undressed and went into the water but could not rend, rally assiitaute. l'eter Itathboue, a gas stoker, 7, AVater-street, said thst &amp;mot 11 50 on the day in question he comnbmced grappling operttiuns and recovered the body opho.ite Wens, Fields mar Paddington Locks at I 1.45 a.ro. There were no marks on the body nor an 3 thing in the bands. The depth of the water where the body was !recovered would he about eight or ten feet. The jury returned a vardicl of " Accielentally I howned," the Foreman slitting tbat they desired to thank Frank Ilurtoft for his commendable action in going to the assisUttice cf the deceased, although his efforts were umuccessful.</p>
        <p>That:MLA of Works has now restored Westminster Abbey to Its original tate, and Mr. Downer, the assis'ant secretary of the department, on behalf of the Ofllto, in ltlon.lay handed over the Abbey to Canon Doodling on behalf of the Dean and Chapter. A Brottou eucine-driver named Richard Thompson fell 3000.. over the cliff onto the rooks below at Solt- born (Yus) on Mondry and was killrked instantly.</p>
