      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, II P. THE FUTURE OF THE INSURANCE</p>
        <p>BILL.</p>
        <p>The unrest in the Labour world has for</p>
        <p>„. a time put the Insurance Bill into the back- ground. But with a return to normal times this important measure will again claim its share of public attention. The interval between now and the reassembling of Parliament will probably settle its fate. In the last week of the Committee stage in the House of Commons the position of the Bill certainly improved, hut a number of things have happened within the last week or two which have not helped its chances of becoming law this year. A number of Unionist members, who have taken a keen and sympathetic interest in the Bill, have issued a manifesto upon it in which they complain very strongly that the Chancellor of the Exchequer has not carried out his promise to let the House of Commons take a free hand in shaping the Bill, and in trying to make it a satisfactory and workable measure. The attitude of these Unionist members up to the issue of the manifesto had been to do nothing which could lay them open to the charge that they had been trying to wreck the Bill, but they are so dissatisfied with the amended part of the Bill, as it now stands, that they have expresed their intention to leave themselves free to vote against the Bill</p>
        <p>on Third Reading if it cannot be made more satisfactory on Report. The meeting of the Associated Chambers of Commerce, which</p>
        <p>met in Dublin last week, passed a resolution asking for the postponement of the Bill. This is a kindly way of putting the Bill to a pain- less death, for it is certain ;that if it does not pass this year it never will pass in anything like its present form. If one could be assured that another Bill would take the place of _ . . . .</p>
        <p>this, then one would heartily wish to see</p>
        <p>this out of the way, because the second Bill would certainly be an improvement on this, as the Chancellor would be bound to adopt the more reasonable suggestions which have been made for improving the scheme. FROM FRIENDLY QUARTERS. But the most serious criticism which has been brought against the Bill within the last fortnight has come from two quarters which hitherto have been very friendly both to the Bill and to the Chancellor. The first was a speech delivered by the Grand Master of the Manchester Unity, in which he charged the Chancellor with having broken faith with the Friendly Societies. It must be that one of the charges at least made by the Grand Master is true, namely, the Chancellor's _ .</p>
        <p>amendment by which collecting and insurance societies may become "approved societies" under the Bill. These were expressly excluded in the Bill when it was introduced, and the price the Chancellor had paid to the insurance companies for their exclusion was to prohibit the "approved societies" from giving death benefits as part of the national scheme. But the insurance companies have been moving heaven and earth to get their fingers into this pie, and they have succeeded. To gain their support the Chancellor has broken faith with the Friendly Societies. A THREATENED RUPTURE.</p>
        <p>Newspaper readers will have noticed in the reports of meetings of friendly societies where the Insurance Bill has been discussed that there has often been a good deal of feeling shown. This is only a little of the steam which escapes from the boiling condition inside. It is a matter of common knowledge that the leaders of the great friendly societies are feeling very sore about the way in which they have been treated. 1 The Insurance Bill as it was introduced into Parliament was not recognisable as the same measure which had been agreed upon between the Chancellor and the friendly societies. The greatest danger to the Bill is in the present attitude of the friendly societies. The Chancellor's proposal to allow the insurance companies to manage the Insurance scheme will probably bring the great friendly societies into open hostility to the Bill; and if that happens the Bill is dead.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CRITICISM. The second serious criticism of the. Bill came last week from the "Nation," the chief weekly organ of Liberalism. In a lengthy article on the future of the Bill this party organ says that the Bill has lost so heavily in popularity under detailed criticism that the doubt has arisen whether the Govern- ment will do well.to persist in it. It goes on to say that the burdens to be imposed by the Bill on workmen and on employers are very serious, and only very urgent reasons can justify the imposition. The journal admits that the Socialist criticism of the measure has been the most damaging brought against it, and that this criticism has impressed thoughtful politicians. The article sympa- thises with the contention of the Socialists that the State contribution is too inadequ ate A POSSIBLE DISASTER.</p>
        <p>The " Nation " in conclusion urges a con- tention which I have advanced in this column and elsewhere, namely that such a Bill as this had far better be dropped altogether than forced through Parliament against the popular will. Nothing but practically unanimous national approval could justify the State in imposing a com- pulsory scheme of this sort upon the workers and in demanding from them a compulsory contribution. Unless the workers approve it, it will turn out to be, if it should be passed, the most disastrous piece of legisla- tion ever enacted. And it must be admitted that so far as we can judge by all the means available, the attitude of the public is not one of approval, but rather of opposition. The " Nation " makes the practical suggestion that Mr. Lloyd George should frankly try to ascertain what the working classes really do think about the Bill. The Liberal Party agents in the country have reported that the Bill is not a good election asset. The recep- tion by the friendly societies is not encourag- ing. THE TRADE UNION OPPORTUNITY. The Trades Union Congress which is meet- ing this week in Newcastle might help the Chancellor of the Exchequer. But it will be an unusual thing if the Congress gives a clear and definite lead. The special Con- ference on the Bill which met in London in June was little less than a farce. The delegates came to the Conference without any instructions from their lodges. There was no real discussion of the Bill. The Con- ference was caucus-ridden from beginning to end. It simply registered the decision of the Joint Committee, which had never seriously considered the Bill. The members of that Joint Committee carry with them the votes of their unions, and these are sufficient to carry their own recommendations. Nobody who knows the actual facts will attach the least importance to the decisions of that special conference on the Insurance Bill. As a matter of fact, so far as the rank and file of the trade unions have expressed their</p>
        <p>views on the Bill, these have been in direct opposition to the resolutions of that Conference. The Trades Congress may be too fully occupied in dealing with purely industrial matters to find time to adequately discuss the Insurance Bill, but whatever it does it is to be hoped that it will not come to a decision without a full discussion. The delegates will be better posted in the Bill than could be the case last June, and I shall be very much surprised if the Congress supports the Bill with the same unanimity as the Conference last June did. The Trade Union Congress has been very much to blame for its neglect to express its mind on this question of national insurance in the past. It has known for three years that the Government were contemplating the intro- duction of measures dealing with sickness and unemployment by way of insurance. The Parliamentary Committee sent a deputation to Germany to look into the systems at work in that country. It issued a report which added nothing to the information of those who had studied the books on the subject But nothing at all was done to give the Government a lead. The Chancellor had to work in ignorance of what the views of trade unionists were on these matters, and it is not he, but the trade union leaders who are to blame if the Bill he has drafted is not satisfactory. One other thing it is hoped the ' Trades Congress Will not do. It is to be hoped it will not follow the advice which will come from certain quarters, and express I its willingness to take the Sickness part of the Scheme in any form if it can have the Unemployment part with it. If the Sickness Insurance scheme is dropped we may not get another scheme just yet, but if the Unem- ployment part is not passed this year some- thing better will be passed at no distant dite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>ICHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductcd by UNCLE CALEB</head>
        <p>ICHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductcd by UNCLE CALEB</p>
        <p>DOGS AS SENTINELS.</p>
        <p>The other day I was reading that the authorities of a Cathedral had chosen a dog to be its custodian by night, and the news- paper gave a photograph of the dog that has taken up duties. Perhaps my readers would like to know of a dog that fulfils a much more useful place. I was reading of it only this week in one of the American papers that comes to me. A DOG THAT RINGS A FOG BELL. No animal in the world equals a dog as a faithful sentinel when it comes to " keeping watch and ward" in the interests of its master. Off the shores of Alaska, on a small, rocky island, is a little light station, connected with which is a fog-bell. During the continuance of dense fogs, which frequently prevail, the bell is used to warn vessels of the danger of approaching too near the island and coast. The light-keeper has a large and very intelligent shepherd dog that answers to the name of Don Carlos. This dog has been trained to toll the fog-bell when the weather is very heavy. So well trained in Don Carlos that, when the fog comes rolling in, he rushes, unbidden by the keeper, to the bell and begins to tug at the rope and to sound the alarm. This the faithful fellow continues to do until relieved by his master. Don Carlos often takes his turn at the bell during the night when the keeper is busy. "I do not see how I could get along without Don Carlos," the keeper declares. "as lam all alone, and have no other assistant, mt I can always rely implicitly on my dog Fle knows his duty and faithfully performs. t, whether told to do so or not." THIS WEEK'S STORY. PERHAPS SHE WAS RIGHT.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Neighbors: They tell me your sin is in the football team. Mrs. Malaprop: Yes, indeed. Mrs. Neighbors: Do you know what position he plays? Mrs. Malaprop; Ain't sure, but I think he's one of the drawbacks. TUE TRAVELS OF A SMILE. My father smiled this morning when He came downstairs, you see, At mottle, ; and wheu he smiled then She turned and smiled at me; And when she smiled at me I went And smilid at Mary Ann Out in the hitches, and she lent It to the hired man.</p>
        <p>So then he smiled at some one whom .He saw when going by, Who also smiled and ere he knew Had twinkle.. in his eye; S r he went to his office then And smiled right at his clerk, Who pot some more ink on his pen And smiled back from his work.</p>
        <p>So when his clerk went home he smiled Right at his wife, and she Smiled over at their little child As happy as could be; And then the little child, she took</p>
        <p>The smile to school, and when She smiled at teacher from her book Teacher smiled back again. And then the teacher passed on one To little James Mcßride, Who couldn't get his lemons dose No matter how he tried ; And Jamesy took it home and told How teacher smiled at him When he was tired, and didn't scold, But said: " D.n't worry, Jim !"</p>
        <p>And when I happened to be there That very night to play, Bis mother had a smile to spare Which came across my way ; And then I took it after while Back home, aud mother said " Here is that very selfsame smile Come back with us to bed ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>HOMING.</head>
        <p>HOMING.</p>
