      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PILGRIMAGFS</head>
        <p>PILGRIMAGFS</p>
        <p>HA WARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE HOMELAND OF GLADSTONE.</p>
        <p>Chester is, naturally, a favourite venue for many local cyclists, and as it is, from its position, the gateway leading to North Wales from the great manufacturing towns of Lancashire, many thonsand of cyclists pass through its ancient streets in the course of the summer months. Tha city of the Dee is a capital centr for many short tours, and the round I am about to describe is one of the many easy excursions which may be included in the list.</p>
        <p>Leaving the ancient city of Chester, the Deva of the Romans and the quarters of their crack regiment the Twentieth Legion, I crossed over the old bridge which spars the Dee, by way of Bridge-street. A notice on an old house reminds the traveller that here King Edgar was rowed across the river by eight tributary princes, and it was here that one of the oldest mills mentioned in history stood. This historical building,how- ever, has recently been taken down, and only the foundations remain. I cycled along the opposite side of the river to the pleasant village of Eccleston, where there is a picturesque ferry, and then on to Eaton Hall. The famous "Golden Gates" stand opposite to the main entrance to the hall and grounds, which are open to the public at intervals on payment of a small admission fee, the proceeds going to the Chester Infirmary. On reaching the gates I turned to the right, and passing the obelisk cycled along a fine open drive, with a view of the Welsh Hills in front. On</p>
        <p>wedding took place. We may well believe it was a happy day for Harwarden, but it would have been a happier had the villagers realised how much it would mean for them. We stroll into the park and see the terrace from which Mr. Gladstone used to address the assembled thousands of trippers. It is adorned by fine red roses and commands a beautiful view of the park. We do not ask permission to inspect the inner preserves of the Castle grounds, preferring to take a walk through the woods. But its quiet beauty and home traditions suggest a striking con- trast to the days of yore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>'A GRIM REMINDER OF TIIE PAST.</head>
        <p>'A GRIM REMINDER OF TIIE PAST.</p>
        <p>Less than a hundred yards from the terrace is a stone hidden under the bracken which bears the words; "Trust in' God for , Bread, and to the King for Justice, Protection snd Peace. This mill was built A.D., 1767, by Sir John Glynn°, Bart., Lord of this Manor. Wheat was this year at 9s. and barley at ss. 6d. a bushel. Luxury was at a great height, and charity extensive; but the poor were starving, riotous and hanged." Leaving the castle I turned down a steep road with a gate at the foot immediately opposite, and I had an enjoyable and leisurely walk and ride the entire length of Bilberry Hill, say, for two miles, under ancient trees, with a carpet of bracken, until I joined the highway above coming from Chester towards Mold. Here is the populous neighbourhood of Buckley, with its great brickworks and colleries ; and it is marvellous that,</p>
        <p>sandwiched as they are between this district and the great villages and works in the Dee Valley below, Ilawarden Park and Woods retain their sylvan beauty. On the fringe of the woods, and near the Mold road, stands the village of Penymynydd, which contains a small modern church no passer-by would think it worth while to explore. Thousands of cyclists pass it yearly without knowing that its interior reveals some</p>
        <p>!• 101t**41#'44' '44" ,4,34 k • ... • , • , ' ,Natre-s•-f -144 . ' • i 1.411 :1-91 aigligtil ~. „ _ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ST. DEINIOL'S LIBRARY, HAWARDEN.</head>
        <p>ST. DEINIOL'S LIBRARY, HAWARDEN.</p>
        <p>coming to the Wrexham and Chester high- road I passed through a gate on the opposite side, and then through other gates, and along a road lined with trees, until I came to Broughton Church. Here is a junction of the Mold and Holywell highroads from Chest”r, and continuing towards Holywell I came to a lodge at the left of the road, and passing through the gate I was in the picturesque park of Hawarden. The land rises to the right and drops towards the left where a considerable brook ripples along to join the great waters below. The park is well studded with trees, and the wooded heights beyond are known as Bilberry Hill. The road undulates considerably, and there are one or two sharp gradients. Passing through another gate the Castle of Hawarden appears in the distance to the right, and the tree-covered ruins of the old , castle to the left. These ruins, open to the visitors, are well worth seeing. The flag- , pole on the top, when furnished with a flag, is an object of interest to travellers on the London and Holyhead railway in the valley below, as marking the spot where once lived the greatest statesman of his age. Cyclists visiting Hawarden may use any of the roads in the park except those marked private. After passing the old castle the road terminates in the centre of the village opposite the Glynne _Arms Hotel. _ . _</p>
        <p>wonderful work, the result of the many years labour of a former vicar. In the church is a brass tablet erected by Mr. Gladstone as a tribute to' John Ellis Trougton, M.A. who " used his rare gifts, genius and taste, in himself decorating all the interior of this church, consecrated in 1843, by colouring and frescoes and sculptures and window-staining with much labour, but with no detriment to the faithful discharge of his manifold parish duties." The afternoon had passed all too quickly, and the dusk was now rapidly coming on, as I hastened along the beautiful length of road outside the park. The old mill showed dimly through the trees to the right as I emerged again into the village of Hawarden, near the new mansion erected by Miss Helen Gladstone, and with the lights of the great ironworks at Shotton before me, I reached Queen's Ferry Station in time to entrain home. J.N.</p>
      </div>
