      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CANCER VICTIM FOUND DROWNED.</head>
        <p>CANCER VICTIM FOUND DROWNED.</p>
        <p>An inquiry was held at the Chapel-house In Sankey, by the district coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse) on Monday afternoon concerning the deat's of Harriet Purcell, aged 41 3ears, the wife of Dsvid Purcell, a compositor, ...siding at 20, Manley•street, Warrington, and swims. body was recovered from the St„ llvleos and Sankey Cann), at S.nkey, on Suaday. It appearrd from the evidence of David Pore*ll that the deceased hal been in very bad health for over twelve months. Six month, ago she was °pentad upon for Cancer of the breast at the %Varrington Infirmary, and transferred to the Warrington Workhouse Hospital three months ago. Deceased left the hospital on Saturday last, and was then in poor health. Ile last saw her alive &amp;boot elven o'clock on Saturday night when she left the house for the purpose of looking for other apsrtments. . . .</p>
        <p>Patrick Conlon stated that whilst walking along the towing path of the canal on Sunday morning And when within about 2on yards of Buttermilk Bridge he came emcee a woman's straw hat and black hat, which be Look to the Police Station.</p>
        <p>_ P.C. Jones epoke as to grappling for the body, which he recovered at 2.15 on Sunday last. The jury returned a verdict of " Pound Drowned," there being no evidence se to bow the deceased got into the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DEAR FOOD.</head>
        <p>DEAR FOOD.</p>
        <p>It has frequently bee—n—e'l:o;;;Tn.these rises that the great cheapness of food, %hinb began in the early eifghties and continued up to the first two or three year. o the present century, My dile to aCCideatal conditions that had never occu,red previously in the history of the world, and as far as can he seen, are never likely to occur again. To soycne who has really investigated the problem this statement is merely • truism. The phenomenon of abundance wan due to reasons an clear that no one could mistake them. They were the opening up of new lines and the improvement of means of transport. Yet, although the abundance of these years was no thoroughly acchiental, it has beta roofs the basis of all kind. of policiee, theories, prognostications and beliefs an though what \VAS the most transient occurrence possible was a permanent and durable °lmago is the quantity of our food supply. The muftis of this bed reasooing are much more merino, than might have been expected, because people who been been pampered is the years of sbuadasce And it very difficult to return to those hard times to which their encestorn were accustomed. Take the poor people of out own country, who, on the whole, are bettor off than a similar class is any other country of the world. They, when food became cheep, atend,ned most of the homely methods they used to Imre of eking out the resources of the table. In this process they eli ..instol many wholesale srticles of food, such as oatmeal porridge and wbat used toles called pudding (We mean the pudding that William Cobbett talked ahout), not the dainties that go by that same to-day.—" Couatry Life."</p>
        <p>STANDARD BREAD WITH RAISINS.</p>
        <p>Replyies later to the toast of his health at the Coorectioners' hikers and Allied Trades Exhibition on Saturday, Sir George Reid held op a piece of stauderd bread mad remarked that he bad nerer teen it till now. He had been led bye newspaper to form etch • high opinion or it Oat he confessed his first view was dis- appointing. He angsasted to the bakers that a few Dudes or currants would make it as instantaneous SUOMI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>VILLAGE.</head>
        <p>THE VILLAGE.</p>
        <p>After lunch I walked through the village, and saw what is called the House of Correc- tion, a small dismal dungeon a few yards , away from the road, possibly in olden times an adjunct to the castle, but now quite shorn of its terrors. I expected to find Gladstone's Library, named after St. Deiniol, a small building containing a few hundred books, and was surprised to see it quite a collegiate- looking structure, containing apartments for students who come here to study books of reference on every imaginable subject. The Hawarden folks dearly loved Mr. Gladstone. There is a fine token of this love _ _ .</p>
        <p>in the square, a magnificent fountain erected on the occasion of his golden wedding and bearing the inscription "Whosoever will, let him take the water of life freely." It has also medallion profiles of William Ewart and Katherine Gladstone in bronze. The villagers come to your side as you stand and admire it and assure you that it was a real labour of love to put up this monument. And when they see visitors from every corner of the world standing to look at it they are proud indeed. Mr. and Mrs. Gladstone were the father and mother of the village and they are greatly missed. Young Hawarden is not neglectod. There are the local library, Institutes and Recreation Rooms promoted by Mr. Gladstone and his sons. MRS. GLADSTONE'S_ SECRET _CHARITIES.</p>
        <p>Just inside the Castle grounds, hidden from passers by, was Mrs. Gladstone's Home for elderly matrons. Her tender soul had a deep sympathy for those who had borne the burden of the day and were threatened with poverty in their declining years. Here many old women rested and spent the evening of their days in comfort. This Matrons' Home was one of Mrs. Gladstone's</p>
        <p>little secrets. She never allowed people to talk about it and enjoyed this piece of philan- thropy the more because she refused to let her right hand know what the left was doing. Close by is another philanthropy which also was dear to Mrs. Gladstone—a home for orphans and needy children. This comfort- able ivy covered home with its bell turret was controlled and supported by the Lady of Harwarden. How she must have enjoyed doing these things! I THE CHURCH AND A FAMOUS WEDDING.</p>
        <p>I went to the finely-proportioned parii.h church as a matter of course, but as it wa.i nearly destroyed by fire about 50 years ago I found little of antiquarian interest. The visitor is shown the seat near the choir which Gladstone occupied whilst listening to his favourite hvmm, ' l'raise to the Holiest in the height,' and a small brass cross inserted in the book-board marks the spot where died ' Edward White Benson, Archbibliop of Canterbury, Sunday, October 11th, 1896,' while on a visit to Hawarden. Here on a July day in Ifi39, there was a double wedding. Katherine and Mary Glynne were married to W. E. Gladstone and Lord Lyttleton. A gloom hung over the ' event for a mob had attacked Lord Lyttleton's house at Hagley. But at the last moment the storm cleared and the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>"I thiuk su. You must do more.</p>
        <p>"Whnt do you man?'</p>
        <p>I:117r;;;11-be "; man on board who will u.lp you. He is my servant. His name is ielencus. Ile will go on board whatever sea- el Nadir Julaver sails in. He will be dis- guised, perhaps as a Russian; anyhow, so ef- fectually disguised that neither Julevee nor his servant will recognise him. I dare not describe his appearance to yott, bat 1 communicate , RIM Be will know von by a ring which I shall place on the smallest finger of your left hand. You and he can therefore work in concert. But please understand that affairs have now reached such a serious magnitude that not only must I get the diamond back, but Nadir Julaver must not leave that vessel alive, for he must on no account whatever communicate te the Shah the story which he hopes to tell him." Mary Deepe started. "You want me to commit murder?" she said. "I —I am not prepared." "You can please yourself; I want nothing From you. If you have injured me, you are in danger. All my enemies are in danger. You have come into my presence add said , too much."</p>
        <p>Mary quailed. After a time she Raid: Will not the craft of the East allow itself to be met on one occasion by the craft of the West?"</p>
        <p>"*hat do you mean, woman?" mid the Miran.</p>
        <p>"Thin, Your Excellency. I happen to know the story of the diamond. I know your position. You fear Nadir Jtilayer because he can tell something to the Shah which will cause him to deprvie you of your rank, and probably to take your life." "Public dishonour, and death by slow tor- ture," said the Mirza in a tone of supreme indifference. "That is the portion of the man who fails."</p>
        <p>"Murder is always bad, Your Excellency. if you will permit me to say so," said Mary. "Now why can I not, with the assistance of your servant, Seleucus, play the trick upon Nadir Julaver which he has already played on you, and substitute for the real stone the false one? If I can manage that, nerd his life be sacrificed? Even if he does reach Persia and the palace of the Shah before you, you have but to appear boldly soon afterwards and prove him a liar, for he pre. scuts the false stone to the Shalt, whereas you, Your Excellency, bring him the real • Fountain of Beauty.' " The Mirza made no reply, lie resumed its restless, noiseless movements up and down the room. After a time he returned to Mary.</p>
        <p>"YOU will do," he said, "exactly as my urvant gelenens requires Of you. You will follow his directions in all respects. You are a woman—a clever one. You ought tc have been an Eastern, you were thrown away on the West. Name the reward you require for the service you propose to render me." "Ah said Mary. (The Mirza looked at her with the faintest smile hovering round his lips.) "I would ask." she said, "thirt thousand pounds in English gold on the day that I place the 'Fountain of Beauty' in Your Excellency's hands."</p>
        <p>"Yon shall have it, woman. Now go." "I•want money for my journey."</p>
        <p>"How much, "Three hundred pounds, Excellency." The Mira* touched his silver gong, end the man, Kern, entered the room.</p>
        <p>"F'etch gold and notes to the amount of three hundred pounds, and bring them here," said the Mirza.</p>
        <p>Toe man departed, brought back the money. and then immediately withdrew. "Take this money, and go," said the Mina. "My servant, Seleuens, will know all about you when you arrive. This little ring on your finger—allow me to put it on my- self, madam (the Mira&amp; slipped a small ring with a tiny glittering diamond on Mary's smallest finger)—"will ensure your recogni tion. Go. Understand the great peril of failure, and the reward—the brilliant reward --of success." "I shall not fail," said Mary Deepe. The velvet curtains were opened, and she passed out. When she had gone, Mirza Kara Irma sat for some time in deep thought. Then he drew his n-ruing materials towards him and wrote carefully in the Persian tongue • letter of directions to Setenet's. He addressed it to a small hotel at Marseilles, where he knew his servant would wait for the arrival of the ship Amaryllis. His letter was brief and emphatic. It gave directions which would have caused Mary's hevrt to shudder if she but knew them. Was he not a great Eastern potentate, cruelly revengeful, re- gardless of life. Mary had, by her own doing, placed herself as a tool in his hands. He smiled as he thought how, and in what fashion, she would bear the consequences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>(All nrritfl,</head>
        <p>(All nrritfl,</p>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</p>
        <p>Hy L. T. MEADE,</p>
        <p>Atiihnt ol "The Rebellien &lt;I La_ Carrington." " A</p>
        <p>Sweet Girl Graduate," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVII.</p>
        <p>TAILI THIS MONEY' AND 00.</p>
        <p>No untoward events had ever yet been known to disturb the calm of the great Mirza Kara Yusuf. After Lonsdale's departure OD that special night, when he had sent his servant :4eleucus to Marseilles, in order ti shadow Nadir Maw..., the Mires sank quietly into a chair, folded his delicately - made hands on his knees, and gazed into the glowing tire. "The English ways." he repeated once or to ice to himself—" the English folly matched 'iy the Eastern wisdom! Ha, ha!' He laughed a low and musical laugh with- oni a vestige of anything but pure enjoyment in it. He then once again touched his silver gong. Two of his retinue appeared. "Bring supper," he said. "Your Excellency," said one of the men, your servants Sti and Ktim lime returned, and would speak with you." "Admit them," said the Mirsa. . . .</p>
        <p>The men withdrew, and a moment later the PPrltili,lB who had followed Lonsdale stood just within the portiere curtain. The Mirxa glanced at them without speaking. "Your Excellency," said Kum, advancing a few steps and making a very low salaam, "we have followed the gentleman according to your wishes. He is residing to-night at the Metropole Hotel. His bedroom is No. 84 —it looks on to the back court, it is a small, single room on the fifth floor. The English ceatleman ordered supper when he arrived at the Metropole. We gathered our informa- tion from a page boy who belongs to the hotel."</p>
        <p>"In a casual way, I hope, Su and Kum?' paid the Mimi. _ .</p>
        <p>The men grinned. They dropped their eyes. Their impassive faces quickly resumed a wooden impression. "In the Eastern way, your Excellency,' they said, and then withdrew. The Mirsa's supper was brought in with some stale. He ate sparingly, evidently deep in thought. He had just finished a frugal meal, and his servant had removed the dishes. He was alone in his apartment, which had been expressly fitted up for him in Eastern style, for he invariably carried Per- sian rugs and curtains about with him. He was smoking, and slowly turning with • spoon the contents of a thick cup of Turkish coffee, whcA the man Kum once again en- tared his presence. A lady, an English lady, to see your Excellency."</p>
        <p>"Her Ulmer said the Mims, raising his eyes slightly and looking at his servant. "She refuses to give her name; she says her business is urgent." The Ifirza thought for a moment. "Show her up," he said. "Stay with Sit between the portiere and the door.</p>
        <p>The man 'salaamed and withdrew. A moment later Mary Deepe was in the pre- s:nce of the great Mirza Kara Vomit. She came forward a few steps. The Mina, who was intensely polite when occasion de- manded, rose and bowed low. He offered a seat to the lady with the manner and gesture of one who was in the presence of a prineess. Mary accepted the seat. She looked into the inscrutable eyes, at the calm and unsmiling face. She realised that alit- had come face to face with a rock—a huge boulder. Mary was a brave woman, but in the presence of the Mirza she trembled. He looked at her again. have courage, madam," he said, state the object of this interview,—</p>
        <p>" and</p>
        <p>" I have come with news," said Mary. "Ah !" said the Miran. "News which in- terests you or me, madam?" "" " •• • " ' '</p>
        <p>"You" replied the girl. Again he looked at her from head to foot. With his panther like tread he now cross-d the room, drew back the curtain where the two Persians were biding, and said a word to them which caused them to go imme- diately to the other side of the door. He then locked the door ostentatiously and loudly, and returned with his panther movement to his own chair.</p>
        <p>it is kind of you to trouble about my affair'', madam, but you will pardon me if I ask you to be exceedingly brief. I am fatigued and would retire early to slumber. I am awaiting the pleasure of the opium pipe, madam, and if you have ever knowo what that luxury means, you can but well understand my feelings of impatience to transport myself to that land of shadowy joys."</p>
        <p>Mirza spoke with extreme slowness, and with that clear, bell-like enunciation which had also characterised the speech of Nadir Jtilaver. Mary, after her first terrors, became more composed, and her fleeting courage began to return. "I have come on your business, Mirza," she said. "You will perhaps understand this when I tell you that I an. the wife of an Englishman, Arnold Deepe." The Mirza did not show a trace of any feeling on.his features. „</p>
        <p>-...:"1;P0e.eed," he said. His words were very gentle.</p>
        <p>"My husband and I have information to g:ve you with regard to the diamond known as the 'Fountain of Beauty.' That diamond lay in my hand not many hours ago." "Madam!' "Yes," repeated Mary, "not many hours ago—forty-eight hours, perhaps, not longer. 'I hrough an inadvertence, we have loot it.'</p>
        <p>"You come here to tell me this? Are you aware of your danger?" asked the Miran. "I am in no danger," said Mary, stoutly, "for I have come with my husband's know- ledge to say that he and I can help you to find it. I will speak frankly. You have an enemy, .Mirza. His name is Nadir Julaver.</p>
        <p>My husband and I were on his side; we mew f/oftVV r to yes. We can help you to recover the diamond from Nadir Julaver before he enters the Shah's palace at Teheran." The Mirxa did not speak at all for a few minutes. Then he rose and began again his walk up and down the room. he had done so an hour and a half ago in Lonedale's pre- sence. Once more his hands were clamped behind his back, and Mary saw the glitter- ing jewels, and noticed the sinewy strength of the hands themselves. After a pause she said: "I am prepared with a scheme which I can unfold to your Excellency." On hearing these words, the Mires COME and stood within a foot of the girl. "Speak !" he said. "Nadir Julaver is on his way to Tehe.l ran," said Mary. "He sailed yesterday morning in the merchant ship Amaryllis. He will reach Marseilles in five days time. I propose to meet him there, and go on board ote vessel, and before the ship reaches the other aide to obtain the diamond for myself. I will bring it back to you, great Mires." "Have you ever met Nadir Julaver?" asked the Alm's. "My husband has seen him, but I do not know him." H. has never laid eyes on lout" "Never."</p>
        <p>look at you." The Mira&amp; looked the alight young woman all over.</p>
        <p>" You are like many other Enghob women," he raill---"small, of no reputation, and of no beauty."</p>
        <p>Mary winced, tnt held her ground. Pre- sently she tapped her forehead. "I have intelligence, " she said. " he answered ; " you have craft. I can see that in your. ayes."</p>
        <p>7Y;;;;-e-he—re'Plied:ju-n-abashed. You think you can get me the diamond!'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CHAPTER XVIII. I HAVE THOUGHT OF A PLAN,</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XVIII. I HAVE THOUGHT OF A PLAN,</p>
        <p>Hitherto in all her life Rebecca Henshall had been carefully dealt with, kindly nur- tured, looked after, petted, loved. All of a sudden, after a few brief days, she found herself deprived of her father. At the same time she was burdened with a secret of some mag.nitude, and was more or less in the hands of a clever, wicked, and designing woman. Then Gerard Lonsdale litld ap- peared on the scene. The diamond, with its burden of misery, had been given to him. Be bad been to see her. He had told her that the diamond was restored to Mirza Kara Yusuf. Both these young people had owned in their hearts to a feeling of relief and re- joicing. They were of the sort of folk who do not put money first. They both had enough for their requirements. Great wealth held out no temptations whatever to them. But friendship, kindliness, the society of those they cared for, could and would make sunshine in their lives. The fact was, that each WPS mutually attracted to the other; each read in the eyes of the other sincerity, fortitude, and a certain unworldliness which is snore or less rare in the present day.</p>
        <p>There was a side, however, of Ifebecca'a character which had hitherto not been touched. She had inherited it from her father who was as brave a man as could be found. She was essentially faithful, and where those she loved were concerned, knew no fear. She would not whisper to her own heart that she had any real feeling of love for Gerard Lonsdale. Nevertheless, she re- garded him as her friend. She acknowleged to herself that he might possibly become a very groat friend. and he rejoiced in the fact that her father had taken a great lik- ing to this young man. Marv's letter, which eke read at first with only dust understanding, then again with a sense of the wildest indignation, awoke within Rebecca's heart such a tumult of emo- tion as she had never before experienced. Rage, horror, a determination to save an innoeent man from unknown peril visited her with such force that for a time she was scarcely mistress of herself. She— e?.e—had brought Gerard Lonsdale into this cruel and terrible position ; unwittingly—oh, yes, un- wittingly. She had been a victim; he was a victim. Oh, how wicked was the world! What a terrible thing it was that her dear brave father was dead! .</p>
        <p>• She quickly recovered herself, putting aside all outward semblance of emotion. The danger to Lonsdale was imminent. He was in the hands of the worst foe in the world— an Eastern enemy, who was now in London, and who would certainly revenge himself on 'him when he discovered that the stone was false. Rebecca left the drawing-room, ran down- stairs, and put on her out-door things. She was met when she came down by Lucy. I "It is getting late, ma'am," said die girl. "Do you wish dinner postponed, or will you be long out" "I cannot exactly tell when I shall return. Lucy," said Rebecca. "Tell Cook to put the dinner back. Don't keep use row,:l</p>
        <p>"But shall I not whistle for a hansom for ron,.ma'!un%." .</p>
        <p>'No, thank you; I am going to walk." Lucy opened the door for her young tress with seine inward trepidation. "I don't like it," she said, when she went down to Cook. "Our Minh; looks very queer. find why should one like her go out all by hereelf in the dark and not so much as 'are a 'ansom to take her where she wants to go? You're to keep back the dinner, 'Arriet. Our minis will be in—she don't rightly know when."</p>
        <p>"She's took with love for that young man. to my way o' thinking," said Harriet. "You leave her alone, Lucy. We're all alike when we're in love—not, so to speak, responsible. She don't want us to interfere, that's pain. Oh. I'll keep the dinner; there ain't no trouble about that." _ _</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, Rebecca walked down the itreet. At a corner she hailed an omnibus and got insid . self opposite the Metropole Hotel. She ap- proached and entered the great building She inquired at once if Mr. Lonsdale were in There was a dark, silent looking man, wear ing the livery of the hotel, who came up to her when she spoke. "I will inquire, madam," he said. Rebecca looked at his face, felt a queer sense of being puzzled, but told him curtly to get her the information she required. The man withdrew to ring Lonsdale up on the telephone. He presently came back and said respectfully to Rebecca that the gentle- man was in, and wished to know his visitor's name. .</p>
        <p>'Pleare Ray ' Miss Henahall,' and that I should like to speak to him," cold Rebecca. The man again communicated with Lous- dale on the telephone, and announced pre iently to Rebecca that the gentleman would be down in a minute. Lonsdale came down moAt directly. He approached Rebecca eagerly. His pleasant face smiled at her. She made a brave effort and smiled back al him. . . •</p>
        <p>"I want particularly to see you," she said. "Will you come out with me for a little?'</p>
        <p>"Would you not prefer to see me here in a private room?" "No: I would rather come out with you,' said Rebecca. . .</p>
        <p>She spoke in a very low voice. The dark servant had removed to a little distance. The two went out through the spring doors. The moment they did so the servant in the hotel livery put on an overcoat and a slouch hat over his eyes and followed them, but not too closely. They walked very slowly. They turned into Trafalgar-square; their steps led them past the National Gallery. Then they went up St. Martin's-lane, and presentiv up Shaftesbury-avenue. The dark man in the Illetropole livery hovered behind, but not ane..alk te. hoar what they wore saTitta (To be cobtatimi).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>The first of the annual harvest thanksgiving services in this locality trick place on Sunday last. Originating among Churchfolk in the West Coubtly something over three score years ago, these ci lebi a- I firma are now alintst universal. So far as I have been able to ascertain, the custom, now general with churches and chapels throughout the land, began with a conmon thanksgiving mdered by Queen Victoria about midway in the last century. Then it was that people first at this season decorated churches with the fruits and finwrra of the earth in token of gratitude, ai.d the practice has coatinved to grow and I extend until there are very few exceptions indeed. NOVELTIES—MORE OR LESS DECORATIVE. . .</p>
        <p>At a certain London church in the neighbourhood of Billingsgate, fish enters freely into the scheme of decoration—and so it is at various fishing ports. In some colliery districts, coal has actually been employed for the like purr) se, and cotton has been prominent on such occasions in some p.irts of Lane- shire. Once open a time I dropped a line to an old clergyman c f my acquaintance requesting 10 be favoured with a few pait,culars respecting an matt of the kind in question sod his reply was t the irlffect that the collections were so much, but that he did not allow his church to be" turned i“to a cabbage show." After all, our places of worship never to .k so beautiful as a hen ornamented u lilt the loveliist of Nature's creations— and by that, of course, I do not mean coal, or even cabl ages. 1 imagine that coin- paratively few of the pie sent genenition would care fr a harvest festival without a decorated church, therefore I venture to reproduce here a relevant hint or two which have come my way in the. course of years.</p>
        <p>USEFUL SUGGESTIONS, Even if only fruit and flowers he used, these must be skilfully bestowed in order to be effective. It has been pointed out - and, indeed, is as plain as the proverbial pikestaff - that much of the success of any kind of floral decorations must always depend upon the taste, skill, and knowledge of those who arrange matters. ghat a rerson s ith an art training is much more likely to group colours ard shades pleasingly than one who does not ham en to ',tsar.. such know- ledge goes *ithout saying. To the primary colours, one notes, they of the former class would bring the complimentary ones in due proportion. Again, in the matter of light and shade, having secured their ' principal high light, they could seek to establish the secondary ones properly. Aid possibly they might he able to bring the tout eneembie of light and-shade 'under Sir Jcshua Reynolds' gelden rule, chich, he maintained, was the base of every successful attempt at pictorial effect—a quarter intense a quarter intense dark, and the rest mezzo-tint. A lady writer whom I am happy to reckon among my friends pointed out a year or two ago that as a rule bold des.gns look better than a number of small details, and it is a mistake to allow anything that tends to cant of dignity, such as pumpkine, baskets of apples, tte. If things like there are tffered they should be kept well in the background, and never suffered to obtrude themselves. I have seen a font most beautifully decorated with nu thing but green trails and single white Japanese anemones (continued my authority on the occasion referred to), the latter stuck upright in sand (which was hidden by greenery) all round the base, and also round the top. If the interior is not required for use, flowers look very pretty within it. For pulpits and altar rails nothing looks so well as corn, small sun- flowers, and chrysanthemums, arranged to follow the designs of the panels; and, by the way, nails should nevi r I e hal:mend into church wood carving, panels, or stone work it it is at all possible to avoid doing no. Many fine old screens and panels have been lunch injured by this careless practice, and it is generally quite possible to manage with wire. If nails must be used, they should be bidden in dark corners. I dare- say that almcst any Warrington chapel keeper will bear me cut when 1 say that the young people who are so ready to drive nails into the woodwork at these times too often go away and forget all about the matter in a day or two, and leave him to undo as best he may the mischief they have a remelt through want of thought. 0 MEMORY!</p>
        <p>' However, if I continue in this attain, these same young folk will condemn me for an old fogey. Alas, alas : a thousand times alas ! and one more alas on to that, I am beginning to suspect myself in that respect —al d not without reason. '1 he illustratir n of Cleopttra's needle in last week's " Examiner" reminded me of the rime when the present site of that famous monolith was alternatel7 a mudbank and water. As a youngster in London I heed successively at Manley Place, Kensington, and in Fountain Court, Stilted, and the Thames embankment had not then come into existence, though men were at work pre- paring for the foundati, ns. Facts concerning my own career cannot interest readers in the least, but 1 thought it worth while just to note that the now so familiar thoroughfare in prestit.n and the mire important of the buildings abutting thereupon have sprung up in the course of the last -10 to 50 years. " HOWLERS. "</p>
        <p>The further examples appearing in last week's " Notes and Notions" reminded we of a collection of " Bowlers" which I Once made. Several of them wete unquestionably genuine, others were obviously maeufactured ; most of them were equal to the samples reprinted in these columns on Saturday. I always read with interest anything of the tort, On the very day on a hich those now particularly referred to saw the light at Warrington, I came across the follow- ing Mute, ingloriotis Throe are epitaphs used by a writer who was envious of Milien's -being Poet Orient." " Magna Charts said</p>
        <p>that the King had no right to bring soldiers into a lady's use and tell her to mind them." " Wolsey saved his life by dying on tLe way from York to Loudon." "Tennyson wrote a poem called 'Grave's Energy.'" " The Deserted Traveller' it the most famous of Goldsmith's poems." "1 he gods of the Indians ale chiefly Malminmed sod Buddha, and in their spare time they do lots of carving." Those a my readers who recognise old friends among these will perhaps forgive my thus troubling them; to others I do not one any apology, THE MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>The recurrence of he annual report to Ore Museum Committee sent me erred' to a back number of the " Nineteenth Century," in which, writing on "The Edueseive Value of Museums," Sir Walter Giebey sum steel the collection of objects of local interest, and taking echool children to see them. I am glad to think these heave long been acted upon in Warrington ; but some of Sir Walter's other observatione may be worth noeng here. Among other things lee urged that the local museum should be modestly local in its aims. It should seek to acquire collections of articles of local interest, natural, antiquarian, industeial, and artistic. ancient Ilcman remailes, and natural history specimens obtained in the neighbour- hood possess stronger interest when shown on the spot than when sent to acme distant city, and they are appropriate to the local museum. Examples of local i edustriee, ironwork, wood-carvirg, lace, or what Lot, displayed in proper chrorological order, possess both interest and ntiliey—an interest and utility which must increase as time passes, when no many of our old local industries hove fallen into decay. Another matter to which inch museums may most profitably devote themselves is the collection of old-time domestic eppliarces and utensils. These vary to a ecinarkable degree, in different ports of England, in material and shape. Take so common-place an item as the jug; an r xpert in these articles can tell at once in what district it was made, and assign to it the permed of its making In some parts of the country leather jugs were in regular use; in others, odes vessels built up in pieces bound together with hoops. The kcal pottery of England is a study in itself. Then ttere are those appliences %bleb were common to all the country, but are not for that reason less seen worth preservation in the museum of the county where they have been found. Some of these things, once familiar in every cottage, are gone so long and so completely out of use that few persons living know what they are - just as, I think, few realise that the great Metropolitan engineering and building scheme to which I have previously alluded has been conceived and hies material- ised within living memory. After all, P.M pleasant to reflect that Mr. Charles •Madeley and his committee have rare still and sound judgment in such matters, and are largely guided by the very principles and considerations here set forth. METHODISM.</p>
        <p>The appearance of the newly.arrived Wesleyan Ministers iu their respective pulpits on Sunder, and the circuit we!mane Meeting in Bold-street on Thurs- day eve' ing are my excuse for a a few remark', in relation to the founder of their order. Ss me critics' 'of John Wesley have said, aa many now-a-days object to General Booth, that he was an autocrat. The reply is that it is too severe a word, though tho re is to doubt that Wesley was, a Booth is, a b rn orgabiser, and Mat the sucoese of loin organisation, 1113 is now the case with the Salvation Army, rested upon obedience to orders. Here and this is my main point for the moment —are three or four of Weslm's many rules for Lis preachers Be dligett. Never be unemployed a moment. Never be triflingly empl. yed. Never while away time ; teither spend any mere time at any place than is strictly necessary. Let your mo to be, " Holiness to the Lord." Av id all lightness, jesting, and foo link talkicg. Converse sparingly and cautiously with women, rarticolarly with young women. Do not affect tl.e genii man. You bare no mere to do with this character than with that of a dancing-maater. A preacher ti the Gospel is the servant (fall. You have nothiog to do but to save souls. Therefore spend and be spent in this work. . . . And remember, a Methodist preacher is to mind every point, great aod small, in the Methodist discipline ! Therefore, yen will need all the sense you Love, and to have all your wits about you !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The Ilitipodrorne was racked from door to ceiling on Monday when Tom Foy made his debut, and crowded " houses " have been the rule each night during the week. Rarely has such enthusiasm been mat.ifested as there was the whole of the time the great little c mtdian was on the stage. His entry, mounted atoll a very clean and intelligent looking tioLkey was the signal for thundi re of applause, and Foy hardly needed to ejaculate " Cum." lie taken the lead in a very funny sketch "The First of April," and suffers matrimonial adventures which are quite out of the common. Tbe little man is "on " slot( St the whole time, and hi.; at tistic drolleries were a treat after the elephantihe humour of certain imitators who have occupied the boards at different times. Foy's Company was also quite accomplished and none perforce ed bet ter than the donkey. 'hhere is a lot of humour in the bill this week, and "My Fancy's " wonderfully clever dancing makes a nice contrast to the more mirthful turns. One cannot laugh for ever. Then, too, there is Jimmy Caravan, ihe by who has risen from the ranks of the Liverpool news-bop, to the ix sition of pianist and composer. He acquitted himself well, but would look better in evening di ens than the Eton suit which he affects some- what Mt ffectively. Tom Got t, the Yorkshire comedian, suffered little from employing the same dialect as Foy, and Iris funny songs and serio-comic recitative were enthusiastically applauded. lie is a genuine humotist. Rose and Challoner present &amp;comedy duo in the etrarige roles of ballad singer and railway porter. Theirs is one of those turns in which music seems to be merely a vehicle for low comedy. Edna Payne presents impersonations of Vesta Tilley, Harry Lauder, and Mark Sheridan, which are somewhat incongrumis through want of makr-up. Tom Lloyd foots the bill with an excess of make-up, and a song about the sausage and the saveloy which left one unmoved by mirth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>40 TEARS WITH POISONED SORES.</head>
        <p>40 TEARS WITH POISONED SORES.</p>
        <p>Crippled Widow's Splendid Cure by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Mrs. M. B. Smith, a widow, living at 12, Osborn Road, Harpurbev, Manchester, has had a wonderful experience of Zam-Buk's power to heal the most obstinate and painful sores. "As for back as 1870," said Mrs. Smith, "I got three severe kicks on my right leg that fairly crippled Me. I remember that n.v stocking stuck in the wounds, and I had to bathe my leg well before I could get my stocking off. Soule of the dye evidently got into the sore places and set up blood-poison. Por nine weeks I was unable to get upstairs to bed. "The poison in my leg travelled clown to my feet, which got very swollen and painful. To my dismay a sore broke out on the side of my foot. This got wider and deeper, and made my foot so painful and inflamed that I couldn't bear a boot on. "1 do a lot of machining, and I found my Dore foot and leg so great a handicap that I often had to give up in deafen-. During the fors!' years illy leg and foot were ulcerated, I tried ointments and salves of all kinds, besides home-made remedies, but the sores defied them all and got bigger and deeper. "Hearing '/.am-Buk highly spoken of, caused me to try the balm, and the first box showed me how different Zam-Buk is from mere oinimenut. I got more relief from that box of Zam-Buk than I had known for years. As I persevered with my ' new friend ' the swelling and inflammation of my foot and leg went down, and I sae able to walk comfortably. The ulcers gradually healed up, after Zaus-Bul: had cleared array all effects of the poison and new healthy skin finally grew over the old sore Aces. " I can now work my machine in comfort, and can walk as easily as ever, though lam well past seventy. Zam-Buk has been a great_bleasing in my olil-age."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ST. HaLRNs WORTH Y'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>ST. HaLRNs WORTH Y'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Mr..larnes Lewis, a well-known St. Helens gentle- man, panned away on Tueeday at his residence, Brook. land., Ecc'estop, at the advanced age of neventy•tbreeo The deceased gentleman WWI 006 of the first director of the St. Helens Tramway Company and also of the old Burial Board. He waa a native of Liverpool, but become apprentice to the late Mr. Blinkhoro, of 03arob- street, St. Helens, about and as a young man toot over the business of grocer and wine and sprit dealer, carrying it on until about twelve years ago, when be retired. Mr. Lewis was at one time churchwarden at the pariah cherch, sod he bad held s similar office at Christ Cborch, Ecotone.. He was of a quiet and retiring Imposition. but was most highly respected an one of the oldest residents of the town. Ile had been in failing health for about five years, and passed peace- fully away at 3 :30 on Tuesday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
