      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</p>
        <p>THE TARIFF PICNIC.</p>
        <p>Another Challenge.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Women's Tariff Reform League, an open-air meeting was held at Town's End on Saturday evening. Lady Greene!l presided and among there present in the van were Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.0., Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Mr. Hargreaves (Conservative candidate for Bedford), Alderman Smethurst and Mr. Herbert Ilayes. There was a largo audience. Owing to the excessive somber of children the meeting was extremely noimy and even when the speakers were addressing the meeting they frequently indulged in dilties in rake of Lady Greenall and Tariff "Reform."</p>
        <p>Lady Greened! Etlid it was necessary that everyone should thoroughly study the proposals of the present Government, and if they aid so they would sweep the Radissla on une side. Sha did not think the electors thoroughly realised the reeponeibility that rested upon them. They must remember that, by their VAIM they could eldest raise the country to great prosperity or bring it to ruin. They could not say tbat, they were having a good time under a Free Trade G rvernmenr, and they should reverse that slate of affairs and return e Government which by keeping trade in England would give them prosperity. (Applause ) They should put the prosperity of MI chimera before such things as Borne Mule and the Lords' Veto Bill. Tar iff " Itel " wean thing which touched all classes, and it was a thing they intended to fight for until they won. (Applause.) I Mr. HARUKKAVY.S said tuat dozing the short time Mr. Smith had been in the !louse of Commons he had eons position for himself, end can regarded by the leaders of the Tory party as one of their corning men. (Applause.) Continuing, he said that we needed a tisersl system which could raise sages w thout the agency of the Government and one which would relieve the distress which hid made itself felt recently in almost every industry in England. Free Trade was introduced in order to keep down prices try keeping down wages and was it any wonder that we bad such industrial upteavals ? With regard to</p>
        <p>MOM R RUI,6 FOR -IRELAND the speaker said the fact that the Liberals boasted that they had sacrificed 20 }ears of office for their principlts did not look as lithe people of England had wanted to give a mandate for Home Rule. When Mr. Gladstone introduced his Lill he said, " We want justice for 1 relaud,which lies been oppressed, and England Me been guilt) of neglecting her. " But all that had changed. la recent years act bad pledgad our cridit for L 200,000,060, in order that !retard might Imre &amp;fait land aystem ;we bad aatabliabed peesaut proprietor.bips, en ' had given them old-age pensions (A Voice Who has ; and we were going to give them an Insurance Bill. In ell those wnys, he said, we had more than r.deemed ourselves and the case of justice for Ireland had gone by the board. The English people wanted justice for everybody, but we were not going to be bullied by King John Redmond. Even if the people of Ireland were in favour of Home Rule and the people of the British Isles were not then it should nut be granted. (Applause.) Mr. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., said that (hut meeting was held under the auspices of the greatest women's political organisation in the world, judged either by enthusiasm or numbers. And</p>
        <p>W RRINOTON LOVED 711RIR ratsinm•</p>
        <p>(Leidy Gres 111111111111 l of the handful of traducers, whom they came soros. on the other political plst- form " Before I pass on to matters strictly political," continued Mr. Smith " I want to say one word about the great picnic to Frodsham which took place a fax weeks ago under the auspices of the Women's Tariff ' Reform' League. I feel at is my right to say • word because the Radical party in IVarrington hare seen lit to</p>
