      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DENOUNCE TOR PICNIC</head>
        <p>DENOUNCE TOR PICNIC</p>
        <p>and the ih'omen's Tariff 'Reform' League. Became they are women they felt they had a warm corner in their 'hearts for the little children and I should just like to know where we stand in lA'arrington if it as the view of the Radical patty that because the Ter.li ' Reform ' women helped those who have fallen by the way- side—because you are a 'tariff Reformer you cannot bring one day's happiness into the Irvin of those poor, miserable, thousands of children whom we have in our midst in every great town in England. If this is the view of the Radical party I say there is not a working man In Warrington who would not strike a adore at them when the next election came round. It is one of the great principles of English charity—it is greater than the principles of English charity, it is one of the first things which you ought to find in the bottom of every true heart—that it you have the means and the opportunity of belpiarg those whose lives are equalid, whether Tariff Reformer or Home Ruler, for Uod's wake do what you can to brighten their live; and homes. (hood applause). I can only say that if the Radical party continue to denounce the picnic I will chrerfully face them on any platform in Warrington and I will ask any one of the leaders of the party to attrmpt to justify their denunciation of that wonderful picnic. " (Applause) Ile w ished to any a word about the recent industrial opheavals that had taken pleas through the recent strike. Ile felt it his duty, as repreeenting a working- clans constimency, .o refer to it. Whilst there were many hundreds of meu in Werriogton out under this Feee Trade Government and many works were closed for •few days he was glad to find there had not been one d sg race(ul incident such as bed occurred in Liverpool. Applause.) He honestly believed that the disorders and riots which took place in the streets of Liverpool were not crested by the men on strike for better wages but they were calmed by the hooligan clues which were to be found in every city in the laod. If a Chancellor of the Exch.-pier went about the country delivering , speeches mach am the roe at Liewhouse then he (hit. Smith) sail he was offering inducement to the people to take the law into their own bands and to destroy the King's peace. Ile took the view that in many &lt;warm where the men saruck for higher wages that they were right. Ile tad not the slightest hesita- tion in ,o,yiiig w lib regard to the shipping strike that the men were underpaid. They were not getting • living wage for a day's labour but when they CM* Oat</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>10. DiTrtICT /4_,___T</head>
        <p>10. DiTrtICT /4_,___T</p>
        <p>_ Winwick. CONI. RE, kTIDNAL CHRINHAN SERVI( E BAND.—lhe meeting held on Monday evening last in the school FIRE BRIG ADE.—The Winwicic Asylum Fire Brigade vestry was very well attended. Mr. .1. H. Holt gave an Rave a good aociount of themselves on Saturday, at the address from the text" For the kingdoms of this world meeting of the National Fire Brigade Union (North are not to become the kingdoms of the Lord and His Western District) which was held at Crewe. The team, Christ." Ile said that even in the worst type of human consisting of chief fireman H E Parker, Messrs E beings there was some of the Holy Spirit discernible, Sidgreaves, A Dominate, J Woods and G Demon, won which pointed to the Kingdom coming nearer every the handsome silver cup presented by Lord Haddlngton. day, hat there still remained a great deal to he access- president of the Union, for the hydrant chill, also £2 plished. Mien A. Miller presided and the meeting enn- ead • gold medal. Messrs Greenall, Whitley and Co's. cluded with several short prayers. team (St. Helens), won the shield for the hose cart drill, after being runners-up for the last three years Culeheth. when the Asylum were the winners 'ids time. INDEPENDENT MI I HODISTS.—The usn al monthly Risley. P.S.A. in connection with this place was held in the chapel on Sunday afternoon last presided over by Mr. INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH, FEARNHIAD.— George Fenton. Short addresses were given to the An open Sunday was observed on Sunday in the ScndaY children by Messrs. G. Fenton and W. Kenyon. Solos school for the first time. A very interesting programme were smug by Miss Runcorn and Mr. E. Barton and • was gone through. Mr. Joseph Bate occupied the chair. trio by Miss Runcorn, Mr. E. Berton end Mr. W. and in the course of his address encouraged the juvenile Kenyon. There was a good attendance and the meeting portion of his audience to enter into the active work throughout was very enjoyable. of Church life as early aa possible, as it was the means of not only benefltting them, but it proved a stimulus Grappenhall. to the elders. Recitations and solos were rendered by PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. the scholars. Mr. W. Durant gave a short address, bin , subject being " A Sunbeam." The monthly meeting of the Grappenhall Parish Council was held on Wednesday evening last. There Thelwall. were present :—Mesirs. P. It. It iberti (chairman), A. . , Lister-K ' aye F. Borrows, J. Carter, A. Radflrth, F. Fox, DROWNINt. VATALITV.—Mr. Ridgway (coroner) ne,m and CherlesH. bolt (clerk). an inquest at Thelwall on Tuesday evening, touching The Clerk read a letter from the County Surveyor, the death of William Green, aged 5/. years, son of po ing that the request to tar-spray the Chester William Green,a ferryman employed on the Manchester purporting t d rireat season, wluld hive the attention of his corn- Ship Canal. The child, also resided at Ferry Cottage, mittee. Thelwall, was drowned in the Ship Cartel on Sunday , evening. The father stated that he last now his one ' alive at 2 p.m. on Sunday when he left home to go to the Sunday school. About 5.50 p.m. he heard his daughter, who was four years old, crying, end he asked her where her brother was and she told him he had fallen into the water. He at once undressed and dived into the canal but could not fled him. The body was recovered by grappling about 9.30 p.m. A piece of string stub a piece of wood attached to it (w ith which deceased bad been playing) was twisted round the child's left leg.—Evidence baying been given by Albert Bennett, a ferryman, and Edward John Murray, a lock gate keeper, a verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>BAD DR.!I•4'S:</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated th'aZT3eVialirei no reply from the nuisance inspector ,e the cpen ditches In the parish. Mr. Ridfirth mid that the hot weather we bad been experiencing had caused these ditches to be worse, and very dangerous to health. The Chairman : It is possible that n othing can really be done, but at the same time I will write personally on the matter. Mr. Burrows: The best thing to do is to preen on with the sewerage scheme. At present the Council are being delayed for taunt of loot Mr. Rai:Birth recommended that the different: Parish Councils press the question forward.</p>
        <p>Croft. • FLON, en SHOW.—The Croft Horticultural Society Society held its 3th annual show of flowers, fruit and vegetables on Saturday. The exhibits were attractively staged in the Council school and, considering the exceptionally dry season, they were very creditable. The Judges for the vegetables were Messrs 0 March and H E Southern, and for the plants, flowers and fruit Mr. Clare, of High Peak, Kenyon. The show was opened at four o'clock by Captain Withington, th re being a large and representative attendance, including the Rector (Rev. H. I. Wadeson), the Rev. Webster Hall, Lowton ; Dr B Brearley, Mr A Withington, Ac. Captain With ington said he had opened the show on many occasions and each year he theught it looked better. The Rev. H. T. Wadeson, in moving a vote of thanks to Captain Witbington, said the Captain was always ready to do a kindly action and they in Croft were pleased to see him on all occasions, lie also proposed a vote of thanks to the secretary, committre and judges. The Rev. Webster Hall seconded and complimented Mr. Cawtboroe, the secretary, on his SUCCORS in securing first pries for the premier onion, after 20 years' unsuc- cessful efforts. The motion having been carried, Mr. Cawthorne in reply said the efforts of the scholars in the cultivation of the school garden had met with success, a number of their exhibits having secured prizes. The scholars took great interest in it, and he hoped the knowledge gained might be useful to them in after years. (Applause). Mr. G. Marsh also briefly replied on behalf of the judges.</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBER FLECIED. The Chairman sta.^d that owing to the resignation of Mr. Leach a new member would have tu be elected. Mr. Fox: I have pleasure in nominating Dr. C. W. Hott. The Chairman supported the nomination and mid that Dr. Hutt was highly qualified. Through him they would have valuable assistance. Mr Carter seconded the nomination which was unanimously o arried,</p>
        <p>TI .SSVIGIIR!DGF.</p>
        <p>Mr. Burrows complained that the conditions of work- ing the sating bridge at night time were an inconveni- ence to the public, after 5.30 p.m. they worked the bridge with one man. The Chairman : I be lieve they bave statuary powers to do so, Rod they are within their legal rights in so working the bridge. Mr. Kaye Although they work the actual move- ments of the bridge in four minutes, they can I believe allow it to remain open as long an they think it necessary. The best thing to do is to ask the Rural District Council to make an application hr better C an- ditions. This course was agreed tu.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>PADG ATE DISTRICT NrnslNG ASSOCIATION.—The Padgate Parish Magazine says :—Very regretfully the committee have to announce that Nurse Montgomery has been obliged, owing to continued ill-health, to send in her resignation and will not be able to return to Padgate as district name. The following extract from her letter will show how regretfully she has taken this step :—" As long as I live I shall remember my sojourn with you as the happiest time in my life, and the kindness everyone without exception has shown to me personally and the sympathy and encouragement I received in my work." The committee hope soon to have anew resident nurse. In the meantime Nurse Molyneaux, 12 Elmwood-avenue, Padgate-lane, a fully trained nurse, is willing to continue to fill the gap as long as we wont her. We ate very grateful to her for serving us so efficiently during the long period of Nurse Montgomery's illness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>WE,LEY GI:MD.—The monthly devotioral meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday evening last, whoa a very interesting paper on Citizenship was given by the Rev. J. Gunnell. There was a very good attendance and the lecture was a source of interest and</p>
        <p>enjoyment to all who were privileged to hear It. PAD(' ATE WESLEYANS.-7he harvest festival services In connection with this place were held on Sunday last. The morning preacher was the Rev Gunnell who &amp;Iva a very inspiring address from the words What mean ye by this service." Mr Gunnell very ably appropriated the words to the Harvest thanksgiving service and shewed in many ways how much we were indebted to Almighty God for the numerotia benefits which we enjoyed. In the afternoon a visit ASS paid ta the Sun- day School by the Rev E Morgan the newly appointed minister and his address to the children was listened to with great attention. Mr. Morgan asked the children to go home to their parents and give them his love (not to be content with kind regards Ac.) and invite them to come to the evening service. Judging by the large congregations which assembled at the even- ing service to bear the Rev. E. Morgan, it is evident that the children must have carried out the request and invited the parents to come to the evening service.— Mr. Morgan gave a very instructive address from the words " Menai be the Lord, who daily loadeth us with benefits." The preacher made a special apreal to the younger portion of the congregation to join the Church, and his first sermon made a great impression. Mr. A. Doraville presided at the organ. THE RECTOR'S SIMPLE LIFE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 0. B. Thurston, writing in the' Lymm Parish Magazine " from Tysse, Sondflor, Bergen, under date August 25th, says : It is not an uncommon thing in these days of highly organised civilization to read snicks and paragraphs extolling and commend- ing what is called by writers tho simple life, an existence in which one lives naturally and where one's nerves sre not racked with the discharge of conflicting,</p>
        <p>disturbing and unnecessary duties. It is comparatively; easy to write of such things, hot to fulfil them is a very different matter, as we must all be victims of the socaetyaruong a hich we live and over whosecomplicated systems we have little if any control. But it is pleasant and profitable to get away from these necessary worries and troubles for a time, and in the quiet holiday I have been enjoying for the past three weeks in this primitive land my only regret has been that pm could not all have the privilege of shar- ing its benefits with me. It would takes more eloquent and practised pen than I can lay claim to to set out the scenes among which I have been living, with a power stall adequate to what might be described. Try and imagine, then, whet it would mean to li est a distance of some hundred miles from a railway station cut off from the outer world altorether were it not for the occasional visit from a steamboat which intrudes on one's privacv with letters, newspapers sad other com- modities of life three times a week. I must confess that one is not so completely enamoured of this detachment as not to look forward to and to welcome this link with the greater world, and especially have I felt this this year with so many exciting and disturbing events going ou in England. When one uses the worl primitive to describe this country it must not be understood to imply a want of knowledge of social customs or a want of intelligence of those higher moral matters which are essential in every civilized and well organized community, for I should be doing the people of Norway a grave injustice if an impression were given that they were one whit behind the peoples of any other country in the world, for as a matter of fact they are well above the average. Nature has hes'owed some of be! most favoured gifts on this .</p>
        <p>land. The scenery which ban no great an effect upon 4 the mind is grand beyond description. Mountains and wide stretches of aster are present everywhere in full abundance, giving the impression on every band of the strength and beneflcenca of nature. The prospeota are bold, wide, and far, the land is well wooded with onks, limes, elms, birches, and spruce a ith a largo variety of nmaller trees and shrubs ouch as alder, mountain ash, a.-•^s. and juniper, freely interapersed with a multitude of oi...hne of willows, bilberries, whortleberriee. wild strawberries &amp;c. One need never starve or feel faint for want of food in an excursion over the Norwegian bills, as there in always to hand plenty of ripe fruit to assuage both bringer and thirst. The Norwegian peassnt's life is a busy, happy and healthy one. they well understtnd how to enjoy a holidsy when a wedding or other festivity taken place in their neighbourhood. Excursions out to sea among the outlying islands are eagerly appreciated, and as intoxicating drinks are dis- allowed by law, their pleasures have a more innocent tone than we are accustomed to sometimes in England. The cost of living is extremely small, Ile bread and butter and coffee for breakfast, oatmeal porridge for the mid-lay meal, herring and other salt fish with potatoes at tea time, and lit bread, butter, and cheese, for supper. There is a good eopply of ministern schoolmasters and doctors, all of whom are appointed to their various districts by the Government, the people by these mean. in every part being enabled to have the comforts and support of religion, the benefits of educa- tion, and skilled and careful attention in times of sick- ness. Communication between place and place is almost entirelzi water. -.1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>MR. Al) MRS. PARKER', SILVER WEDDING.—Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker celebrated their silver wedding on Saturday last, when a large numler of relatives and friends were preaent to offer them congratulations. They were the recipients of many valuable presents, including a pair of silver tarns from the officers and commitse of the Warriagton Liberal Associstion ; also silver vase, with pendant and bonbonietiss from the Penketh Tannery e.nployees, and a beautiful illuminated address from the members of the Penketh Wesleyan Men's Bible Clam. This presentation was made at the Men's Afternoon Class on Sunday by Mr. W. Moores, who eulogised the good Mr. Parker, as president, had done during the 27 years he had been leader. He stood before them as a pattern to others in the same social position, and he was loved by every member of the class. On behalf of himself and members he prayed God's blessingwonld rest upon him and Mrs. Parker,and hoped they would be spared to celehrate their golden wedding.—Mr. Parker, speaking with emotion, thanked them all. He wished Mrs. Parker were with them, for it was due to her good management of various things which otherwise he would have to give his time to that enabled him to take an interest in as many ontside thirga. Apart from the intrinsic value of the eresent he should always value the address.—ln moving a vote of thanks to Mr Moores for making the presentation Mr Clay said as one who had helped in the eddreas he could assure Mr. Parker the words inscribed were a true expreesion of every members feelings. Mr. Worencroft seconded and said it was Mr Parker's irfluerce in the class which had enabled him to stand where he stood today. ANNUAL TRIP.—In connection a ith the Ssulsey church, the parochial trip took place on Saturday. The party, which numbered about 80, left Bank Quay at 8.30 a.m, en route to Rhyl, which was reached at 9.30. The party travelled by two saloon carriages. In the afternoon a drive to St. Asaph was very much enjoyed. The party arrived home again at about 10 p.m. after a real good day's enjoyment. Messrs. F. T. Newbold and E. Dunbill were in charge. PENKETH BAND SUCCESS.—At the New Brighton Tower brass band contest lest Saturday the Penketh Tannery prize band took premier honours in winning the first prize, the test piece being the grand selection "The Land of the Shamrock' (by Ed. Newton). Mr. ' .1. A. Greenwood conducted, the adjudicator being Mr. Rufus Fletcher. Thirteen bands competed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED CASE OF ANTHRAX.</p>
        <p>A bullock, the property of Mrs. Ellen Griffiths, of Clifton Hall Farm, Rockaavage, was found dead on Rocksavage Marsh on Saturday. Mr. Ferguson, veterinary surgeon, of Warrington, was called in, and expressed the opinion that the animal had died from anthrax. The carcase was cremated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>AN INGENIOUS EXCUSE.</head>
        <p>AN INGENIOUS EXCUSE.</p>
        <p>For trespassing in pursuit of game, the right to shoot for which belongs to Sir Richard Brooke, four men were fined .£1 and costs each on Monday, by the Runcorn magistrates. The defendants were Thomas Worthington, Ilandley.street ; Thomas Jones, Wivern- place ; Edward Johnson, Stanley-street ; and Jonathan Stringer, Grosvenor-street. Gamekeepere gave evidence that they saw the lest three defendants on the "Spoil Bank," Astmoor. For the defence, Mr. Hough said his clients had only fired on the Marsh, where it was well known that folks went down to shoot. They thought they had found one spot on the face of the earth where they c3uld practice no as to be useful in case of invasion. MOTOR OFFENCES.</p>
        <p>At Runcon2 Sessions, on Monday, Peter Antwis, of Runcorn, was summoned for driving a "heavy motor- car" without having a licence. William Moore, the owner of the waggon, bin employer, was summoned for employing an unlioensed driver, for failing 0 have an identification plate on his trailer, and also for not having a man in the trailer in a position to work the brakes. Mr. Moore claimed that the clerk of the Cheshire , County Council had told him that the trailer needed i DO identification plate, but the magistrate's clerk said that what was meant was that the trailer did not, in itself, require registering as a motor-car. Antwis was fined 10s. 6d. and Moore 10a. in respect of each of the three offences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CRUELTY TO CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>CRUELTY TO CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, six charges of neglect and cruelty to children were heard in stleCtlision—s local record. In each case the chief fault was filth, and the revelations were most disconcerting to the Bench. In the case of Mary Ward, Nelson atreet, it was stated that the elder bo.ts refused to go to bed it night in consequence of the vermin crawling over the floor, ceiling, and bed. Mrs. Ward remarked that it was old property, end every house in the street was the name. Mr. Ockleston.—•Ciood Lord !</p>
        <p>James Moores and Phwhe Moores, of Stenhills, were each sentenced to two months hard labour ; Mary Campbell, of Shaw-street, to three months, and Sarah Cheaterton, of Stanehills, to two months. John Chesterton was discharged with a caution, the bench holding that his wife was principally to blame. There was a sad wine after the passing of the sentences, the women prisoners sobbing hysterically end clinging to their children and friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WOMAN'S ATTENIPTED SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S ATTENIPTED SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>At midnight, on Monday, John Davies, a boatman heard a splash in the Bridgewater Crawl, at Top Locks Runcorn, and espied. woman struggling in the water With the aid of a young man who was passirg he got the wcman on the bank.</p>
        <p>She appeared before the magistrates at Runcorn Police Court, on Tuesday mornin;, on a charge of attempted suicide, and turned out to be Sarah An Pickatock, of Bridgewater-street. Police-constable Crompton said he beard of the affair, and made a search for the woman is the early hours of the morning. He found her lying in her wet clothes in r n unoccupied house in Bridgewater-street. She told him her husband had locked the house against her and gone away, taking the children with him. She threatened to go to the canal again. She smelt strongly of drink. Prisoner had nothing to say, and was remanded for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Examiner Saturday S eptEmt er 9, 1911 TWO KINDS OF MEN AND WOMEN</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday S eptEmt er 9, 1911 TWO KINDS OF MEN AND WOMEN</p>
        <p>thing of this sort."—The case was remanded for seven days.</p>
        <p>Tll URSDAIT.—Before Mews. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), W. Roberts, A. .1. Jolley, W. T. Williams and</p>
        <p>5. vison. " LIKE A WILD BOAST."—Joseph Maguire, a cab dsiver residing at 2:5, Greenallatreet, was charged w th having been drunk and disorderly in Winwick-street, and with assaulting P.C. Gibbs the previous day.— The Constable said that he went to the assist- ance of P.C. Armstrong and helped to take the prisoner to the Police Station. Whilst in Golborne- street the prisoner started to kick and bite. and bit four pieces out of his (P.C. Gibbs', hand. When they reached Museum-street prisoner lay on the ground the constables having to carry him to the Police Station. Prisoner stated that he did not bite the constable, lie would like the case adjourned for the doctor to see the constable's hand as he was sure the marks were not like bits merks. The Chief Con- stable said that the prisoner was more like a will* animal than a Christian and the noise he was crestless was most disgraceful. The Chairman of the Bench said that they were determinedAs fares it was possible to paVan end to suck behaviour, and they would senteucf,the prisoner to smooth's hard labour without the entire% of a fine. THE PENALTY OF DEFIAN( Manfredi, so ice-cream vendor, 33, Winwickatreet was summoned for creating an obstruction in Sankey with an ice-cream cart. Defendant did not put in an appearance. The Chief Constable said that when the officer served the summons the defendant ieplied : "I can do seven daps as well nanny other man " and now lie has abso- lutely defied the Court.—He was fined 20, and costs with the alternative of a month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>FRID kY—Before Aldermen Forshaer (in the chair) Bolton, Pierpoint, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. S Wright, W Furness, II Thornton and G. Pilling. DES ERTest A PMIF.IIEND lliam Pitchlaw, '29, Frederick-street, was charged with deserting from the 11th Hussars stationed at Shoreham since June 24th, 1910.—P.C. Yost said that he apprehended the prisoner in Mersey-street. He first denied the charge but after- warda admitted it. The Chief Constable raid that word had been sent tothe regiment's heado tatters but he had received no reply. The case was adjourned until the arrival of the military (non end the Constable was allowed the usual fee. FACTORY PROSECUTION. —Picton Bros. were charged with employing a young person without a necessary certificate. Mr. Warren, H.M. Factory Inspector, said that the deftndants carried on the business of _ wheel- wrights. Ile visited the premises on July lath and ascertained that they were employing ft boy named Frederick Dalton, who was fifteen years of age on December 9th last year. The boy stated thlt he bad worked for tbe defendants since May, 1910. Instead of getting a certificate within fourteen days they had not got one within fourteen months. They had been pre- viously written to about employing another boy with. out a certificate. Mr. Picton said that he overlooked the matter entirely. It was through forgetfulness. Defeodants were fined 54. and costs (75..6d. in all).</p>
        <p>MATRIMONIAL THOU BL Silos Thompson wee summoned by his wife for desertion. He pleaded that his stepdaughter's impudence had compelled him to go away. The case wan adjourned for a month to see if the parties could be brought together.—William Charnley, 62, Oldham-street, was summoned by his wife Marpret Charnley, for persistent cruelty, and she asked for a separation order. The complainant stated that the defecdant had such a nasty temper that no sooner was there a word than there was a blow. Defendant stated that his mite was always going away and leaving him in charge of the children. Complainant stated that it was partly through ill-health and partly through the fact that her husband had not got regular work that she went to live with her mother for a time. The Bench adjourned the case for four weeks to see if the parties could be brought together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>LEADFRS AND THE LED.</head>
