      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>HORSE.</head>
        <p>A HORSE.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Session on Monday, a carter I named George Parr, of Latchford, Warringtan, was summoned at the Runcorn Petty Sessions, for cruelly ill-treating a horse at Runcorn on August 22nd. Defendant pleaded not guilty.—P.S. Griffiths maid that on examing the horse, which was attached to a float laden with firewood, he discovered a wound on the left shoulder about the size of half-a-crows, a scar on the I other shoulder about half-an-inch long and quarter of an I inch wide, and another scar on the near fetlock. He called defendant's attention to them and defendant said be was not aware that the horse was unfit. He examined the collar and found an old scar underneath which appeared to be rubbed a great deal. Defendant I took the horse out at witeessa request— Superintendent Ennion said he examined the horse, and thought it was not a had example of cruelty, although the horse was not fit to work.— Defendant said there had been a sore on the shoulder and consequently the horse had only been worked for three days in a fortnight. Previous to taking the horse out on August 22ad it bad not been out for six days in succession. When he discovered the sore under the collar he paid every attention to it, and had the collar padded specially. He examined the sore previous to starting out, and felt satisfied that the wound was hard and that the horse was quite capable of being worked. He would not cruelly ill-treat a horse.—He was fined 53. ANOTHER ECHO OF THE BRASS THEFTS.</p>
        <p>Th 9 case of Samuel Warburton of no settled abode, who wa.s remanded the previous week on two charges of stealing, fibs, of brass bearings and nibs. of scrap brass on August 20th and 22Ibs of brses bearings on August 21st., the property of Messrs. Monks Hall and Co., was agaid before the court on Thursdry. Mr. Rymer Young was the presiding magistrate. Prisoner pleaded guilty to both charges. The Chief Constable said a week ago that day two men who had put in a plea of guilty to stealing and receiving the above named property were c3mmitted to prison. There was little room to doubt that the present prisoner incited the man who was committed for steal- ing the property to commit the theft. itappeared that the man who stole the property has 'e 1 it t 3 the prisoner while he sold it to the prisoner Broadhurst at the rate of 2d. a pound. Broadhurst had stated that the present prisoner had worked fn r him and yet he did not know his character. He (the Chief Constable) believed that the present prisoner was ondoubtedly the worst of the three. His record included quite a number of charges tor stealing and unfortunately be 1168 know N as a notorious fowl thief, having been for years practising fowl thieving although the police had not been able to catch hid. The Chief Constable also stated that another step weighing nibs had been recovered from Broadhurst's premises bat they had not been able to find an owner for it. The Bench sentenced the prisoner to two months hard labour on each charge (four months in alit the sentences torso consecutively, the Chairman reit aching that the , prisoner's character was very bad indeed,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —Before the Mayor (Lo the chair) Alderman Smethurst, Mews. T. Locker W. Furness, S. Wright</p>
        <p>and H. Thornton. STRUCK His MOTHER WITR A BRUSH.—Robert Forber, a youth, 101, Fox-street, was charged with assaulting and beating his mother Emma Forber on Saturday tat'. His mother asked that the charge might be withdrawn. Ti the magistrates would give him another chance she was sure he would never repeat the offence. She thought his having been in prison since Saturday would be a warning to him.—ln reply to a magistrate witness stated that the prisoner hit her on the head with a brush. If he over did it again she would turn him off her bands altogether. Prisoner was ordered to pay the costs (Br. 6d.) PLAYING CARDS UNDER LAMP AT MIDNIGHT.— Robert Welgh, 6, Halifax-street, a labourer, Was charged with playing a gime of chance for money and also with assaulting P.C. Porter.—P.C. Porter stated that at 11 55 p.m. on Sunday be was on duty in Buttermarket-street and when be got to James'-street he heard the rattling of coins. He went into James'- street where he am the prisoner and three other young men round a lamp post playing cards. The prisoner refused to give his name and address and so he arrested him. Whilst taking him to the Police Station and when opposite the County Court the prisoner struck the constable and bolted up Bold-street. The constable blew his whistle and P.C. Gibson caught the prisoner.— Prisoner said he did not strike the constable intention- ally bathe only tried to break away. He was fined lOs and costs with the alternative of 14 days imprisonment. AN UNENVIABLE RECORD—John Heaton, of no settled abode, was charged with being drunk and disorderly. He pleaded guilty and said that he would sign the pledge if they would look over the matter.— The Chief Countable: He has been here 76 times hefore.—The Mayor : How many times his he promised to sign the pledge ?—The Chief Constable : 75 oat of the 76. (Laughter )—Prisoner : I promise to leave the town as well as sign the pledge —The Chief Constable : It would be a pity if such a celebrity left the town.—Prisoner MS fined sa. and costs. TUESDAY.—Before Councillor H. Roberta (in the chair) Alderman Pierpoint, Councillor C. Jackson Holmes and Messrs..l. Rymer Young and T. Locker. STREET OBSTRUCTION.—For obstructing the footpath In Rowe-street, on the 31st ult., Thomas CUMBIiII9 and Patrick Moran were each fined ss. with the alternative of seven days imprisonment, while for a similar offence in Bswsey-street, John Stanton and Alfred Beckett were similarly dealt with.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAV..—Before Mr. Cbarlfs White (in the chair, Alderman Smethurst, Cou ncillor C. Jackson Holmes , and Mr. W. T. Williams. _</p>
        <p>CII A rto ED ON SIISPICION.—CherIee Naylor, 43, Lythgoes-lane, a joiner, was charged on suspicion with stealing a smoothing plane, a brace and a rule from a yacht at Atherton's Quay.—The Chief Constable said that some time on Friday night cr Saturday morning I last, the cabin of a yacht in the river at Atherton's Quay was broken into and some property stolen. The prisoner was seen in the vicinity by a man at six o'clock on Saturday morning. Later in the day the prisoner was trying to dispose of some property in the ltodney public house, which tallied with the tools stolen from the yacht. Detective Clough visited the prisoner and when asked to give an account of the articles he stated that they were his own property. The prisoner's daughter had stated that the property did not belong to her father. The police paid a visit to the prisoner's house the previous night and there found a number of keys. Several of them had been transformed into skeleton keys and in addition a skeleton key and a little tile were found in his possession. When asked to account for the latter, he stated that the skeleton key was for the purpose of gaining access to his daughter's house, but there won no truth in this statement. He asked that the case might stand adjourned for seven daye.—The Bench acceeded to this request. PR IoONE a's OBJECTION TO A It elit Q.: D.—George Wilson. no settled abode, woo charged with attempting to break into a lock-up shop in Rylands-street, on August P2tly. The Chief Constable said that early in the morning of the day in question prisoner was seen to be acting in a very suspicious manner in Rylands-street. lie was kept under observation for a considerable time by a civilian but eventually he lest sight of him. Since that day the prisoner had not been seen but on Tuesday the police received information that he was again in town. Detective Holland visited a nomter of lodging- houses that morning and found the prisoner in one of thorn. He was taken to the Police Station but denied all knowledge of the affair and !stated that he was not . in the town on that day. As the police were 'lush's to • produce the necessary information that morning be ; asked that the case might be adjourned for a week. When asked if he had any objection to the cue being t remanded, the prisoner objected, " Is it likely "he said " that a roan of my experience and knowledge would no</p>
      </div>
