      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Corporation Audit.</head>
        <p>The Corporation Audit.</p>
        <p>Sir—The Chairman of the Finance Committee,in his real to deservedly eulogise the present auditors, no doubt unwittingly east an unfortunate reflection upon some of the work of the previous auditors. lam not at all surprised that Alderman Bennett and Councillor Wilson remonstrated against the latter portion of the remarks. In making his comparisons the Chairman overlooked the fact that in certain recent audits, owing to the unfortunate illness of one of our colleagues, the bulk of the audit fell upon the shoulders of Mr. Councillor Wilson And myself. The time stated to have bees taken up by some of these audits dots not by any means represent the extent of the time taken up, as both Councillor Wilson and myself made certain visits to other departments of the Corporation beside the Borough Treasurer's departments f which the officials themselves were not aware, in order to check certain books and stocks belonging to the Corporation. I have thought it as well to join in Alderman Bennett's and Councillor Wilson's protest in order that a false impression that may have been created in the public mind should be cleared away.—Yours truly, , E. ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>, trers iE.2 to the EDITOR; '1 '1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Mr. Harold Smith's Wire Tangle.</head>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith's Wire Tangle.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l notice that Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., has been speaking at Town's End on the Strike and Tariff' Reform and reference was made to Warrington Wire v. German Wire and the " problem re-put.' Since this controversy started in your columns. I have searched the files of your contemporary in the hope Jf getting some 'a sfaction from the Member's speech when he was supposed t r introduce the " problem," but, althrugh there appears to be a very lengthy report, I can find no reference to it. 1 wasderirous of seeing what all the bother was about but, to my auto is 3, apart from the usual tilt at the Free Traders worshipping the god of cheapness (and a really stppld question) I was not enlightened. He did not propose to task the intelligence of a Tariff Reform crowd by tvguing it further then,Lis he hoped to have an opportunity of arguing the question on the platform with Mr. Crosfield—before, presumably, an unintelligent gathering of Free-Traders.</p>
        <p>Now sir, seen.,.! that there seems t) be some uncertainty of such a debme taking place, may I be permitted to say that it it is " economically sound," according to the Smithian school of economics, to ay a Warrington firm £m mire than a German firm a contract of £3,000, it most be equally economically right to pay a Warrinicon firm £5OO, or even more, to secure the order h cally. Why siould a paltry eight per cent. be the maximum ? If Mr. Harold Smith, If P., would tell us at what percentage it would be ecomically unsound to cease favouritism, we should be better able to appreciate the " problem." For the moment, however, we cannot help thinking that he is playing another joke upon us. Another shaving stick :—Yours truly, EDMUND OSBORNE. 22, 80ld-street, Warrington, Bth Sev,raber. 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS INTERFERENCE WITH</p>
        <p>TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>A substantial start has at last been made with the Warrington Bridge works, the contractors having got a considerable quantity cf machinery and plant on the ground. It has been found necessary to enclose a considerable portion of Arpley-road m order to get down to the west abutments of the proposed new bridge. This will cause considerable inconvenience to traffic for some time, but every effort will be made to minitniie it by means of notice boards and the assistance of the police. The road will be narrowed IMF the .Icademy to 19 feet. The engineer has reported that it may he neeeesery to fence in the footpath on the west aide of the bridge, but we understand that the Corporation will not agree to this being done until a temporary footway has been erected over the river on the east side of the bridge. The committee made an inspection of the works yesterday, . .</p>
        <p>'rho oilier for the steel required in the construction of the bridge has been placed with Messrs. Monks,Hall and Co., Ltd., Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. JOSEPH WHITFIELD.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOSEPH WHITFIELD.</p>
        <p>An Old Walton Resident.</p>
        <p>Plwto Top/c.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Tuesday last of Mr. Joseph Whitfield who for upwards of 40 years had carried on business as a market gardener at. Lower IValton. ' respected in the beCeseed Wet. i51.1-lilrwa . - district, having Wen militant 6iifioaf fu f Walton Inferior for nearly 30 yeargi. bhlj tetighliig , that I position about two yore agd oWlea to 1,!•-,:raLced age.; Ho had reached the ripe age of Al years, having been bOrtl In idersepstreet, Warrington in the year 1821. His early education was received , at the Notional School. At a very early age he left school to follow the occupation of garden- ing. His early manhood was spent as head gardener for Sir Thomas Bazeley at OldTraffotd, where he remained for several years, eventually returning to his native town about the year 1863, when he was engaged by the late Mr. George Crot.field to superintend the laying out ' of the grounds at Walton lea, and afterwards remained in his employ as head gardener for several years until he commenced business as a market gardener. By his death the Wesleyan body of the district low* ode of its oldest members and adherents, be having worshipped in the old Bank-street chapel and was also present at the foundation stone- laying and attended the opening services of the present Bold-street Church. For many years he attended cervices at the latter place of worship, travelling 12 miles each Sunday to attend services there with other members of his family. In later years he was associated with the Ebenezer Chapel, Stockton Heath, where he held several offices and when the new Wesleyan Chapel was built it became one of the joys of his life to lay one of the foundation stones. Deceased leaves two sons and two daughters to mourn his loss. The interment took place at Hill cliff., on Friday afternoon, the funeral service being conducted by Rev. John thionell and assisted by Rev. W. Nicholson. Many loral wreaths were sent by his large circle of friend.</p>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome. Next week's programme should appe.d to all sections of the patrons of this popular place of entertainment as almost varied bill of fare is announced to be on view. A number of first-class artistes have been engaged and crowded houses should be the order of the week. Edward Crosland's company of melody makers occupy the premier place and will be seen in "The Burglars Dream " while Sanday McNah and company will present their novel and funny sketch entitled "Xing Pharaoh." The other turns are as follow Sims Woolley, eccentric comedian ; Barrie and Wood, corned, entertainers ; The original dancing Gilberts . The Ives, the clever ball punchers ; and Dolly Ross, the little lady with the big voice.</p>
        <p>t Ora* , , Vl* f</p>
        <p>'THE LATE MR. J. WHITFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>PRIZES FOR GARDENS, ALLOTMENTS,</p>
        <p>AND WINDOW BOXES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Committee of the Beautiful Warrington Society held at the Old Academy on Wednesday evening, the following reports were read and adopted The judges have visited the outside exhibits, includ• , lug the Victoria Park Allotments, entered for com- petition ; the entries were rather below the average, probably owing to the long continued dry weather and heat which has hastened the flowering of the plants and caused the owners :to refrain from entering their grrdens for competition this year. Many of the exhibits were exceedingly good, and gave the judges a d Moult task in arriving at their final decision. Special attention may be called to the entries in class 3 (neatest and best arranged rind stocked back garden) here the judges felt compelled to recom- mend that two special prizes should be given for the gardens of 221 end 225, Knutaford-road. The awards are as follow :</p>
        <p>The neatest and beet arranged window box : 1 and 2, divided, Mrs Hatton, 227, Knotaford.road, A Bradley, 221, Knutsford-road ; 3, E Glenton, 37, Willis-street. The neatest and best arranged small front garden : 1 and 2, dit ided, E</p>
        <p>Weston, F H Robinsor. 14, Causeway Avenue. The neatest and best arranged and stocked back garden. I Two special prizes of 7s 6d each awarded to Mn Halton and A Bradley. 1, S Williams, 78, Howard-street; 2, E I Ice, 11, Eaille-street ; 3, F Bottles, I, Ford-street. For the best general appearance of front or back of house : I, F H Robinson (ack) ; 2, Mrs Hatton (back); 3, F Bettlzs (back). The best kept and best stocked allotment garden : I, Thomas Howliaton, 7, Enville-street ; 2, R H Drury, 9, Marbury-street ; 3, W Saunders, 204, Kt:ate:ad-road. The neatest and best arranged garden at any Public Elementary School in the Borough (girls' section) : 2, Bolton school ; extra prize. Bolton school infants.</p>
        <p>any rate, take a real interest in their plants. Only two school gardens were entered for computio lion, one in the girls section and the other in the infants section, and in both instances there was evid- ence of a gveat deal of time and energy having been Test to produce the good reoults obtained. We think that school gardens should be more encouraged, and an endeavour mode to induce the children to take an interest in them at all the schools where gardens exist. The judges feel they oust t not to omit to refer to the really good esEibtta at Hamilton-street (Girls) and !it. darner Scimols.</p>
        <p>!action or stocks, asters, chrysanthemums and 3 in fact a moderate garden.</p>
        <p>I Armitape ; 3, May Hill • h c, Hannah Hinchcliffe and iJorris Allston ; c, Jessie Bibby. ST. MARYS', BCTTERMARKET-STREKT.—Geraniums : 1, Alban Eckersley ; 2, William McGarry ; h c W Butler and J Lee. Fuchsias : 1, William Newell ; J Eckersley and Thomas Tavlin. Hour TRlNlTY.—Geraniums : 1, Ida Whitby ; 2 Clarence Cotton ; hc, Albert Forster. Fuchsias: 1, 11111211 Ellison ;2, Bessie Mather ; h c, Dorotky Cotton ARPIASY-STRIET.--Genthillll3B I, Doris M Cross; 2, Harold Cross; h c, Dorothy Silk ; c, Harold Silk. Fuchsias : 1, Hilda Shutt ; 2, Cissy Cross ; 3, Nellie Jones ; hC, Eveline Pepper and Doris Greenwood.</p>
        <p>SA( RED HEART.—Geraniums 3,T Jones. Fushciss: 1, II Bonehill ; 2, E Donlan ; 3, Florence Smith ; h c, Thomas Savage ; o, Elizabeth Ryan. ST. BenteehAs.—Geraeituns : 1, William Barrel!; . F Lane ; o,—, Hill. Fuchsias : I,Ro hart Cook : 2, Mary Braoegirdle ; 3, James Higham ; h c, Edith Halt; c, Cecil Rhodes and Henry Burwell. ST. JAM/W.—Geraniums : 1, Jack Oxley : 2, AR; 3, M Daynall ; h c, Lilly Rowe and Elsie Newton ; c, C Wilcox. Fuchsirs : 1, W Fairclough ; 2, Gladstone Sherratt ;3, Mary Munn and Eva Newall hc, A Savory and E Davenport ; c, E Morris, and F Jenks. ST. MARY'S, LATCHFORD.--OaraMUMS :I, Harold' Taylor; 2, E Rigby ; 3, W Ashurst ; h c, H Cregan and I' Hardman ;a, W Carr. Fuchsia. 1. M Ruby; 2, A Walsh ; 3,1 Rigby ; hc, F Walsh BEd W Lowe; c, Nora Lynch. CHRIaT CRuttett.—Geraniems : 1, Arthur Bradshaw;' 2, W Looney ; 3, John Heywood and W Denton ; h c James Parlitt and John Taylor. Fuchsias : 1, Wilfred Boardman ; 2, Arthur Hadkiss ; 3, Edward Lucy ; h Archie Douglas end Thomas Warren. BOLTON ScuooLs.—Geraniums : 1, Edith Derby- shire ; 2, Vera Higham ; 3, Phyllis Burrows ; ho, F Seabury. Fuchsias : 1, Gladys Boardman ; o, Mabel Bennett ; 3, Alice Bothwell ; h c, Bertha Muakett.</p>
