      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WESLEYAN MINISTERS' RECOGNITION MEETING.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN MINISTERS' RECOGNITION MEETING.</p>
        <p>THE SUPERINTENDENT AND SOCIAL EVILS.</p>
        <p>Wherever Wesleyan Methodist ministers take up their residence they always receive a cordial public welcome at what is. called the" recognition meeting." And though Warrington has had scores of such meet- ings before, never have the Wesleyans extended a welcome to so many as five new pastors as they did on Thursday at Bold-street. In the afternoon the Rev. W. A. Beckett preached in the church and afterwards over 200 persons from all quarters of the circuit enjoyed a well-served tea in the adjoining schoolroom, A large congregation assembled in the church in the evening. Mr. F. A. Harris (circuit steward) presided and along with him in the rostrum were the Revs. W. A. Beckett, E. Morgan, H. Hugh Williamson,B.A. H. Middlewood, W. Nicholson, John Gunnell, and Dr. Ferguson (circuit stewar,i). The CHAIRMAN hoped that the ministry of all the "ye brethren would be en esceedinglY happy cue to atene;attea Elttbelf fatgiliei—thoss who had them Ho also hoped the future would te prosperous for all the churches in the circuit and that their work would redound to the honour and glory of God. (Applause.) Dr. FERGUSON said that as far as the membership in the circuit was concerned they took a good place in the connestion, but though he did not want to he pessimistic all was not well with them. They wanted more members. As long as there was so much indiffer- ence among them, as long 39 their churches were halt or more than half full, and as long as there were so many people outside, who never heard of Jesus Christ, they were not in a position to say they were doing well. He did not want to damp the aspirations of the new ministers, for if they and the members put in plenty of work they would progress by leaps and bounds. (Applause.) Particularly in the town there was much room for aggressive work. The</p>
        <p>ee CONDITION OF THE STREETS ON SUNDAY 'SMUTS was distressing, for young people, who had once been to Sunday school, but thought themselves too old to go now, paraded up and down, instead of going to bear the Word of God. He thought that among themselves they could certainly do something to lesson, if not altogether, reverse the state of affairs. Then there was another clam—the poor, degraded, and the drunkard— whom they had got to reach. If they were to follow in the footsteps of Jesus Christ—if they aspired to serve; Him—it was their duty to do as much as in them lay.' !Applause). The Rev. JOIIN GUNNELL said that at that meeting they could not forget the ministers who had gone to labour elsewhere and they followed them with their , prayers and strong hopes that they would he equally useful where they had gone. (Applause.) Regarding recognition meetings, he could say from bin own experience that they were just happy occasions when they had an interchange of thought and sentiment and kindly wishes. And those of course, bad their advantages. But to his mind the purest joy in the ministerial life was not found in public recognition meetings, but the things telling in ministerial life were the esteem of people, the con- fidence of those in whose judgment they might place reliance, the love of simple souls and last, but not least, the warm affection of little children. (Applauee.) Methodist people made MORE DIMANDS UPON TOE LAITY THAN ANY OTHER CHER, 11, .</p>
        <p>and he could hardly conceive of a more hopeless con- dition if ministers had not the co-operation of the laity. To begin with, what would the services he like but for the joy that was derived from the singing of their</p>
        <p>chairs? Then there were their local preachers, who were rendering invaluable help, and also the class leaders, Sunday school teschem, remperence and Guild workers. They were surrounded by thoughtful and devoted man end woman who were toiling for the Master. He beseeched them all to pray earnestly for the ministers and for the Christian work that was being carried on. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. W. A. BECKETT (stationed at Bewsey-road), said they all appreciated the welcome that had been accorded to them ever since they had been in the circuit. It was said of the Methodist people that they were fond of different kinds of preachers—intellectual, sympathetic, sentimental and anecdotal, but above everything else they liked a new preacher. Well, if that were the case with the Warrington folk then they bad plenty of new preachers. (Laughter.) It was eery honourable to have a place in the Church of the people called Methodists. It might be that he bad a prejudice in favour of their own Church, for when one had been brought up in a certain churzh and when one's forbears had been associated with the same Church for some generations it was only to be expected that one slop d be prejudiced in its favour. He had nothing against other churches, they were all doing their work splendidly, they all had an honoured place in the great universal Church of God, but he liked his own Church best for many reasons. He believed that Methodism was God created, and had been God sustained. They were grateful for what had been accomplished by the Methodist Church, Professor Drummond had said that Chrietianity was God's Pfograngen to anke the winlti betOr e 94 he (the Siniakeal •••.. • • • . .. h .at they ad their place in that programme. (Applause.) There was still the need for the great truths, which their fathers taught, to be pro. claimed from their pulpits—the reality of sin, the reality of redemption and the reality of supernatural power. If the vocabulary of their utterance of those troths differed the troths still remained—and those were what mattered. Continuing, Mr. Beckett said that in these days of drift from organised ohristianity the</p>
        <p>CHURCH MUST NOT STAND ALOOF FROM THE NEEDS OF</p>
        <p>THE PEOPLE. They must keep an eye open to the removal of the social wrongs that were affecting this country. The church must take its plac3 iu helping to lift men Applied Christianity was the greet remedy for social evils. (Applause.) In conclusion, he said that the secret of all success in individual life and in the cor- porate life of the church depended upon their contact with the Spirit of God.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. M0n0...'. (of Padgate) said Methodist people must not forget that they were essentially evangelloal, that God gave to John Wesley and his sucoessors s mission—the mission of working for conversions. He put in a plea for larger attend- ances at the morning service, and said it would be a good thing if they would put forth an effort to be present as much as possible. The Rev. TICGII WILMA (Rold-street), said that though English people were English people there were many tribes among them and each tribe had different characteristics. They hal their own way of expressing their appreciation. Sine did it by word of mouth, others in the more practical faabion. that of the work they would do. Now he came from Northumber- land to here and he wantei to find out ia which of the two ways Lancashire people expressad themselves. In the first place he was taking it for ;ranted that every- one of them had a gad sound Christian heart, be was tsking generous views of everyone he lust, he was assuring that in each of them there was a real, good, , sound rho;.! of Christianity—something that was God-, I like and Divine. Though some with a cynical under.' 'standing might say it was very foolish and the kind of ' idealism that would not fit in with the facts of human life it was the better plan. (Hear. hear). He wanted to learn what were the ways of Laacashire folk, he wanted to understand them. He also bad s right to esk that they should understand him. He was not ask- ing for any indulgence, he did not want to be a pet or pampered by flattery. (Laughter. Rut one was entitled to a little consideration. So that if it did not come to pass that they did not fall in with his way of</p>
        <p>thinkiag or preaching they must not say" We will not put up with it, as we have never had it before." He would only let them listen a little bit longer and welt and see and then he should have no reason to complain. Otherwise he was altogether at their uor,ice and he hopsd they would not spare him in appealing to him in any way in which ha might be of help. (Applause. Rev H. MIDDLIMO3D (Penketh) impressed upon the audience the need for a revival, and for that they needed consecration, prayer and enthusiasm. Perhaps, he raid, the greatest hindrance to aggressive work amongst them was that they lacked men who were thoroughly devoted to the service of Christ and whose best energies were culled for by the claims from the outside world. They must bring to tivdr work, alertness, enterprise and courage, (Applause). The Church that had not a passion for the salvation of men cussed to be a church in the trie seose of the word. (Applause).</p>
        <p>, In the course of a breezy speech the Rev. W Nicholson, (Stockton Heath, Latchford, Lymm) said they must not expect the ministers to be always sitting on thair doorsteps and complain if theywere not at their houses three times a week for tea. (Laughter.) They must remember that they had their sermons to prepare and their studies to atteal. He trusted that they would soon have a direc aggrea!ive Inlvrlent Qbring outsiders into their church. (Applause). During the meeting Mrs. Booth rendered is her usually finished style " I will extol Thee, 0 God" and , the choir contributed the anthem "Come unto Him." Nr. Frank Hickman ably presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
