      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>AN UNUSUAL PREDICAMENT.</head>
        <p>AN UNUSUAL PREDICAMENT.</p>
        <p>A case of unusual interest was heard at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, before Mr. Charles White (in the chair), and Sir J Backhouse, when Joseph Jonas, 27, Wakefield-streat, was summoned to show cause why be should not forfeit the sum of £lO that being the amount of bail in respect of James Little Wilson who was summoned to appear before the magistrates but who conld not be found. The Clerk Have you made any inquiries to try and locate the man ?</p>
        <p>Defendant : I have made every inquiry possible but cannot find atroon of him.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Smith seid that practically the whole poultry had been searched for him. The Clerk (to Sergeant Smith) : Is it not the general opinion among his friends that be has done away with himself ? Sergeant Smith: I believe thst is el.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the 133nch mud thst be was very sorry for the defendant but he would have to ray something and they would let him oft with 103.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was. good attendance on Sunday presided over by Mr. Joseph Owen, who nooks upon " Encourage- ment." Mr. E. Jamas gave the address, his subject being" What must I do to be saved " and " Roll away the stone." He nontendel we had something to do in securing salvation. We must not only believe but roll all difficulties on one side—unbelief, doubts, indifference, which were a great hindrance to being saved. Mr. James led the prayer, Mr C Cotton read the lesson, Mr Payton presided at the organ. The secretary welcomed a new member, and also moved a vote of sympathy and condolence with Mn. Thorniley of Marsh House-lane on the death of her son. Although he was note member, yet the members felt they would like to express their sorrow with the mother in her great trial after losing her husband no suddenly a short time ago. The closing }lrma Forward be our watchword," was heartily joined in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>REPORT ON THE SCHOOL CHILDREN'S PLANTS</head>
        <p>REPORT ON THE SCHOOL CHILDREN'S PLANTS</p>
        <p>List of Awards.</p>
        <p>The total number of plants presented for judging is about the same as last year, and although it is rather disappointing not to be able to report an Increase in the numbers, yet many of the specimens exhibited were, considering th." very dry and hot season, very fair, and showed that considerable care) and attention been iteetowed upon them. , It would be of corsiderable advantage if the plants I could be brought to the schools two or three times during ithe period they are under the children's care prior to their being presented for judging and instruc- ticns given to the children on any points in their treatment requiring correction. This would result, not cnly in an insproveement in the condition of the plants, but probably in an increase in the numbers brought in for competition, and help to create and sustain their owners' interest in the plants. We would suggest for the future that to each school only one variety of plant. either geranium or fuchsia, should be supplied This woold give every child an equal chance with others tn tha name !school, and could be varied by giving, s iy, ge-aniums one year and fuchsias the next.</p>
        <p>We think the time has n'wv arrived when a separate class should be made for w coed ray plants. At several schools yen good spi c'osens were brought in for inspection which shows thst some of the chi.dren, at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The Burtonwood Street Scheme.</p>
        <p>REV. A M. MITCHELL AND AN ALLEGED</p>
        <p>" PACKED " MEETING.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. J. Backhouse presided and there were also present :—Rew A M Mitch*ll,Dr Murray, Messrs C Mather, W Furness, J Ball, A Brooks, J W Knowles, W Houghton, P Longton, J C Sutton (clerk), It S Newcombe (surveyor), F Kerfoot (inspector of nuisances).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>BURTONWOOD STRF.ET SCHEME.</head>
        <p>BURTONWOOD STRF.ET SCHEME.</p>
        <p>REV. A. M. MITCHELL AND "A ONE-SIDED PARIS DIESTING." The CLERK read a letter from Mr. J,lns Fairclougb, clerk to the Burtonwood Parish Council, with refere°_," to the proposed nsw streets in that parish. It Mat°. that a parish meeting was held on August 28th to em eider the plans and estimates of the cost. and a resolutl" that the work be not preceded with was carried by 38 votes to nine. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: To ten.</p>
        <p>The letter continued : I summoned a meeting of the Council for last night (Monday) but only two members attended. A quorum u.ot being found, no business was done, The Rev. A. hi. lkirrummd. said be felt he had a grievance in the matter of the streets. In the first place the delay had, to his mind, been altogether inexplicable on the part of the District Council. There had seemed to be for some reason or another, some hesitation in pushing forward the wish of the Council which dated so far back as May of last year. He had another grievance because, after he had waited no patiently, a full dress debate on the question should have taken place in his absence. He was, however, glad that Mr. Knowles, who was the real mover at the outset in the matter and to whom he (the apt akar) gladly and beertily gave his support, was there to speak on that occasion. He (Mr. Mitchell) was in the habit of saying what he thought and of speaking plainly, and he thought that it would have been a little more courteous if the Council had deferred consideration of the matter until be could be present, because he was never absent if he could possibly attend. According to the wish of the District Council, a parish meeting was summoned. He did not say that the meeting was deliberately " pecked," but it certainly was a very one-sided affair. He must say—and he expressed it at the meeting —that he thought it was very bad taste for the chairman of the Parish Council to preside at the parish meetirg, for the simple reason :hat he was the manager of a colliery company who were the parties chiefly interested in the liability in connection with the streets and the expenses which would be incurred if the work were proceeded with. The rev, gentleman further contended that workmen in lon positions who attended the meeting had ants fair opportunity of expressing themselves, but were dominated by the presence of the colliery manager in the chair. They were also deliber- ately told—and he regarded it as a very bad form of intimidation—that if the work were proceeded with, the owners of the property would " have it out of them "in the way of increased rents, lie maintained that to bold that avers number of householders, hold- ing their tenancies under the colliery company, was an unfair proceeding and he altogether took exception to what took place in connection with the meeting. He did not Mink the resolution that was adopted ought to he taken too seriously by the District Council. That Council was the sanitary authority and such a resolu- tion, adopted under the circumstances he had related, did not free it from responsibility. Three great out- standing facts remained—the danger to health, the menace to comfort, and the greater menace to the safety of the district in the dark winter nights. The Council ought to insist upon the removal at such condi- tions. He felt sure that the Parish Conceit as a whole was not with the parish meeting. There were only three councillors present at the parish meeting, and two of them, including the chairman, were repre- sentatives of the colliery company. As far es the parishioners were concerned, he believed it would be far better for them to insist upon the work being done, even under the threat of an extra Bd. or 9.1. a week in rent, rather than live under the eery unsatisfactory conditions under which they were at present compelled to live. What they would spend in extra rent would probably be saved in fbctors' bills. Mr. K:sownes suggested that the proper course to I adopt would be to lay the whole of the facts before the Local Government Board, and to make it clear that some members objected to the way in which the matter was being dealt with. First of all the District Council passed a resolution that the streets should be made. Then they hanged fire for month after month, and nothing had been done. Plans were not prepared, nor were powers sought from the Local Government Board to make the streets. At a Council meeting a month ago, it was proposed that the question of making the streets should be referred to the Burtonwood parish meeting. The parish meet- ing was called and, from what he gathered from the representative of Burtonwaod, it was presided over by the manager of the colliery company and the secretary was also present. The people attending the meeting were chiefly tenants of the houses in question. . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Bali: That has to be proved: Mr. KNOWLES t gather that the majority of the people present at the meeting are tenants of these very houses and, if the Vicar's statement is right, they were told tl at if the streets were made they would have additional rents.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Itrtooaa : The Vicar did not say Unit. Ile said the owners would have it out of them.</p>
        <p>The Be,. A. NI. Mums:Lt. -What was said,l think, was to this effect—that the expense would have to come out of somebody's pocket and that would ulti- mately be the pocket of tbe tenant. Mr. BROOKS: That is different from" having it out of them." The Rev. A. M. Miveiikti. : I think those worth were used, but! would not be positive. Mr. KNOWLES: It is the same thing. Mr. BROOKS : No, "Having it out" suggests Viet there is a good deal of ill-feeling. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: I did not mention it in that way at all. Mr. BROOKS : I felt sure that nothing like that would be said, but it ki very reasonable to say 'lf you are going to put us to this expense you will have to he!p on to pay for it.: . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. KNOWLES : My own personal feeling is that the proper way to get at the matter is that this should be communicated to the Local Government Board. If the Council won't do it, then you (Rev. A. AI. Mitchell) do it. The Local Government Board, as the sanitary authority of the kingdom, will no doubt came some inquiry to be made as to how this Council discharges ita.taniyary duties, . . .</p>
        <p>Mr iiii6oic-W7I hope that will be done. It will perhaps stop a good deal of talk both from Mr Knowles and the Vicar of Buttonwood.</p>
        <p>The CLERK : Mr Knowles suggestion of Mr Mitchell, as reputing the parish going to the Local Government Board would be very proper, but it would not be at all proper for you to go to the Local Govern- ment Board with a view to clruplaining that you should yoo.rae.lves have the power _</p>
        <p>Mr IifaIWLE.?. ; I don't say this Council should. I iay Mr. Mitchell. The CLERK The Local Government Board have no power to order proceedings. Mr KNOWLEs : I think port will discover that the Local Government Board have means of making local authorities carry out their duties. We are the health authority and weara not carrying out our duty. In the first instance we are hindered by the oftl dale who did not set to work to do nezessary things and make the necessary application. Mr. A. BROOKS Application has been made long ago. Mr.l;sowces : Did yon apply to the Local Govern- ment Board to adopt the necessary urbrn powers? _ _</p>
        <p>The CLRRK : Yes. Mr. KS:OWL/CS : And ask them to hold an inquiry? . . . .</p>
        <p>The Ctittr7::— They wrote back salting me to mete! a declaration, which I did mike, on the subject.</p>
        <p>The estanotsw If Mr. Kno'wles would attend the meeting; he would know theae things. Mr. KNOWLEs : I know the matter was not attended to in the way it is attended to in other places. It mac allowed to he for twelve months, practically. The CLERK : But you are recommending the Council to go to the Local Government Board to complain that thlo.Conocil have not done whet they have asked them. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. !NOWLIN : Na. I recommend the VidlT to lay the twitter bef see the 1. scat Cl war talent Bowl, and hope then we shall have something d one. We as a health authority ought to carry out. our duty:_. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. W.'s bib 7 At the Same time the'Vicsr will have to say what the parish meeting has done and then what ground will he have to stand on ? Mr. LANGTON It in not the first time the parishion- ers have been over-ruled by the Local Government Board and this Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. 1:r:owl-Es : They ought to be. Mr. BROOKS : What we base adopted so far ban been in accordance with our general procaelare. When we are to spool the money of a parish we generally ask the feeling of the parishioners. If they do not acquiesce and the work has to he done, then we have to over-ride thous, hut I do not nee there is any necessity for this work to be done.</p>
        <p>• Mr. KNCM : You are setting up • dangerous precedent. If a maa starts building • colony In any pariah and the people know that if the streets are made their rents will go np, thoselltenants will fswamp the parish meeting and it will ;be !impossible !for as as a sanitary authority to discharge our duties. The Cnroz.,i: There In nothing before the meeting so we shall have to leave the matter MI it is. _ _</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. MIT IFLL I do not eon my way individually to adopt the come Mr. Knowlea has proposed. l think It in a District Council matter and I 'bail move : "That the Perish Council of Burtonwood be requested to submit a scheme for the treatment of Erects, cross-streetv and back paeiagea Is the colliery settlement which will enanre the health. comfort Ana</p>
