      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PADGATE SEWAGE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Ltsyset-KA vE, tate Office, GrappeohaP wrote as follows with reference to Padgtte sewage May I again call the attention of your Council to the above nuissnce. For some years it hes been very bud, but owing to the hot summer, it is absolutely danger- ous to live anywhere near the open brook, which for some weeks now has been one mass of orude sewage. I have many timts pressed for something to be done in the matter, but no move has yet been mule to cure it. I have a notice from your Council about some minor alterations to one of our cottages, which we will carry out, but it seems a curious position to take up, looking after little things, such as a ventilating grid in a bed- room, which the tenant will block up as soon no we put in, and leaving undone surh a very serious and danger- ous thing as this Padgate sewage. Mr. BALL: I am afraid Mr. Kaye's letter is only, one of a lot that cculd be written.</p>
        <p>- ..... Mr. FURNESS suggested that a reply be 3 ant to Mr. Kaye, telling him what the Council was doing wilt_ reference to the sewage armngements. Mr. M.cmilLß acid other letters had been received and the Council bad asked the Surveyor in shut time be could prepare the scheme for the sewering of Padgate. Instructions had been given for the work to be pushed on as rapidly as possible and it was expected that the Surveyer's report would be ready in two months. Mr. Furness's suggestion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-09_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TOOK SALTS OF LEMON</head>
        <p>TOOK SALTS OF LEMON</p>
        <p>A Mysterious Letter.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the Police Station, yesterday by Coroner Brighouse on the body of Hannah !Aycock aged 55, wife of John Laycock, a wiredrawer, who died in the Infirmary on Tuesday last from the effects of poisoning. Alice Blackshaw, wife of Albert Blackshaw,7, Jolley- street, said the deceased was her mother. She new her for the first time for nine weeks at 11 o'clock on Tuesday morning, when the deceased called at her house. She was then under the influence of drink. She stayed a while and then left but returned at 3 o'clock the same afternoon. After playing wlth thy children for a while she went into an outhouse. When she returned she complained of feeling unwell. She again went out and Called for adriok of n Ater. Witness, took her a drink and the decessod said " Alice, I am dying, yon don't know what I have done. I have taken four pennywcrth of ealts of lemon." Witness called in Mrs Hart who looked at the deceased's tongue. They then took her to the Infirmary and when the 11111110 asked her what she had taken the deceased replied" Three penny- worth of felts of lemon, I thought that it would do me good." She (witness) was preeeot at the Infirmary when death took. place at 4. 10, the same day.</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Hart, living with her husbend at 1, Jolley-street, said that the previous witness called he, to see the deceased in the outhouse. She a:unified the decessed's tongue and found it yellow and coated. She enlisted to take deceased to the Infirmary. She afterwards found a bottle which bore a label with the words, " A penny on this bottle." The Coroner read a paper which was found on the deceased at the Infirmary and which bore the following: " Mr. John Laycock, 5, Jolley-street, Dear Alice. Joseph, Ernest and all of you. I am goleg away for good no goodbye. Hannah Laycoct." Detective Woollscott then informed the coroner that the deceased bud previously left similar notes when going away from home. This was corroborated by the deceased's daughter who stated that although deceased never threatened to take away her life she had said that she WWILI go away for good. In reply to the Coroner Mrs. Blackshsw stated that the deceased had been in the habit of drinking whisky and depositing the bottles in thi outhouse. In her (witness s) opinion she thought the deceased had taken the salts of lemon in mistake for whisky. . . .</p>
        <p>•-- The Coroner (to witnegq; : I understand thst your mother was sent away to an isebristee home by order of the mmistratea. Witness : Tbnt N no. .</p>
        <p>X..717;1 en that statement about taking thesalts of lemon, it is quite evident she knee wba she was [eking. The Coroner said that if there had been no ev:•lence of deceased leaving notes similar to ttia one band iu her possession at the Infirmary there could have been no hesitation about corning to the conclusion that she had taken her own life. When people gat lota the habit of drinking heavily they seemed to take all kinds of things. The jury retired and upon their return the Foreratu said that they had come to the c...nclsion that the deceased died from ta'ain; salts of I,non, b...t they were not sstisfied situ the circumstie.es unier she drank the poison.</p>
